2<lb/> put them in a sieve to expose them to the air for<lb/> a few days till quite black, then put them in<lb/> a jar, &amp; boil some vinegar, with the following spice<lb/> a little nutmeg, mace, and a few cloves, peppercorns,<lb/> &amp; ginger, a few shalots, and a bit of horse radish.<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Indian Pickle.</hi><lb/> Take one gallon of the best vinegar, put in your jar, with<lb/> out boiling, 1/4 lt ginger, shalots, garlick, flour mustard<lb/> common salt of each 4 ounces, - mustard seed,<lb/> <sic>Twinerie</sic>, of each 2 ozs, of black-peppercorns 1oz, cayenne<lb/> 1/2 a quarter of an ounce, Put nuts your jar, a portion<lb/> of everything the season affords, equal to the quantity of<lb/> named. it must be stirred every day for a fortnight.<lb/> then vegetables are merely dried, before put into the jar.<lb/> <hi rend="underline">"To Green Pickle."</hi><lb/> To one quart of water, 2 oz. of bay salt boiled, &amp; poured,<lb/> hot over the Beans, steam them down, &amp; set them<lb/> by the jar until the next day, take them out and<lb/> dry them, with a coarse cloth, then put the vinegar<lb/> and spice over the fire, &amp; when it boils put it on the<lb/> Pickle, and <hi rend="underline">let it simmer, but not boil</hi>, till they become<lb/> green, &amp; keep them down, with a spider or wooden <hi rend="underline">spoon</hi><lb/> put them in a jar, and keep them warm, for the day,<lb/> you may then tie them down for ease. "<unclear>Mrs <hi rend="underline">Watson</hi></unclear>."
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/007|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
3<lb/> <head>- To - <lb/> <hi rend="underline">- Pickle Onions -</hi></head> <lb/> After peeling them, simmer them about 10 minutes<lb/> in an equal quantity of vinegar + water, + a bit<lb/> of salt the size of a walnut, put them on cold<lb/> then drain them on a cloth, &amp; put them<lb/> in your jar, &amp; boil white wine vinegar, with<lb/> mace, ginger, &amp; pepper-corns &amp; pour hot over.<lb/> <head><hi rend="underline">To Pickle Mangoes.</hi></head><lb/> Take large Cucumbers, and cut a square piece out<lb/> of them, and take out all the seeds, put them<lb/> in strong salt and water for 3 days, then put<lb/> them in a brass bottle with the brine, set<lb/> them over a slow-fire till they are green, let them<lb/> drain, fill them with horse-radish, white<lb/> pepper-corns, mustard seeds, ginger, and garlick,<lb/> and some cucumber chopped fine then <hi rend="underline">sow</hi> the<lb/> piece on, and put them in a jar, boil the best<lb/> white wine vinegar with what spice you please<lb/> put it over the Mangoes, and stop them down.<lb/> for uses.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/008|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
4 <head>- To Pickle -<lb/> - Mushrooms Brown. -</head> <lb/> When they are washed clean, put as much<lb/> vinegar as will cover them, nutmeg, cloves, mace<lb/> pepper, a small onion, a little piece of<lb/> horse-radish, set <unclear>sheins</unclear> over the fire, till<lb/> they are tender, add a little salt.<lb/> <head>White Mushrooms</head><lb/> Take Mushrooms, and make them clean, in<lb/> two waters as quick as you can, wipe them<lb/> clean with a flannel then let them be on<lb/> on a cloth to drain, boil vinegar, and when it boils<lb/> put the Mushrooms into it and let them boil<lb/> 10 minutes they must be put on a cloth to<lb/> drain, put your spice in the vinegar, cloves and<lb/> mace, when it is boiled from it upon them<lb/> and when cold tie them for use.<lb/> <head>To Make Gooseberry Vinegar</head><lb/> To one gallon of water, allow 1 gallon of Gooseberries,<lb/> boil the water, and let it stand till new<lb/> milk warm, then bruises the Gooseberries,<lb/> and from the water over them, let it stand
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/009|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
5<lb/> 10 days or a fortnight stirring of several times<lb/> to every gallon of the liquor put 1/2 lt of coarse<lb/> sugar to the gallon, when strained, put it into<lb/> a Barrel, loosening it unstopped for a few days<lb/> and let stand in the warm sun.<lb/> <head>-Primrose Vinegar.-</head><lb/> To 2 quarts of Primrose heads take 1 gallon<lb/> of water 1/2 lb of sugar, boil and pour it on<lb/> the heads when boiling hot, when it is milk<lb/> warm, work it with a little yeast.<lb/> <head>To Pickle Red Cabbage.</head><lb/> Cut a fine Red Cabbage in thin slices, lay it<lb/> in an earthen colander, with a little salt<lb/> let it stand a few days, after which, lay<lb/> it in your jar, and boil sufficient sugar to<lb/> cover it, with a little <unclear>Cochineal</unclear> and spices<lb/> pour it hot over the cabbage, and tie it up for use.<lb/> <head>To Pickle Onions -</head><lb/> Peel the Onions, and lay them in strong salt and<lb/> water for a few days, changing them frequently<lb/> drain them and dry them in a cloth, and<lb/> boil a little vinegar, with Ginger, Black pepper-corns<lb/> pour it over your Onions, and tie them for use.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/010|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
6 <head>Gooseberry Vinegar.</head><lb/> Boil the water, let it stand to be cold, take<lb/> 3 lbs of Gooseberries to 1 Gallon of water, break<lb/> them with your hand let it stand 24<lb/> hours stir it twice or three times, then<lb/> strain it through sieve afterwards through<lb/> a flannel bag, to every 3 quarts quarts of Liquor<lb/> put 1 1/2 lt coarsest moist sugar you can get,<lb/> break it <hi rend="underline">well</hi> into your Liquor, put into the<lb/> Cask, balm some brown paper and put over<lb/> the bung-hole, prick it full of holes, let<lb/> it stand one year before you tap it.<lb/> <head>Oyster Ketchup</head> To 100 of Oysters bruised and their liquor,<lb/> allow 1 lt of Anchovies, three pints of Basin<lb/> Wine (or Mountain) a large lemon sliced<lb/> with half the peel, boil it for half an<lb/> hour, strain it off, and add, of cloves, Mace<lb/> Black-Pepper-cours burred a quarter of an oz. of<lb/> <unclear>cack</unclear>, a Nutmeg, sliced or broken, a little<lb/> cayenne, bottled for use with the spice.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/011|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
7 <head>- Elder Ketchup. -</head><lb/> To 1 Gallon of ripe berries, put a quart of<lb/> vinegar, and bake them in a Pot, strain, and<lb/> run them through a jelly bag, boil it with<lb/> salt spice and garlick to your taste, when<lb/> it is cold, bottle it for use. =<lb/> White Elder Ketchup :: made in the same way<lb/> with the addition of Anchovies boiled in the Liquor<lb/> <head>- Cucumber Ketchup. -</head><lb/> Take any number of large cucumbers you please<lb/> and half the number of Onions, stir them<lb/> and put them in an earthen vessel, and <lb/> strew salt over them, after they have stood 12 or<lb/> 14 hours, mash them down with your hands.<lb/> Let them stand as much longer, then pass<lb/> the liquor through a sieve and boil it, taking<lb/> off the scum occasionally after it has stood,<lb/> boil it and scum a second time, in order to make<lb/> it as clear as possible, then to ever quart of Liquor,<lb/> put an equal quantity of Lisbon/or any other White<lb/> Wine, and also 1/4 lt. of anchovies, boil them till the<lb/> anchovies are dissolved and pass through a<lb/> strainer, then add to every quart of it a quarter of
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/012|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
8<lb/> an oz. of Mace, half a nutmeg, 1 spoonful of<lb/> white pepper, a little Ginger, a few cloves,<lb/> and some shalots, boil it all together for<lb/> about 1/2 an hour and when cold, bottle it up<lb/> and put into the bottle the spice and shalots.<lb/> <head>- Walnut Ketchup. -</head><lb/> To 50 Walnuts cut in slices, put 2 ozs of Shalots.<lb/> cut through, put them altogether into a stone<lb/> mortar, and beat them all <hi rend="underline">well</hi>, put to them<lb/> a quarter of a lt, of salt, and 3 parts of a pint<lb/> of best vinegar, let them stand a week in an<lb/> earthen vessel, stirring them 2 or 3 times a<lb/> day then put 2 ozs of Anchovy 1/2 <unclear>a oz</unclear> of nutmeg<lb/> sliced, Mace, and long pepper, boil altogether<lb/> and take off the scum, as it rises, when<lb/> cold, bottle it but <hi rend="underline">not</hi> cork it for 3 or 4 days.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

