15 <head>Spiced Beef</head><lb/> Take the leather <unclear>staves</unclear> on top ribs of beef cut<lb/> out all the bones, then rub over it 2 handful<lb/> of common salt, 1 handful of saltpeter, 1/2 oz. of<lb/> Jamaica Pepper, 2 spoonful of black pepper<lb/> and about half an Nutmeg, a little salt, let<lb/> it remain a week, then wash it in cold water<lb/> and then put over it 2 table spoonful of common<lb/> salt, the same of black pepper, half a nutmeg, a little<lb/> mace, an cayenne, roll it up and bind it very tight<lb/> put it in the oven in <unclear>pipkin</unclear>, with a little<lb/> water, when it is baked sufficiently, bind it<lb/> round with a strong cloth and set a upon it<lb/> till it is thoroughly pressed<lb/> <head>-To Pot Beef-</head><lb/> To 2 lbs of Beef a 1/4 of salt peter, and half a quarter<lb/> of an ounce of common salt, salt the beef with it,<lb/> &amp; let it be 4 days, turning it every day, then put<lb/> it into a Pot for baking, covered over with a 1/4 lt<lb/> of butter, when taken out of the over, blanch 3<lb/> Anchovies, &amp; pound them &amp; the beef very small,<lb/> put the butter which is baked on the meat,<lb/> with 1/4 lt more clarified, work that and the
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/020|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
16<lb/> beef well together, then put it into the Pots.<lb/> close down, and pour clarified over them.<lb/> <head>Another example to Pot Beef</head><lb/> Put 3 lbs of Beef, with a little brown sugar, &amp;<lb/> Salt-peter, let it be 24 hours, then wash it<lb/> clean &amp; dry it well with a cloth, season it<lb/> well with a <hi rend="underline">little</hi> beaten Mace, Pepper, &amp; Salt<lb/> to your taste, cut it into 5 or 6 pieces &amp; put it,<lb/> in an earthen pot, with a little water +<lb/> a lump of sweet such upon it, let the pot<lb/> boil in a pot of water, till the meat is tender<lb/> (this is much less waste than baking it)<lb/> then take it out, &amp; beat it a Mortar, add to<lb/> it a little more, Mace, pepper + salt, 1/2 lb butter<lb/> in the gravy and fat that came from the Beef<lb/> put in as you see it requires it + beat it exceeding-<lb/> ly fine, then put it into your Pots, and press<lb/> close down, pour clarified butter over it<lb/> and keep it a dry place.<lb/> To cure <head>Hams Bacon Beef, Tongues &amp;c.</head><lb/> First rub them with a little salt petre, +<lb/> let them lie one day, then make a Pickle,<lb/> as follows. - Bay Salt 4 lbs, common salt 8 lbs.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/021|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
17<lb/> Boil them in 5 Gallons of spring water for a<lb/> 1/4 of an hour, skin it well and when cold<lb/> put in the meat, &amp; let it remain for 3 weeks<lb/> after which, let it drain well for one night<lb/> then hang it to dry; or send it to be smoked<lb/> the meat will <hi rend="underline">not</hi> require soaking before boiled<lb/> &amp; the pickle will serve half a year, being boiled<lb/> every month or 6 weeks. you can make half the quantity.<lb/> <head>To Collar a Pigs Head.</head><lb/> Take as much of the head, hocks, + ears, + feet<lb/> as you think proper with the tongue, clean<lb/> them <hi rend="underline">well</hi>, then boil them will they are very<lb/> tender, &amp; the bones will take out, put the<lb/> meat in something round with a cloth under<lb/> it, stirring a little pepper + salt amongst the<lb/> meat, press it hard, while it is quite hot, +<lb/> let it stand over night; Previous to boiling (let it<lb/> stand in the Pickle that drains from the<lb/> Baeun) a full night.<lb/> <head>- To Collar Eel. -</head><lb/> Bone a large Eel, but do not skin it, mix,<lb/> pepper, salt, mace, allspice, &amp; a clove or two in<lb/> the finest powder &amp; rut over the whole inside
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/022|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
18<lb/> roll it tight, and bind with a coarse tape<lb/> Boil it in salt and water till enough, then<lb/> add vinegar &amp; when cold keep the collar in<lb/> pickle, season it either whole, or in slices,<lb/> chopped sage + parsley, + a little thyme<lb/> <unclear>knotted</unclear> margorem + savoury mixed with<lb/> the spices, <sic>grately</sic> improve the taste<lb/> Mackerel, or Salmon, may be done the same way.<lb/> <head>Soup made from Sheeps Head and Pluck</head><lb/> Take a pluck and put it into 4 Quarts of water then<lb/> add Onions, Carrots, Turnips, &amp; a cup of Pearl Barley<lb/> a few pepper cornes &amp; a cloves, stew it till nearly<lb/> done enough, wash the head &amp; <unclear>pick</unclear> it into the<lb/> stew pot, when the head is quite tender turn<lb/> it out &amp; pour off the liquer, let it all stand till<lb/> the next day then take off the fat thicken with<lb/> flour &amp; butter properly browned, add a little cayenne<lb/> pepper &amp; send it to table with the head cut with<lb/> small pieces &amp; sewed up in it, put in a<lb/> good deal of mushroom ketchup.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/023|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
19<lb/> <head>- To Jug a Hare. -</head><lb/> Cut it into small pieces, baist them with small<lb/> pieces of strips of Bacon here &amp; there, season them<lb/> with cayenne pepper &amp; salt, put them with an<lb/> earthen jug with a little, mace and onions<lb/> stuck with cloves, &amp; a bundle of sweet herbs cover<lb/> the jug or jar you do it in close that nothing can<lb/> get in them set it in a foot of boiling water &amp; three hours<lb/> will do it then take it out into a dish &amp; take out<lb/> the onions &amp; sweet herbs, &amp; send it to table hot,<lb/> if you do not like it larded have it out<lb/> <head>- To hash Rabbits -</head><lb/> Cut them up, &amp; put them into a very clean stew pan;<lb/> with a little water, &amp;a blade or two of mace, stew<lb/> them, till they are sufficiently done then skim off<lb/> all the bits, &amp; add half a pint of cream a little<lb/> butter, nutmeg, cayenne, &amp; salt.<lb/> <head>Chicken and Ham Patties</head><lb/> Skin &amp; mince very fine the breast or white fleshy<lb/> parts of a cold chicken and about half the<lb/> quantity lean ham or tongue highly flavoured.<lb/> Have in a little sauce pan a little good veal gravy<lb/> drawn from bones or trimmings, or the jelly of roast<lb> veal or lamb, thickened with a bit of butter<pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/024|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
20<lb/> rolled in flour, add a little grated Lemon<lb/> peel, whole pepper, salt a very little<lb/> cayenne &amp; a teaspoonful of lemon juice, stir the<lb/> mince in this till quite hot, &amp; fill up the Patties<lb/> which are best Baked empty, as the mincemeat<lb/> hardens in baking, This plan of baking the crust<lb/> separately will on trial be found much superior<lb/> to filling them at first, lay a piece of paper <unclear>over pkd</unclear><lb/> up into the lined <unclear>pathy pans</unclear> to support the top<lb/> while baking<lb/> <head>- To make Soy. -</head><lb/> 1 lb of coarse sugar 2 lbs of salt, fry it in a pan for<lb/> half an hour, keep stirring it all the time, then<lb/> add to it 3 pints of boiling water 1<hi rend="superscript">s</hi> worth of anchovies<lb/> a bunch of sweet herbs, a little cloves boil it<lb/> till the sugar &amp; salt is <sic>disolved</sic>, when cold<lb/> bottle it<lb/> <head>Colouring for Soups or Crabies. -</head><lb/> Put 4 ozs of lump sugar, a gill or water, &amp; 1/2 oz of<lb/> the finest batter into a small tosser &amp; set it<lb/> over a gentle fire, stir it with a wooden spoon<lb/> till of a bright brown then add 1/2 pint of water<lb/> boil, <unclear>seum</unclear> &amp; when cold, bottle it and cork
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/025|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
21<lb/> it close, add to soup or gravy as much of this<lb/> as will give it a proper colour.<lb/> <head>Vegetable Soup</head><lb/> Take green Peas that are rather old, boil them well<lb/> &amp; rub them through a sieve, slice Lettuce, Cucumber,<lb/> Onions &amp; a little suet, fry them in butter, &amp; add<lb/> to the soup with a few green peas, going ones/<lb/> pepper and salt to your taste<lb/> <head>To cure a Beef Liver for Gravy.</head><lb/> Take a Beef Liver dip it in water &amp;<lb/> out again, rub it well with coarse<lb/> sugar &amp; salt of each 3/4 lb. for 10 days<lb/> <unclear>twined</unclear> &amp; <unclear>subed</unclear> every day. than put the<lb/> liquer a way make a fresh brine<lb/> 3 ozs of saltpetre, 1 lb common salt, boil.<lb/> the up to a brine just enough to<lb/> cover the liver, (it must be kept covered)<lb/> let it lie 4 weeks. then hang it up<lb/> like bacon to dry, a <unclear>shie</unclear> not so<lb/> layer as three fingers boiled in a<lb/> pint of water for half an hour with<lb/> carrot, onion, &amp; an sprig of herbs, makes<lb/> the richest gravy without pepper or salt.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/026|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
22<lb/> <head>Colouring in Gravies</head><lb/> 2 lbs of Course sugar and I lb. of salt, fry it<lb/> in a saucepan till a very dark brown<lb/> keep stirring it all the time, then add<lb/> 3 pints of boiling ale, boil the whole till<lb/> the sugar and salt are dissolved.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/027|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
23
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/028|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
24
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/029|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
25<lb/> <head>- Irish Sauce. -</head><lb/> 1/4 lb of butter, 1/4 pint of good cream, and about a<lb/> teaspoonful of flour to thicken it, when melted<lb/> add 2 tablespoonful of Essence of Anchovies, and<lb/> boil it up just before sent to table.<lb/> <head>Another recipe for Irish Sauce.</head><lb/> Take walnuts fit for pickling, slice them &amp; beat them<lb/> in a marble mortar, put them in a strainer cloth<lb/> + press them in a cheese press till quite dry, let the<lb/> juice stand &amp; settle for one day then pour off the<lb/> clear, + to every pint of juice, put 1 lb of Achovies.<lb/> 1 lb shalots, 1/4 oz. Mace. 1/4 oz. Cloves, 1/4 oz. Jamaica Pepper, 1/2 pint<lb/> best Vinegar, + cayenne pepper to your taste, let it boil 20 min<lb/> -utes, then run it through a Jelly Bag, when cold bottle<lb/> it off ("N." "B.") the husks of the walnuts &amp; the sediment<lb/> of the juice that is left, may be boiled then again with<lb/> a little water + additional spice, + cleaned off as before<lb/> it will make a very good sauce for made dishes and all<lb/> sorts of brown Gravies.<lb/> <head>Lobster Sauce</head><lb/> Bruise the body of a Lobster, &amp; cut the flesh <hi rend="underline">thick</hi><lb/> melted butter, stew altogether, + give it a boil<lb/> season with a little pepper, salt, and a very small quan-<lb/> <hi rend="underline">tity of mace</hi>."
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/030|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
26<lb/> <head>Bread Sauce</head><lb/> Boil 1 pint of milk, with a little allspice, &amp; strain<lb/> it over as much grated bread as will fill a pint<lb/> basin 3 parts full, with a small onion in the<lb/> middle of the bread, let it stand covered close<lb/> down for 2 hours, then take out the onion, &amp; add<lb/> 1/4 pint of cream, with a small bit of butter, some<lb/> pepper &amp; salt, then simmer it altogether for 10 min<add>utes</add><lb/> <head>Another recipe for Bread Sauce.</head><lb/> Slice some bread very thin, boil the milk with<lb/> head onions, &amp; peppercorns, when tender, put in<lb/> the bread give it a momentary boil, &amp; put it in<lb/> a basin covered over, when cold rub it through a<lb/> sieve, &amp; add a tablespoonful of very thick cream<lb/> put it on the fire to make it quite <add>hot</add> for table add a little salt.<lb/> <head>Sauce for Beefsteaks</head><lb/> Equal parts of ale, wine, &amp; Ketchup a piece of butter &amp;<lb/> a little pepper, stir it over the fire in a small<lb/> saucepan, and pour it hot over the Beefsteaks.<lb/> <head>Oyster Sauce</head><lb/> Boil your Oysters in a very little water for a<lb/> <hi rend="underline">few</hi> minutes, then make a <hi rend="underline">very very</hi> thick<lb/> butter with the liquor from the Oyster
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/031|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
27<lb/> then thin it with some of your thick<lb/> est cream, cut your oyster in two and<lb/> put them into your butter, some peo-<lb/> -ple beard the oyster, but when you<lb/> require a quantity it is much better each<lb/> to do so.<lb/> Stuffing for a Boiled Turkey.<lb/> Make a Stuffing of bread, herbs, salt, pepper,<lb/> nutmeg, lemon peel, oysters or anchovy<lb/> a bit of butter, some suet &amp; an egg<lb/> put this into the crop of your Turkey<lb/> fasten up the skin, and boil it in<lb/> a floured cloth to make it white<lb/> Have ready a fine Oyster Sauce made<lb/> rich with cream, a little cream<lb/> and a spoonful of soy pour over the bird.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/032|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
28
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/033|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
29
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

