40<lb/> <head>Cheshire Pudding</head><lb/> 2 ozs. of Lump sugar (pounded) 2 oz of Butter 2 ozs.<lb/> of fine flour, 3 eggs, leaving out one white, 1/2 a pint<lb/> of new milk, melt the butter + mix all well toget<lb/> her put them into four shallow tins, + bake them<lb/> 1/2 an hour in a moderate oven. - White Wine Sauce<lb/> The tins should be on <unclear>sise</unclear> above another, + when<lb/> sent to table the pudding are put out upon<lb/> the other, the smallest of course at the top, it<lb/> is an <unclear>exelant</unclear> pudding.<lb/> <head>Mildred Dumplings</head><lb/> Take 1/4 lb. of bread crumbs, 1/4 lb. of suet, 1/4 lb. of sugar<lb/> and mix them up with 2 eggs - This quantity will<lb/> make 6 little dumplings. - Boil them half an hour.<lb/> <head>New fashioned Apple Pic</head><lb/> Pare and slice apples, scald them, beat them<lb/> with a spoon, with some o the liquor, add<lb/> grated Lemon peel + the juice of a Lemon<lb/> white sugar and a piece of butter. Put a<lb/> paste round the dish and cover it with<lb/> bars or flowers of paste.
