62<lb/> a dry day before they are ripe, + lay them separ-<lb/> -ately on a dish, beat + sift over them their weight<lb/> of refined sugar beaten fine, crush some ripe<lb/> strawberries, put them into your jar with the<lb/> weight of doubled refine sugar, cover close, let<lb/> them strand in a kettle of boiling water, till soft<lb/> + the syrup is out of them then strain them<lb/> through a muslin bag in a tossing pan, boil<lb/> <unclear>srim</unclear> + when cold, set them on again, put in the<lb/> whole strawberries, + set them over the fire till<lb/> milk cream, then take them off till cold, set them<lb/> in again + make them a little hotter, + do so<lb/> several times till clear, they must not boil or<lb/> the stalks will come off, when old put them<lb/> into jelly glasses with the stalks downwards fill<lb/> up the glasses with the syrup, tie down close.<lb/> <head>Strawberries in Wine</head> <lb/> Put a quantity of the finest strawberries into<lb/> a goosberry bottle, + strain in 3 large spoonfuls<lb/> of fine sugar, fill up with white wine.<lb/> <head>Cherries in Brandy</head><lb/> Weigh the finest Marellos, having cut off 1/2 the stalk<lb/> prick them with a needle, + chop them in a jar<lb/> or wide mouth bottle, pound 3/4 the weight of
