65<lb/> <head>To bottle Green or Red Currants Gooseberries &amp; Damsons</head><lb/> Cut your currants off the stalks, + when quite dry<lb/> put them into wine bottles, cork, + tie bladder<lb/> over them, + put them into a bottle of cold<lb/> water, + let them simmer for 20 minutes<lb/> (this water to be put in after the bottles are<lb/> in) then take them out of the bottle, + keep<lb/> them in a dry place, <hi rend="underline">but cool</hi>, Put gooseberries<lb/> + Damsons in Wide mouth bottles.<lb/> <head>Barberries in Bunches</head><lb/> Pick out all the largest bunches, then pick the<lb/> rest frozen the stalks, put them in as much<lb/> water as will make a syrup for your bunches<lb/> boil them till they are soft, then strain through<lb/> a sieve, + to every pint of juice put 1 1/2 lbs. of Lump<lb/> sugar, boil + skim it well, + to every pint of<lb/> syrup put 1/2 lt. of Banberries in bunches boil<lb/> them till they look very clear, + then put them<lb/> carefully into pots or glasses, + tie Brandy<lb/> papers over them.<lb/> <head>To preserve Raspberries &amp; Black Currants.</head><lb/> To every lt. of fruit add 3/4 of a lb. of sugar. + to every<lb/> quart of Gooseberries a pound of Sugar.
