109. <lb/> <head>Wines &amp;c &amp;c_</head><lb/> <head>Parsnip Wine-</head><lb/> To every 4 lbs. of Parsnips cleaned + quartered put a<lb/> gallon of water. Boil til they are quite soft + strain<lb/> the liquor off without bruising the Parsnips<lb/> To every gallon of the Liquor put 3 lbs Loaf sugar<lb/> + 1/2 oz. of <unclear>onder</unclear> of Tartar, When nearly cold put first<lb/> yeast to it - let it stand 4 days in a warm room<lb/> + then bring it up.<lb/> <head>Rhubarb Wine -</head><lb/> To every gallon of water (rain water is best) add 5 lbs.<lb/> of ripe Rhubarb cut into slices, let it stand 9<lb/> days, stirring it 3 times a day, + it is well to cover<lb/> the tub or pan with a Blanket or cloth. Strain<lb/> or squeeze the macerated substance through a<lb/> cream cloth, to every gallon of Liquor this <unclear>proenred</unclear><lb/> add 4 lbs. of white sugar, the juice of 2 Lemons<lb/> + the rind of one, then to fine it (1 oz. of Isinglass to<lb/> every 9 Gallons, + this of the operation may be this<lb/> <unclear>perfomed</unclear>. - Take about a pint of the Liquor in<lb/> which melt the Isinglass over the fire, be sure it<lb/> is cold, before putting it in the bowl, containing<lb/> the rest of the Liquor. That done, cask it.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/114|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
110.<lb/> when the formentation is over bring it down.<lb/> Bottle in March, + the following June it will be<lb/> fit for use.<lb/> <head>- White <hi rend="underline">Currant</hi> Wine. -</head> <lb/> 5 lbs of currants to the gallon, + 4 lbs. of Lump sug-<lb/> -ar mash your currant well with your hands<lb/> + then add 2 quarts of water to every 5 lbs. of cur-<lb/> -rants + let them stand till the next day<lb/> stirring it often then strain it through a sieve<lb/> + squeeze the stalks with your hands, + if you<lb/> have not wine enough, you next add a<lb/> little more water to your currants + do as before<lb/> then put your sugar + wine into the cask<lb/> as you make it, + 1 oz. of bitter Almonds to the<lb/> gallon cut through + put into the cask with<lb/> a few white raspberries, if you can get them<lb/> + stir it every day until the sugar is dissolved<lb/> + put in what Brandy you please.<lb/> <head>Conislip Wine.</head><lb/> Boil your water + let it stand to be cold, take one<lb/> Lemon + one Seville orange, peel them as thin<lb/> as you can, slice the pulp, add 3 1/2 lb. of Lump<lb/> sugar + four Quarts of flowers to a gallon. Stir<lb/> it very frequently for 3 or 4 days.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/115|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
111.<lb/> <head>Another receipt for Conslip Wine</head><lb/> To 1 Gallon of water put 3 1/2 lbs of Loaf sugar boil it<lb/> + <unclear>scum</unclear> it well for 1/2 an hour, then pour the liquor<lb/> on the peel of 2 Seville oranges + 2 Lemons, let<lb/> it stand till it is lukewarm then add 1 Gal-<lb/> -lon of <unclear>peips</unclear> + a little toasted bread with a little<lb/> yeast on it let it stand till the next day then<lb/> put it in your cask, + add the juice from<lb/> the oranges + Lemons + in about a fortnight stop<lb/> it down + put in a little Brandy, a few Rasins<lb/> is an improvement.<lb/> <head>- Ginger Wine. -</head><lb/> To a gallon of water take 3 1/2 lb.s of moist sugar, 1/2 lb<lb/> of Ginger bruised + boil it in a bag in 1 quart of water<lb/> put your fruit in your bowl + boil the rind<lb/> of your Lemon sugar + water together, + when<lb/> just warm put in the barrel + stir it well<lb/> every day for a week, + to every 3 Gallons of wine<lb/> put 2 spoonfuls of yeast + 1/4 oz. of Isinglass +<lb/> it will be fit to bottle 8 or 10 weeks 10 ozs. of ginger to<lb/> 8 Gallons of wine is best.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/116|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
112.<lb/> <head>- Almond Wine. -</head> <lb/> Boil 6 gallons of water, with 18 lbs. of loaf sugar<lb/> in it 3/4 of an hour, skimming it well when<lb/> nearly cold put a little yeast into it, put in<lb/> 6 lbs. of Rasins chopped a little 10 ozs. of bitter<lb/> Almonds blanched + beaten, 1 sliced Lemon<lb/> to every gallon, stir it frequently for several<lb/> days, then add a quart of Brandy to every<lb/> 10 gallons, stop it down and let it stand 4<lb/> months then bottle it. "N. B." a few sweet<lb/> Almonds will improve it, if you have white<lb/> Brandy it will be best for the colour + put<lb/> a little Isinglass in it. It is made in March<lb/> or April but would do I think made at the<lb/> latter end of the year.<lb/> <head>Green Gooseberry Wine.</head><lb/> To 4 quarts of green gooseberries bruised, 1 Gallon<lb/> of water let them stand 3 days stirring them<lb/> every day then strain them thro. a hair sieve<lb/> to 1 Gallon of liquor put 4 lbs. of Loaf sugar, then<lb/> put them into the vessel, let it stand a few<lb/> days, then add to it 2 oz. of Isinglass dissolved add<lb/> 1/2 a pint of Brandy to the gallon. The <sic>Goosberries</sic><lb/> should be just turning.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/117|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
113.<lb/> <head>- Gooseberry Wine. -</head> <lb/> To every lb. of gooseberries bruised put 1 quart of<lb/> water cold, let it stand 3 days, stirring it twice<lb/> a day to every gallon of juice when strained.<lb/> Put 3/2 lbs. of Lump sugar, when the wine has<lb/> been in the barrel some days. Add to every 5<lb/> Gallons 1 bottle of Gin + put in a little Isinglass<lb/> It must not be stopped down till it has<lb/> done <sic>formenting</sic>, + must stand in the barrel<lb/> for 4 months at least.<lb/> <head>White Elder Wine</head><lb/> Gather the berries ripe &amp; dry pick + bake them<lb/> in a slow oven, when done strain the liquor<lb/> through a hair sieve, + to every quart of it 3 quarts<lb/> of water, + 3 lbs. lump sugar to each gallon. Boil<lb/> the juice. water + sugar half an hour, taking off<lb/> the scum, When cool balm it + let it stand<lb/> Till it works then rinse the barrel with<lb/> Brandy put in the wine with 1 lb. of Malaga<lb/> Rasons, + 1 Lemon to each gallon. The Rasins<lb/> to be chopped a little. Add a little Isinglass<lb/> tied with a string or in bag fastened to the<lb/> bung of the barrel, stop it close + let it stand<lb/> two or three months.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/118|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
114<lb/> <head>Black Elder Wine</head><lb/> Take 5 galns of Elderberries, 6 gallons of water,<lb/> put them into a chopper and let them boil<lb/> an hour, then strain it off thro a sieve and<lb/> to every gallon of Liquor put 3 1/2 lbs. of good<lb/> sugar, and to every gallon 2 oz. of Ginger, boil<lb/> them all together 1/2 an hour then put it into<lb/> a clean pot, &amp; when new milk warm put<lb/> in a little yeast, &amp; let it stand until the<lb/> next day, then put it into a clean barrel<lb/> and let it work a few days before you bunge<lb/> it up - A pint of Brandy improves it add a few <add><unclear>Rasins</unclear></add> <head>Coltsfoot Wine.</head><lb/> To every gallon of water, take half a Gallon of<lb/> Coltsfoot blows. 3 lbs. of sugar, 1 Orange and<lb/> 1 Lemon, 1 lb. Rasins chopped a little, pot boiling<lb/> water over your blows + let them stand 24 hours<lb/> strain them off Take the <sic>peals</sic> of your Lemons<lb/> and the sugar boil them in the liquor, 1/2 an<lb/> hour, when cold put in your toast and <unclear>Balin</unclear><lb/> and your fruit dried. Stop it close in about<lb/> 14 days and let it stand 3 months.<lb/> Bottle it with a little Brandy.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/119|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
115<lb/> <head>Rasin Wine</head><lb/> 8 lbs of Rasins to 1 gallon of water chop them<lb/> boil the water when cold, put in the<lb/> rasins and let it remain until it has done<lb/> <sic>effervessing</sic> stirring it daily, strain the wine<lb/> through a bag + put it into your barrel<lb/> when done <sic>formenting</sic> stop it down + let<lb/> it stand 6 months rack it off. To make it<lb/> good put 1 gallon of Brandy to 20 gallons of Wine<lb/> <head>Another receipt for Rasin Wine</head><lb/> To every gallon of spring water add 8 lbs. of Malaga<lb/> Rasins, well chopped let it remain in a Tub 3 weeks<lb/> stirring it well every day, then press your fruit<lb/> quite dry + put your liquor into a cask to every<lb/> 5 gallons <sic>disolve</sic> 1 oz of Isinglass in a small quantity<lb/> of the liquor, setting it by the fire side when<lb/> quite cold put it to the Wine. - Be very particular<lb/> that the barrel it not <sic>stopt</sic> down before all<lb/> <sic>formentation</sic> has ceased, it should remain<lb/> in the barrel 12 months, then bottle it off<lb/> and add what Brandy you please.<lb/> "Mrs <unclear>Thornens</unclear> Rations Receipt."
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/120|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
116<lb/> <head>Orange Wine.</head><lb/> To every gallon water add 4 lbs. of Loaf sugar<lb/> + 10 oranges let the water and sugar boil to-<lb/> -gether 20 minutes let it stand until it is<lb/> near cold, + having peeled your oranges as<lb/> thin as possible, take the peel + put in<lb/> the Wine, with a little yeast, and the <lb/> next day put the Liquor in the cask with<lb/> the juice of the Oranges squeezed + cleaned<lb/> from the pulp seed +c. &amp;c; - It is best made<lb/> of 1/3 Seville Oranges, when you put the Wine<lb/> into the Barrel, add a little Isinglass if<lb/> <hi rend="underline">required</hi> about 1/4 of an ounce. - <lb/> Let it stand in the cask about 2 months.<lb/> <head>Another receipt for Orange Wine</head><lb/> To 1 gallon of water, add 3 1/2 lbs. of loaf sugar, boil<lb/> + scum it well, till quite clear, when cold<lb/> put in the juice of 8 Seville Oranges, with<lb/> 4 peels pared very thin, a little <unclear>Barm</unclear>,<lb/> put on a <unclear>toach</unclear> let it stand 12 hours, then<lb/> put it in you barrel, it may be stopped up<lb/> closer, letting it stand 3 or 4 months before it is bot-<lb/> tled. - A little before bottling add a little Brandy
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/121|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
117<lb/> <head>English White Wine</head><lb/> Boil 48 lbs. of Lisbon sugar, in 14 gallons of water<lb/> 20 minutes, taking off the scum as it rises<lb/> put it into a Tub, + when nearly cold<lb/> put in it 14 lbs. of Malaga Rasins, picked<lb/> and chopped, + when quite cold, add 2<lb/> gallons of strong ale, when the Ale is ready<lb/> to turn, let it stand 3 days, stirring it well<lb/> every day, Then put it into a cask with<lb/> a pint of Brandy. 1 lb. of sugar-candy, +<lb/> 1 oz. of Isinglass, it may be bottled in 10. or<lb/> 12 Months.<lb/> <head>Ginger Beer</head><lb/> Dissolve 10 lbs. of loaf sugar in 10 gallons of cold<lb/> water add to it the whites of 10 eggs well beaten<lb/> then boil it + scum it well, take 1/2 lb. of bruised<lb/> ginger + put it into the boiling water, boil it<lb/> well + strain it thro a fine sieve into a Tub to<lb/> the rind of 10 lemons when cold put it into<lb/> the cask with the juice of the lemons<lb/> add a small quantity of yeast. When fine<lb/> bottle it.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/005/001/122|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
118<lb/> <head>Another receipt for Ginger Beer</head><lb/> Mix 1 oz. of ginger powder, 1/2 oz. of cream Tartar<lb/> 1 Lemon sliced, 1 lb of Loaf sugar + 1 gallon of<lb/> water all up together, <unclear>scum</unclear> it over the fire<lb/> for 1/2 an hour ferment it in the usual manner<lb/> with yeast, then bottle it close in 1/2 pint bottles<lb/> In a few days it will be fit to drink.
