23<lb/> To make a good Plumb Cake.<lb/> Take a pound + half of Flour. rub in one<lb/> pound of Butter, + one pound of Sugar,<lb/> half a nutmeg grated. warm a pint<lb/> of new milk, + mix it with a quarter<lb/> of pint of nice new yeast. + five Eggs<lb/> well beaten. - mix these all well to<lb/> =gether, + beat it well. - then set it<lb/> before the Fire to rise for one Hour<lb/> frequently beating it during the time<lb/> have ready a pound + quarter of<lb/> Currants well wash'd + picked. +<lb/> plump'd in Brandy put these in<lb/> warm, beat the Cake well. put it<lb/> into a Pan well buttered, + bake it<lb/> Two Hours in a Moderate Oven.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/031|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
24<lb/> Artificial Oyster Sauce<lb/> Boil an anchovy in a quarter of a pint of<lb/> water, five pepper kernels, a blade of mace, +<lb/> a piece of Lemon Peel, till the anchovy is quite<lb/> dissolved then strain it off, + put in half a Glass<lb/> of white wine, + a little good Gravy, thick it with<lb/> Butter braided in Flour. - <lb/> <pb/> To make Tea Biscuits.-<lb/> Take one lb: of Flour, rub in half a lb: of Butter<lb/> + half a lb: of Sugar, grated + sifted, add carraway<lb/> Seeds if you please.- make this into a Paste with<lb/> three Eggs. roll it out + eat it in what form you<lb/> please. bake them in a slow Oven, of a <unclear>pice</unclear> light<lb/> brown. - keep them in Tin Cannister to make<lb/> them retain their Crispness. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/032|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
25<lb/> To Pickle Mushrooms M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi></hi> Barber Watton<lb/> Take the small Mushrooms whilst in Buttons, wash<lb/> them in clear spring water rubbing off all the brown<lb/> with a piece of Flannell, + a little salt. - have<lb/> ready a Sauce Pan of boiling water with a little<lb/> Salt in it. throw them in + boil them very slow<lb/> for a few minutes. - take off any Skim that<lb/> may arise, strain them thro' a cullender, with a<lb/> Clean cloth laid in it, cover them with the Cloth<lb/> + when they are well drain'd put them into wide<lb/> mouth'd Glasses with a little mace, white Pepper<lb/> + sliced nutmeg laid amongst them.- For the <lb/> Pickle which must be made ready + put to them<lb/> when they are both cold. in the following proportion<lb/> one quart of distill'd Vinegar, three quarters of a<lb/> pint of white wine, one ounce of Bay Salt, a little<lb/> Mace, Ginger, + white Pepper, boil it a quarter of<lb/> an Hour; when the Bottles are fill'd tye them<lb/> down close with a Bladder dipped in Vinegar. + then<lb/> with Paper.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/033|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
26<lb/> Honey Comb Cream<lb/> Take the Juice of two Lemons, + one Seville Orange<lb/> put to it a quarter of a lb of sugar powdered, +<lb/> let it stand all night upon the Peel of one of the<lb/> Lemons, + half the Peel of the orange; then take<lb/> the Peel out, + have ready a pint of Cream boiling<lb/> hot, set the Dish in which you wish to serve<lb/> this to Table, with the Juice in it upon the Floor<lb/> put the Cream into a Tea Pot, + pour it very slowly<lb/> into the Juice with as small a stream as you<lb/> can making it froth. - set it by till cold, +<lb/> then prick every Bubble with a Bodkin: + it will<lb/> look like Honey Comb.-<lb/> <pb/> Irish Stew - or Camp Pye<lb/> Take a Loin or a neck of Mutton, Cut it in steaks<lb/> + take away most of the Fat. put in sufficient<lb/> water to cover it. + add some Onions sliced, some<lb/> Potatoes, + Pepper, + Salt. when well stew'd serve it to<lb/> Table.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/034|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
27<lb/> Scalded Pudding<lb/> boil half a pint of new Milk pour it hot upon<lb/> three table spoonfuls of Flour. let it stand till<lb/> cold. braid it well + add three Eggs well beaten<lb/> a little nutmeg + a little Salt. - butter a <unclear>Bavon</unclear><lb/> that will Just hold this quantity + boil it an Hour<lb/> serve it to Table with wine sauce if you like it.<lb/> <pb/> Scotch Collops <lb/> Cut some slices off a Leg of Veal, dip them in the<lb/> yolk of a Egg with some Crumbs of bread grated<lb/> very fine, fry them in Butter of a light brown. then<lb/> put into the Pan, a little good Gravy, some pepper<lb/> + Salt, + thicken it with a piece of Butter braided<lb/> in Flour, put the veal again into the Pan to heat, <lb/> + serve it to Table either with a little dried Parsley<lb/> or sliced Lemon.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/035|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
28<lb/> Potted Beef.<lb/> Bake a piece of lean Beef, with a little water<lb/> + seasoning till it will pull all to pieces, then<lb/> beat it in a marble mortar adding the Gravy<lb/> by degrees. put in some pepper salt, + a little<lb/> beaten mace to your Taste. when well pound put<lb/> it into Potting Pans + pour a little Oil'd Butter<lb/> over it.-<lb/> <pb/> Pound Cakes M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi></hi> Elgar<lb/> Take a lb: of Flour, a lb of sugar powdered, a lb<lb/> of Butter, + a lb: of Currants. beat the Sugar butter<lb/> together till it be like Cream, then add the Eggs<lb/> + beat them all together, + then by degrees put in<lb/> the Flour beating it all the time, then put in the<lb/> Currants plump'd in Brandy. - bake them in a <lb/> slow oven. - <lb/> N: B. - eight yolks + six white are the number <lb/> of Eggs, + it is best to beat the Butter first alone + then<lb/> add the Eggs. then the Sugar, + then the Flour. +c-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/036|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
29<lb/> Diet Bread M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi></hi> Elgar<lb/> Beat 5 Eggs well, then put a lb: of Flour well dried<lb/> a lb: of Sugar, + a few Seeds. Butter the Pan well<lb/> + bake it in an Oven not over hot. - <lb/> <pb/> Cracknels M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Elgar.<lb/> Take three quarters of lb: of Flour, three quarters<lb/> of a lb: of Sugar. + half a lb: of Butter, mix<lb/> these well together, + add 3 Eggs. make them<lb/> in what form you please. + bake them in a slow<lb/> oven. - <lb/> <pb/> Ginger Bread M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi></hi> Elgar.<lb/> Take a lb: of Flour, two table spoonfuls of Sugar pow<lb/> =dered, one Spoonful of Ginger powdered. melt a<lb/> quarter of a lb: of Butter in three quarters of<lb/> a lb: of Treacle, + add three spoonfus of Brandy<lb/> make these ingredients into a Paste, + bake them<lb/> in what form you please. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/037|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
32<lb/> Printing Tub for Linnen. -<lb/> Mix Lamp Black with Liquid Varnish, or with boiled<lb/> Linseed Oil, quite thick; then rub it on the Printing<lb/> Balls, dab the Letters with this Mixture, + press it upon<lb/> the Linnen. - let it be well dried before it is wash'd<lb/> <pb/> To collar a Breast of Mutton.<lb/> Cut off the red Shin, bone it, + take out the Gristles, <lb/> season it with Pepper + Salt, + a few Herbs, roll it<lb/> up tight into a Collar, + skewer it close, then either<lb/> half fry it, or roast it then put into a Stew Pan<lb/> with some good Gravy made of the Bones. it is best<lb/> to lay some Hours in the Seasoning after it is roll'd<lb/> up before cooking. stew it tender with some sliced<lb/> Carrots, small Onions, sweet Herbs, + a few slices of <lb/> Turnips. when quite enough thick the Gravy with a <lb/> Piece of Butter braided in Flour; add a little Mush<lb/> =room Ketchup if not sufficiently Savory. -<lb/> serve it to Table with hard Eggs. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/038|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
33<lb/> Lemon Solid - <lb/> Take a pint of Cream, make it very sweet, put<lb/> in a piece of cinamon then put it to boil, have<lb/> ready in your dish the juice of one Lemon<lb/> with the peel grated in, when your cream boils<lb/> pour it thro' a clean cloth, then give one good<lb/> stir round, let it stand till cold, cut some<lb/> oranges in slices + stick them upright in your<lb/> cream. - <lb/> <pb/> Currant Jelly. <hi rend="underline">M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Dowing.</hi><lb/> squeeze the juice of your currants boil + skim it<lb/> until it is clear, have sugar in the proportion<lb/> of a pound to a pint of the juice, sifted + set<lb/> before the fire to dry, then pour the juice upon<lb/> it stir it till all the sugar is melted, after<lb/> which put it into Jars when cold put paper<lb/> steeped in brandy upon your jelly tye them down<lb/> close. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/039|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
1000.<lb/> A Good Plumb Cake. - M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> <hi rend="underline">Gwya.</hi><lb/> 34<lb/> 4 lb. fresh Butter wash it in Rose Water; 4 lb.<lb/> fine Flour, beat your Butter to a Cream strew<lb/> in the Flour a little at a time, 30 Eggs the<lb/> yolks beaten by themselves into a curd, then<lb/> put to the Flour, the same with the whites<lb/> 3/4 of a lb: Lump Sugar sifted, 5 lb: currants<lb/> well washed, + pick'd, + dried, a pint of Jack<lb/> 1/2 an oz. Mace, 1/2 oz cinnamon, a nutmeg<lb/> a lb: almonds blanch'd + split thin, a 1/4 lb. <lb/> citron, + the same of orange Pell. When well<lb/> mix'd together put it in the Hoop two Hours<lb/> will bake it. - put on what sweetmeats<lb/> you please. -<lb/> Iceing for the Cake<lb/> The Whites of 6 Eggs, a lb. + 1/2 lump sugar<lb/> sifted, + beaten well with the whites of the<lb/> Eggs, put it on the Cake at the oven mouth<lb/> it must not be <unclear>shoved</unclear> till quite cold, <lb/> it must be put in the oven to harden the Icing.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/040|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
35<lb/> Ramakins.<lb/> a lb. of Cheshire Cheese bet it well in a Marble<lb/> Mortar, add the Crumb of a penny Roll, a 1/2<lb/> lb: butter, + the yolk of 2 Eggs beat these well<lb/> together, toast a piece of Bread + lay it at<lb/> the bottom of the dish set it upon a hot<lb/> hearth, when cool'd brown it with a <unclear>Salamani</unclear><lb/> <pb/> Vinegar. M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Johnson<lb/> To one Gallon of Water, 1 lb. 1/2 of the coarsest <lb/> Sugar Boil it a quarter of an Hour when<lb/> lukewarm work it with yeast turn it + Jill<lb/> it wh as it works over, paper it down + set<lb/> it in the Jun. - <lb/> <pb/> To Grill Almonds<lb/> Take Jordan Almonds + their weight in Sugar<lb/> a little water let them boil. scum them well<lb/> do not blanch your almonds let them <add>boil</add> to a <lb/> candy, toss them in a skillet till they cool<lb/> put them on paper. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/041|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
36<lb/> Dorset Breakfast Cakes<lb/> two lb. flour, 2 Eggs 1/2 a pint of Yeast a little<lb/> salt, mix it with new milk + some cream make<lb/> it into cakes put it before the fire to rise an<lb/> Hour, bake it in a quick oven 5 or 10 minutes is<lb/> sufficient. - <lb/> <pb/> <hi rend="underline">To preserve Peaches + Apricots in Brandy.</hi><lb/> The Peaches + Apricots must be gather'd before they<lb/> are quite ripe the poollings must be rubb'd<lb/> off fifty Peaches + apricots 3 lb: of a half of Lump sugar<lb/> make it into a syrup cover all the fruit with<lb/> the syrup, let them boil very gently not to break<lb/> the skins when tender take them off the fire<lb/> let them stand in the preserving pan all night<lb/> cover'd wh close, the next day <del>cover them up</del> boil<lb/> them up again put them on Plates to drain let the<lb/> syrup boil a 1/4 of an Hour after the fruit is taken<lb/> out put the fruit in jars + when the syrup is almost<lb/> cold put an equal quantity of Brandy + syrup
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/042|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
37<lb/> into your jars a cup of each at a time by which<lb/> means it is mix'd as you put it into your jars.<lb/> tye them up close, they will keep a year. if it<lb/> ferments fresh syrup + Brandy much be put to <unclear>them</unclear><lb/> <pb/> French Cheese<lb/> one lb. cheese curd squeezed very dry pound it in a<lb/> mortar with sugar to your taste half a Teacup<lb/> of white wine, half a quarter of a pound of fresh<lb/> Butter and some nutmeg put it in your Tin +<lb/> let it stand three or four Hours to drain<lb/> a little Custard at the bottom of your dish turn<lb/> the Cheese out + put some sweetmeats round it<lb/> <pb/> Lemon Cheese.<lb/> To a quart of good Cream grate the Peel of three<lb/> Lemons, put the juice of four, sugar it to your<lb/> taste. whisk it up five minutes, take a Towel<lb/> + double it put it in a fine sieve, with the Cheese<lb/> upon it let it stand three days put it in a <unclear>Rap</unclear><gap/><lb/> serve it up. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/043|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
38<lb/> Paste for Tarts.<lb/> One pound of flour + one of Lump sugar 3/4 of a lb<lb/> of Butter mix it up together + beat it with<lb/> a rolling pin. - <lb/> <pb/> Cheery Brandy <hi rend="underline">Miss Piggot</hi><lb/> To a Gallon of Brandy 4lb. of Cherries two lb of<lb/> Lump sugar half an oz of cinnamon one oz of <lb/> cloves set them in the sun a fortnight in Bottles<lb/> close cork'd.<lb/> <pb/> <hi rend="underline">Ratifia</hi><lb/> To a Gallon of Brandy 500 apricot kernels take<lb/> care not to break the kernels put them into a stone<lb/> bottle add to them 2 quarts of the best white wine<lb/> with the Brandy at it remain one Month shaking<lb/> the Bottle every day then put two pound of Fine<lb/> Loaf sugar of a fortnight after strain it thro'<lb/> a flannel Bag putting in just a Glass of Milk.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/044|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
''39<lb/> Orange Wine<lb/> To a Gallon of spring water put 3 lb of Loaf Sugar<lb/> + to every twenty Gallons put the Whites of 7 Eggs<lb/> with the shells Boil + skim it an hour when<lb/> cool put to it some ale yeast + the Rinds<lb/> of <hi rend="underline">130 Or</hi>anges into two quarts of Fresh Brandy<lb/> let it steep two days then pour the Brandy with<lb/> the juice of the Oranges + the Liquor into your<lb/> vessel put the Rinds into a Bag with a stone<lb/> to sink it let the Liquor be strain'd thro'a<lb/> Jelly Bag into the Vessel. -<lb/> <pb/> Lemon Drops<lb/> Take good loaf sugar finely powdered grate in the<lb/> Rind of a Lemon + Moisten the Sugar with the<lb/> juice Melt it in a small tin sauce pan over<lb/> a Charcoal fire drop them with a Tea spoon<lb/> upon writing Paper + dry them in a slow oven<lb/> <pb/> Lemonade.<lb/> Steep the Peel of 12 Lemons 5 or 6 hours in two<lb/> quarts of water + to the juice of the Lemons put<lb/> sugar to your taste. put it in a Bottle, but<lb/> first let it stand till the sugar is melted + strain it<lb/> thro' a Bag. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/045|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
40<lb/> Orgeat.<lb/> Blanch + beat a pound + half of sweet Almonds +<lb/> rather <del>Bitter</del> better then half an oz of Bitter ones<lb/> have ready a pint + half of Barley Water let be quite<lb/> cold put it a little at a time to your almonds<lb/> sweeten it to your taste put in two spoonfuls of<lb/> orange flower water squeeze it thro' a Bag<lb/> to get the Bitterness out of the Almonds<lb/> when you use it put 4 spoonfuls of thick cream.<lb/> <pb/> College Puddings.<lb/> Grate <del>the</del> a Penny Roll, put a lb of suet, a lb<lb/> of currants the yolks of six Eggs well beaten, a <lb/> glass + half of white wine some nutmeg grat'd<lb/> sweeten it to your taste mix it well together<lb/> an hour will boil them, this quantity will make<lb/> 5 or 7 puddings. - Melt the Butter with a little<lb/> wine of you like. -<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/046|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
41<lb/> Lemon Pudding.<lb/> Take a quarter of a lb of suet the same of almonds<lb/> beat them into a paste six oz of fine sugar the<lb/> yolks of eight Eggs the peel of three Lemons grated<lb/> the juice of four, half a pound of Butter mix'd<lb/> well together stirring it the same way bake<lb/> it in a light crust with bars laid across of Paste<lb/> upon the Top. - <lb/> <pb/> Lemon Cream<lb/> Take the juice of sour Lemons with the whites<lb/> of three Eggs + a pint of Water beat them well<lb/> put it over a slow <del>oven</del> Fire stirring it all the<lb/> time with the yolks of six Eggs till it is thick<lb/> enough to put into your Glasses or Cups. - <lb/> <pb/> Soup Crepee <hi rend="underline">W. Caton.</hi><lb/> Make very good gravy Soup in which boil a<lb/> quarter of a pint of of old Pease, take a suffi<lb/> =cient quantity of turnips, carrots, onions, celery<lb/> Lettuces, + fried Bread boil them all in the<lb/> Gravy till tender then rub them thro' a Sieve<lb/> afterwards boil the soup till it is a proper<lb/> thickness season it to your taste. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/047|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
42<lb/> A White Wine. -<lb/> Take twelve lb of Lump Sugar 6 gallons of Water<lb/> 6 lb of good Sun Raisins cut them + boil them all<lb/> together an Hour, when cold add 4 ox of Elder<lb/> flowers fresh gather'd, the next day put in 8 large<lb/> spoonfulls of syrup of Lemons, + four spoonfulls<lb/> of good Ale yeast, in about two days <add>after</add> put it in a<lb/> vessel when fine Bottle it. - <lb/> It drinks best the 2<hi rend="superscript">d</hi>. year. -<lb/> <pb/> Oysters to Pickle<lb/> Scald the Oysters in their own Liquor, then take them<lb/> out, beard them; strain the Liquor to prevent any pieces<lb/> of Shell going in, boil the Liquor with white Pepper,<lb/> Cloves, Mace, + Vinegar. - Then put in the Oysters +<lb/> tye them down. - <lb/> <pb/> Italian Cheese<lb/> Dissolve 2 Oz of Isinglass in a pint of Water strain<lb/> it + add the Juice of 2 sweet, + four Seville oranges<lb/> + one Lemon sweeten it to your Taste, whip it up till<lb/> it looks like spunge, + then put it into a mould to cool.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/048|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
43<lb/> Another way to make Italian Cheese<lb/> Mix one quart of thick raw Cream, with half a pound<lb/> of fine sugar powdered, grate in the peel of one Lemon<lb/> + put in the Juice of three. whisk it one way for<lb/> half an Hour, then put it in a Sieve with a cloth<lb/> at the bottom laid quite smooth, let it stand all <lb/> night, + be kept in a cool place. take care not<lb/> to break it when you turn it out.<lb/> <pb/> To make a Lemon colour'd wash for <unclear>varnish</unclear><add>ings</add><lb/> Dissolve 4 pounds of green Copperas in one Gallon<lb/> of clear spring water, then make a Pail full<lb/> of good common white wash, put three pints of<lb/> the Copperas water to it. + it is fit for use. - <lb/> <pb/> Apple Fritters - <lb/> Take the yolks of three Eggs, + the white of one<lb/> two large spoonfuls of Flour, + one of Sugar<lb/> a little nutmeg, + grated Ginger, with a little<lb/> milk. beat it to a nice smooth Batter. + add some<lb/> apple chopped fine. fry the fritters of a nice brown.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/049|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
44<lb/> Calves Foot Jelly.<lb/> boil 4 calves Feet in 2 quarts of water till re=<lb/> =duced to one, when cold take off the Fat from the <lb/> Top, + the sediment from the bottom. then add half<lb/> a pint of white wine, the Juice of three Lemons, + the<lb/> rind of one grated in a quarter of a pound of<lb/> Sugar, the whites of three Eggs well beaten. put all<lb/> in a saucepan, Just boil it, + run it thro' a Jelly<lb/> Bag, till it is clear. -<lb/> To make Lemonade<lb/> Put in a large Pitcher, three quarters of a pound<lb/> of fine Sugar, half a pint of Lemon Juice, + the Rind<lb/> of four Lemons, half a pint of white wine, + one<lb/> Quart of boiling water, let it stand till cold; Then<lb/> add a pint of boiling milk. let it stand all night<lb/> + strain it through a Jelly Bat. - M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Oliver
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/050|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
45<lb/> To make Curry Powder<lb/> Take of white Pepper 2 oz - Coriander Seeds<lb/> 4 oz. - Turmeric Root 6 oz - Ginger 2 oz-<lb/> all these ingredients to be finely powder'd, +<lb/> then mix'd together Cayenne Pepper, + salt to<lb/> your Taste when you make your Curry. -<lb/> To make the Curry -<lb/> Take some Veal, or Fowl, or any Meat you choose<lb/> + boil it for a short time in very little water.<lb/> then add about a pint of Milk with an onion<lb/> cut in pieces after it is boiled sufficiently tender<lb/> (if your Curry is of Veal, + you have any large<lb/> bones in it take them out, take a little of the<lb/> liquor + mix with it two tea spoonfuls of the Powders<lb/> a little Cayenne + salt, then mix all together, <lb/> + stir in a small piece of butter roll'd in flour<lb/> + thick it up. - Serve it to Table with some<lb/> rice in another Dish boil'd dry + tender. - <lb/> Mrs B.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/051|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To make Ginger Beer. - <lb/> Mix one ounce of Ginger powder, with half<lb/> an ounce of Cream of Tartar. then add one<lb/> Lemon sliced, two pounds of Lump Sugar, +<lb/> one Gallon of Water. - simmer these over the <lb/> Fire for half an Hour after it first boils.<lb/> when cool ferment it as you do Beer with a <lb/> table spoonfull of new yeast. - bottle it in<lb/> half pint of pint Bottles, such as are used<lb/> for soda water, + in a few Days it will be<lb/> fit to drink.<lb/> <pb/> Colouring for Soups + Gravy's<lb/> Four Ox of lump sugar, a Gill of Water, half<lb/> an oz of Butter, set them over a slow fire in a<lb/> small tossing pan, stirring it with a wooden spoon<lb/> till it gets brown, put in a little beaten mace<lb/> + a little pepper + salt. skim it well + when<lb/> cold bottle + cork it. colour Soups + Gravies<lb/> with this as necessary -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

