'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/053|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To preserve + increase yeast<lb/> Lady Charles Townshead<lb/> Get a two Gallon stone Bottle, which must be<lb/> never more than two thirds fill'd. -<lb/> Boil 2 ounces of Hops in one quart of water<lb/> twenty minutes. + whilst hot stir in half a<lb/> pound of Flour. add one quart more Water<lb/> to the Hops, + boil them again slowly for one<lb/> Hour, then strain them again upon that which<lb/> was first boil'd; stir it well together; + when<lb/> milk warm, mix in half a pint of good<lb/> new yeast. - when cold bottle + cork it. + keep<lb/> it in a cellar, or very cool place; for if it<lb/> is warm + ferments in the Bottle it will<lb/> break the Bottle. -<lb/> alway reserve a pint to renew the quantity<lb/> again, by which means, no new yeast will<lb/> be wanting after the first making. - <lb/> When used put in the proportion of a<lb/> pint to twenty four pounds of Flour. with<lb/> warm water to make it a proper consistence
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/054|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
but mix it first with a small quantity,<lb/> + make a hole in the middle of the<lb/> Flour, + mix it in + then let it stand<lb/> in a warm place all night. - in the <lb/> morning put in the rest of the warm<lb/> water make the Dough rather stiff, <lb/> let it be breaded well + stand some<lb/> Hours longer to rise, than if made with<lb/> the common yeast. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/055|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
46<lb/> To make Flour yeast<lb/> Put to 3 quarters of a pound of Flour, 2 quarts<lb/> of Water mix it quite smooth, then boil it till it<lb/> forms a substance, then stir in a quarter of a pound<lb/> of moist Sugar, set it to work when milk warm<lb/> with a quarter of a pint of nice new yeast let it<lb/> stand near the Fire twenty four Hours by which time<lb/> it will have done fermenting. - let it settle, pour<lb/> off the Liquid which rise on the Top, this must<lb/> be used as other yeast only putting in rather<lb/> a larger portion of it than common yeast. -<lb/> If you wish to increase the quantity, 4 spoonfuls<lb/> of this yeast will make a new Portion of half the <lb/> quantity, by which means you will have a<lb/> fresh supply. -<lb/> Arrow Root pudding - M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Oliver.<lb/> Boil 2 table Spoonfuls of Arrow Root in pint of new<lb/> milk, with a piece of Lemon Peel, + a piece of Cinnamon<lb/> when cold add Sugar to your taste; put in four Eggs<lb/> well beaten, put it in a Bason previously buttered<lb/> + boil it an Hour. serve it with wine Sauce-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/056|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
47<lb/> To make Shrub.-<lb/> To every Gallon of Brandy or Rum put the Juice<lb/> of twenty Oranges, + Lemons half of each sort, + the<lb/> Peels of them pared off very thin. + 2 - lb: of Loaf<lb/> Sugar to every Gallon let it stand a Week. shaking<lb/> it every day. then pour off the fine + bottle it for<lb/> use. - <lb/> Lemon Solid - - very good<lb/> Take a pint of Cream, boil it with a 1/4 of a lb: of<lb/> Sugar, + a piece of Cinnamon, have ready in the<lb/> Dish you mean to serve it to Table the Juice of 2<lb/> large Lemons with the Peel of one grated in - <lb/> when the Cream boils pour it into the Dish thro'<lb/> a Tiffany Sieve. give it a stir round, + let it<lb/> stand till cold. have ready some candied Orange<lb/> Peel + stick them cut in thin Slices upright in the<lb/> Cream to ornament it. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/057|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
48<lb/> Restorative Jelly<lb/> Pearl Barley, Rice, Sago, + candied Eringo Root of<lb/> each one ounce, let these be well wash'd except the<lb/> Eringo. put into 2 quarts of Water + boil a quarter<lb/> of an Hur, then strain it off, + drink a tea cup full<lb/> of this Liquor an Hour before Breakfast Dinner<lb/> + Supper, if not sweet enough add a little more<lb/> Sugar. - you may put in a little milk with<lb/> each Dose if you please but that must not be<lb/> heated with it. - <lb/> Lemon Cheesecakes<lb/> Take the yolks of five Eggs, a 1/4 of a lb: of clari=<lb/> =fied Butter, the Juice of 2 Lemons + the Rind of one<lb/> grated in, the Crumb of a penny Roll grated in also<lb/> set this over the Fire, + stir it till it thickens after<lb/> having sweeten'd it to your taste. have ready your<lb/> Pans with nice puff Paste at the bottom. put<lb/> in a little of this into each Pan, + bake them<lb/> of a nice light brown. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/058|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
49<lb/> Yellow Lemon Cream<lb/> Take a pint of water, the Juice of four Lemons<lb/> let the Peel of one Lemon be steep'd in the Water 12<lb/> Hours before you make it take out the Peel, add the<lb/> Juice strain'd, a 1/4 of a lb. of Sugar, then add the<lb/> yolks, of six Eggs + the whites of three, set it over<lb/> a slow Fire till it is as thick as cream, put it<lb/> into Jelly Glasses. - <lb/> To make Shrewsbury Cakes.<lb/> Take one lb: of Flour, put in half a lb: of Sugar<lb/> powdered, grate in a little nutmeg. rub in half a<lb/> lb: of Butter. - then with the yolk, of two Eggs + the <lb/> white of one make this into a Paste, roll it out<lb/> + cut it into Cakes. bake them of a light brown<lb/> Puff Cakes for Tea<lb/> Take half a pound of Butter, half a pint of Water, <lb/> + 2 oz powder Sugar, boil these together three Minutes<lb/> Stir in half a lb, 1 oz. of Flour, when very thick take<lb/> it off the Fire, + put in a 1/4 of a lb. of Carrots<lb/> when cold beat in well 8 Eggs. - put it into Cups + bake<lb/> it in a slow oven. you must take care it does not burn<lb/> in the boiling.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/059|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
50<lb/> Maccaroni without Cheese<lb/> Stew a 1/4 of a lb: of Maccaroni in a pint<lb/> of water, (having first wash'd + steep'd it), till<lb/> it is quite tender. - then thick it with<lb/> Cream + a bit of Butter braided in Flour<lb/> + send it to Table. -<lb/> Small Mead -<lb/> Put one pound + half of Honey to one Gallon<lb/> of Water, + to every Gallon of Water the white<lb/> of an Egg well beaten let it boil half an<lb/> Hour skimming it well all the time, pour<lb/> it into a broad Vessel to work. + when cool put<lb/> in a Toast well covered with good yeast. let<lb/> it stand twenty four Hours then turn it into a Vessel<lb/> + when it has done working stop it down close<lb/> let it stand a month. then bottle it off set<lb/> it in a cool place, + it is fit to drink in a<lb/> very short time. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/060|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
51<lb/> Syrrup for preserving Fruit.<lb/> Take the same weight of Lump sugar, as the <lb/> Fruit to be preserved. powder the sugar +<lb/> sprinkle it upon the Fruit let it stand all night<lb/> + draw the liquor is drawn from the Fruit.<lb/> strain it + boil the Liquor skim it well, then<lb/> put in the Fruit, boil it then very gently so as<lb/> not to break the Fruit to pieces. - if Apricots<lb/> are to be done to dry, only pour the Syrrup on<lb/> them hot. let them stand till almost cold,<lb/> put them on writing Paper over Tins, + set<lb/> them in a cool Oven to dry, dipping them<lb/> once in a Day in the Syrrup. - when quite<lb/> tender finish drying them in the Sun. - Keep<lb/> them in a dry place in paper in a tin<lb/> Cannister. - Cherries preserved the same<lb/> as apricots are excellent. - or put into Jars<lb/> filling them half full, + then filling them up with<lb/> Brandy.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/061|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
52<lb/> French Bread.<lb/> Take one pint of good milk, + half a pint of<lb/> water, a tea spoonfull of salt, a slice of Butter<lb/> melted in the milk, + water, the yolks of two<lb/> Eggs + the white of one beaten well. Mix these<lb/> all together into a quarter of a pint of<lb/> good new yeast. - put these into four pounds<lb/> of Flour well dried, make it up into Paste<lb/> set it to rise half an Hour by the Fire. -<lb/> then make it into Bowls, + set them also to<lb/> rise half an Hour before you put them in<lb/> the Oven. - Rasp them whilst warm. -<lb/> Tea Biscuits -<lb/> Rub one pound of Butter into two pounds of Flour<lb/> put to it one pound of Sugar powdered, make<lb/> this in a paste with 4 or 5 Eggs according to<lb/> the size of the Eggs. roll this paste out when<lb/> well work'd, + cut it into Biscuits, which<lb/> bake in a quick oven. but take care they do<lb/> not burn.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/062|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
53<lb/> French Pudding -<lb/> Take the Crumb of a three penny Rool, boil<lb/> a pint of Milk with a Laurel Leaf in it,<lb/> pour it hot upon the Bread, put in two oz.<lb/> of Butter. braid in 4 apples cored + boil'd <lb/> soft. grate in a little Nutmeg sweeten it to your<lb/> Taste. let the Milk + Bread be quite cold<lb/> before the apples are put in. then add 4<lb/> Eggs well beaten, + a little candied orange<lb/> Peel if you like it. put a puff paste at<lb/> the bottom of the Dish, + bake it a Hour.<lb/> Lemon Cheese Cakes -<lb/> Take 4 large Lemons, squeeze out the Juice, +<lb/> scrape out all the Pulp. boil the Peels three<lb/> Hours. then bruise them in a marble morta<lb/> + add 8 large spoonfuls of powdered sugar. half<lb/> a pound of Butter, half the Juice of the Lemons<lb/> the yolks of six Eggs, the whites of <unclear>tro</unclear>. + a little<lb/> grated bread. Mix these well, + bake them in puff<lb/> Paste.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/063|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
54<lb/> Honey Comb Cream -<lb/> Take the Juice of two Lemons + one Seville<lb/> Orange, peel them + put half the peel to infuse<lb/> in the Juice, with two Glasses of Sherry, + a 1/4<lb/> of a pound of Lump Sugar all night. - have<lb/> then a pint of Cream made scalding hot let<lb/> the Cream be will sweeten'd put it into a Tea<lb/> Pot, set the Dish in which the Juice + wine is<lb/> upon the Ground, (which must be what you mean<lb/> to send it to Table in.) hold your urn as high<lb/> as you can, + pour the Cream into the Dish with<lb/> as small a Stream as you can, moving your<lb/> Hand, so that the Stream may bubble all over<lb/> the Dish. - set it by to cool, (for the Cream must<lb/> be poured in scalding hot.) when cold prick<lb/> every Bubble with a Bodkin to make it look<lb/> like Honey Comb. - this is an excellent<lb/> sweet Dish. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/064|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
55<lb/> Bath Buns<lb/> Rub a 1/4 of a pound of Butter into one pound<lb/> of Flour, add a 1/4 of a pound of sugar, + a<lb/> few Caraway Seeds, then beat 6 Eggs, + put<lb/> to them 6 spoonfuls of Rice yeast, + a little<lb/> warm Cream. make this into a nice Dough, lay<lb/> it before the Fire to rise. then work in half<lb/> a pound more Flour well dried. make this<lb/> up into Buns bake them in a moderate Oven.<lb/> Elder Wine<lb/> Gather Elder Berries when dry, strip them from<lb/> the Stalks, + to every quart of Berries put<lb/> 1 pint of Damsons, + a few Blackberries +<lb/> Sloes, - + two quarts of water. boil them well<lb/> for one Hour. strain it off thro' a Hair Sieve<lb/> + to every quart of this Liquor put one pound <lb/> of Lisbon sugar. - when all dissolved + cold<lb/> set it to work with a Toast spread with nice<lb/> yeast. - when cold put it into your vessel<lb/> with
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

