'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/066|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Lipeck Damisons, 1 quart of sloes<lb/> N: B: 15. quarts of Elder Berries. 30 quarts<lb/> of Water. 2. quarts of Black-Berries. 28 lb.<lb/> of Sugar. + 6 lb. of Raisins make a<lb/> sufficient quantity to fill dry ten Gallon<lb/> vessel. allow 6 quarts water for waste in boiling
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/067|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
56<lb/> one pound of Raisins chopp'd to every Gallon.<lb/> + when it has done hissing bung it close,<lb/> putting in a glass of Brandy to every Gallon<lb/> of wine. - let it stand till Christmas then<lb/> bottle it off. it will keep for years, + is nearly<lb/> as good as Port.-<lb/> an Excellent Fish Sauce - M<hi rend="superscript">c</hi>. Gough<gap/><lb/> Take walnuts when proper to pickle, bruise them<lb/> in a mortar, + squeeze away the Juice thro' a<lb/> Cloth, let the liquor stand to settle, strain off the<lb/> clear, + to every pint put one pound of ancho<lb/> -vies, 1. dram of mace, + one of Cloves. + two of<lb/> Jamaica Pepper. boil these together till the<lb/> anchovies are quite dissolved add to this half<lb/> a pint of best white wine Vinegar, a dozen sha<lb/> =lotts, + a little Garlick; let these all boil till<lb/> they are tender. strain this so as to have it<lb/> clear, bottle it for use when cold. - when you<lb/> make the Fish sauce put some of this into<lb/> a little good Gravy, + thick it with Butter. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/068|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
57<lb/> Hollow Biscuits - <lb/> Take three lb: of Flour, make a hole in the<lb/> middle break in a <del>lb</del> <add>oz</add> of a lb of Butter in small<lb/> pieces. then pour in three quarters of a pint of<lb/> boiling water, which will dissolve the Butter<lb/> stir it round into a thin batter; let it stand<lb/> till cool, then add a 1/4 of a pint of nice<lb/> new yeast, work this into stiff Paste as for<lb/> Bread, let it lay an Hour rising, then make<lb/> it up into small Cakes, prick them on the Top,<lb/> lay them upon Tins, + set at a distance from<lb/> the Fire so as to get a little Heat, let them <lb/> rise half an Hour in this manner, then bake<lb/> them in a slow oven. when a nice colour<lb/> take them out, + set them in again to dry<lb/> after the Bread is drawn. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/069|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
58<lb/> To make whole Rice Pudding - <lb/> Take a Tea Cup full of whole Rice, wash<lb/> pick it well, set it over the Fire with a little<lb/> water to burst it, then put in some milk, +<lb/> let it boil tender; take care to have the Rice<lb/> very thick; then add three large spoonfulls of<lb/> sugar, two Eggs beaten well, + a little nutmeg<lb/> butter a Bason to boil it in that will exactly<lb/> hold it, + boil it three quarters of an Hour.<lb/> Sugar Bowl Cake.<lb/> Take 1 lb: of Lump Sugar finely beaten + sifted<lb/> half a lb: of Flour well dried, one lb: of Eggs. -<lb/> Beat the whites to a high Froth, then beat<lb/> the yolks, by themselves, + add them to the whites<lb/> then the sugar, + then the Flour; half an Hour<lb/> will bake it in a quick oven, but not too<lb/> hot. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/070|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
59<lb/> Milk Punch - or Lethee' M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Bowen. - <lb/> Steep the Peel of eighteen Lemons in a quart of<lb/> Rum, three Days; then add three quarts of Rum, + five<lb/> quarts of Water; + three pounds of Lump Sugar, + the<lb/> Juice of the eighteen Lemons; stir all together. <lb/> Then take two quarts of Milk scalding hot + put<lb/> to these ingredients. - let it stand two Hours + <lb/> clear it thro' a Jelly Bag. - <lb/> N: B: - half or one third of Brandy is an<lb/> improvement<lb/> <pb/> <hi rend="underline">Cream Cheese</hi> M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> <unclear>Mirehorse</unclear><lb/> To three parts of new milk from the Cow, put one<lb/> quart of water, rather warmer than the Milk.<lb/> add a small quantity of Rennet sufficient to<lb/> turn it into Curd, when it has stood long enough<lb/> to separate then turn it all out into a cloth,<lb/> holding it by the pan corners, and shaking it<lb/> well till all the w<add>h</add>ey is run from the curd,<lb/> put it with the cloth into a cheese vat, press
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/071|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
it gently, do this two, or three times, into a differ<lb/> =ent part of the cloth, when it is sufficiently hard<lb/> to bear cutting take a piece of bobbin, and drag<lb/> it thro' the cheese, dividing it into as many parts<lb/> as you please, according to the thickness you <hi rend="underline">like</hi>.<lb/> <pb/> White Currant Shurb - D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>-<lb/> Three pints of the Juice of white currants<lb/> dissolved in two lb. of Lump sugar, add one Gallon<lb/> of Rum, put it thro' a Jelly Bag, and bottle<lb/> it. - the juice and sugar should be boild before<lb/> the Rum is put to it, and stir'd well when cold,<lb/> add white Raspberries if you like.<lb/> <pb/> Dying Green. D<hi rend="superscript">o-</hi><lb/> Green <unclear>Bain</unclear> cut in strips, boil'd in water, with<lb/> a piece of allum. - Dark brown with<lb/> walnut <unclear>heaves</unclear>, boild as above. - <lb/><pb/> Pink apples. - M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Minhoven</unclear><lb/> Peal and scoop your apples, let them simmer<lb/> on the fire in a little water, when done, serve them<lb/> up with the Hot juice, color'd Red with any preserve<lb/> you like dissolved, such as Currant Jelly. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/072|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
61<lb/> Pomade Divine. - M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> <unclear>Mirchown</unclear><lb/> Take one pound and half of Beef Suet, or Mar<lb/> =row, after all the Bones, and filaments are pick'd<lb/> out clean, put it in an earthen Vessel, with<lb/> spring Water, which must be changed twice<lb/> a Day, for then days, then drain it, let it<lb/> lay 24 hours in Rose Water, then drain<lb/> this away thro' a thin cloth, then add to it<lb/> one ounce of <unclear>orris</unclear> of <unclear>florence</unclear>, half an<lb/> ounce of Cinnamon, one oz of Cypress Root<lb/> one ox of Benjoin, one oz of storax, two Dram.<lb/> of Cloves. two Drams of Nutmegs, powder these<lb/> all very fine, and mix them with the Marrow<lb/> or suet, then put it into a pewter vessel and<lb/> when it is shut as close as possible, cover<lb/> the Top with a Cloth many times double, <lb/> and make a paste of flour, and the white<lb/> of an egg, and put round it, another cloth over<lb/> it, that nothing may evaporate, then put<lb/> an <unclear>stron</unclear> skewer thro' the Handles and <unclear>sni</unclear>-<lb/> =per.d this in a boiler of water which must<lb/> not cease boiling for three hours, have <lb/> boiling water ready to add as the other
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/073|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
62<lb/> waists the vessel in which the Pomade is must<lb/> touch nothing but the skewer, when it has boild<lb/> take it out, and pour it thro' a piece of fine<lb/> muslin, and drain it into <unclear>opally</unclear> pots, when cold<lb/> cover it up, which will not be in up than two<lb/> days. it is better for keeping. -<lb/> <pb/> Ginger Wine M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> <unclear>Minhow</unclear><lb/> To eight Gallons of water put 18 lb: of moist sugar<lb/> the peel of 12 Lemons, pared thin, let it boil half<lb/> an Hour, skimming it well, when cold add the<lb/> Juice of the 12 lemons strain'd, boil half a pound<lb/> of Ginger in three quarts of water two Hours when<lb/> cold mix all together, then put it into a Cask, <lb/> with four spoonfulls of new ye<add>a</add>st, half an ounce<lb/> of Isinglass, and four pounds of the best jar Rai<lb/> =sins, stop the Cask close, when it has <unclear>close</unclear><lb/> working, let it stand 7 weeks, then bottle it,<lb/> and put a wineglass of Brandy into each bottle,<lb/> soon after it will be fit to drink, the Isinglass<lb/> not to be put in, till it has done working -<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/074|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
63<lb/> Sealing Wax Varnish<lb/> Take a stick of red or black sealing wax put it into<lb/> four ounces of best rectified Spirits of Wine, put it in<lb/> over night, + set it into a warm Place, + you will<lb/> find it nearly dissolved the next morning. -<lb/> then stir it with a stick, + warm it well before you<lb/> use it. do it with a varnishing Brush. + take care<lb/> to wash the Brush well with Spirits of Wine to make<lb/> it clean, or it will spoil it. - <lb/> To make Grape Wine M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi>. Mills - Hope - <unclear>Mansd</unclear><lb/> the proportions are twelve pound of Fruit well bruised<lb/> to twelve pounds of sugar (Lump Sugar is best) if you<lb/> can grind the Fruit in a Cyder Mill it is the best<lb/> put the Grapes to twelve quarts of Water. let it<lb/> stand 48 Hours stirring it frequently. then strain<lb/> the liquor thro a Hair Sieve on to the Sugar, let it<lb/> stand 3 Days scumming the dross from the Top, as<lb/> it rises. - turn it + when the fermentation is over<lb/> add one quart of Brandy to then Gallons of<lb/> Wine, after it has stood 3 Month you may<lb/> rack it off into a smaller Vessel. + fitter the thick<lb/> for present use - keep the best twelve months, then bottle <add>it -</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/075|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
64<lb/> To make unripe Gooseberry wine - M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Mills. <add>Hope-Mansel</add><lb/> <add>to 9 Gallons<lb/>put 27 lb<lb/>Sugar.<lb/>which is<lb/>at 1.9 <unclear>pn</unclear><lb/>lb:<lb/>1.13.9<lb/>2 Bush<add>l</add>es<lb/> of Goose<lb/>berries<lb/>makes<lb/>this<lb/>quantity<lb/>which<lb/>cost <add>1</add> 8</add><lb/> To one Quart of Water put on pound of unripe Goos<lb/> -berries well bruised. let it stand 3 Days. stirring it twice<lb/> a Day. then strain it off as fine as possible, + to<lb/> every quart of liquor three quarters of a pound of<lb/> Loaf Sugar. let it stand 24 Hours, that the sugar<lb/> may dissolve. then Barrel it. in nine Days rack<lb/> it off + to every five Gallons put one Bottle of<lb/> Brandy.- hang a stick of Isinglass into the Bung<lb/> Hole to fine the wine. - it may be bottled in twelve<lb/> Months. - or this from the same Lady. - <lb/> <del><gap/></del><lb/> To 3 quarts of unripe Gooseberries, put one Gallon<lb/> of clear spring Water. let it stand 3 Days + then <lb/> strain it off, + to every Gallon of this Liquor put<lb/> three pounds of Loaf Sugar. put it into a vessel<lb/> + to every six Gallons put in one quart of Bran<lb/> -dy. let it stand 3 <hi rend="underline">months in the</hi> Cask, +<lb/> then put in one Ounce of Isinglass. then let<lb/> it stand 3 - months more + bottle it for use -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/076|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
65<lb/> To make Potato Cheesecakes <add>M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Rixon</unclear></add><lb/> Take some potatoes boil them, <unclear>will</unclear> best not <lb/> to break peel them and let them stand till<lb/> cold then take ounces and pound them fine in<lb/> marble mortar then add half a pound of clarified<lb/> butter the yolks of two eggs some lemon peel cut<lb/> fine and sweeten it to your taste with loaf<lb/> sugar put it in small pans and twenty<lb/> minutes will bake them<lb/> <pb/> To make Lemon Pickle M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Rixon<lb/> Wipe 6 Lemons cut each into eight pieces<lb/> put on them a pound of slat, 6 large cloves of<lb/> garlick 2 oz of horseradish sliced thin; liken___<lb/> of clover, mace, nutmeg, cayenne, a quarter of<lb/> an ounce of each, and 2 Oz of <unclear>loan</unclear> of mustard<lb/> to these put 2 quarts of vinegar, boil it a quarter<lb/> of an hour in a well tinned saucepan on which<lb/> is better do it in a string jar in a bottle of<lb/> boiling water, then set the jar by and stir it<lb/> Daily for six weeks, keeping the jar close covered<lb/> then put it in small bottles<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

