College Puddings - M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Barnett <add>Cobrey</add><lb/> Take a quarter of a lb. of grated Bread, half<lb/> a lb: of suet shred fine, half a lb: of Currants<lb/> well wash'd + picked, three Eggs well beaten, <lb/> a glass of white wine, Nutmeg + sugar to your <lb/> Taste; mix these well together; this quantity<lb/> will make 4 or 5 puddings boil them an Hour<lb/> serve them with wine sauce. - <lb/> <pb/> Potatoe Pudding - Cobrey<lb/> Boil one lb. of potatoes till quite soft beat<lb/> + rub them thro' a sieve mix in 1/4 lb of butter<lb/> melted, 4 <add>or 5</add> eggs 1/4 <hi rend="superscript">lb</hi> of lump sugar a glass of white<lb/> wine stir them all well together grate in<lb/> some nutmeg, add 40 drops of essence of<lb/> Lemon; lay putt paste all over the dish<lb/> pour in the pudding, + bake it a <gap/><lb/> brown. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/102|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
90<lb/> To make puff Cakes for Tea. - M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Elgar<lb/> half a lb of Butter, half a pint of Water, + 2 oz<lb/> of Lump Sugar powdered, mix these together + boil<lb/> them two or three Minutes, then stir in half a<lb/> lb: one Ounce of Flour, whilst on the Fire. when<lb/> very thick take it off, + when cool add half<lb/> a lb of Currants well clean'd + dried, + eight<lb/> Eggs well beaten; put them into tins well butterd<lb/> + bake them in a slow oven. - great care<lb/> must be taken to keep it from burning when<lb/> boild. - <lb/> To make Smyrna Raisin Wine - <lb/> To one hundred weight of Smyrna Raisins<lb/> put twenty Gallons of water that has been <lb/> boiled, let it stand fourteen Days. <add>Stirring it every Day</add> - then<lb/> strain it thro' a hair sieve. - Jar it, + put <lb/> in it, 2-oz of Isinglass steep'd a week in a<lb/> <gap/>ort of Brandy. add the other three quarters<lb/> <gap/> dy to make it up a Gallon. let it stand<lb/> <gap/> then bottle it. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/103|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
91<lb/> To make Elder Raisin Wine<lb/> To every Gallon of Water put six pounds of<lb/> Malaga Raisins shred fine, put them into a<lb/> Tub, + pour the Water boiling hot upon them<lb/> let it stand fourteen Days stirring it every Day<lb/> twice. - gather some Elder Berries full ripe<lb/> strip them from the Stalks, put them into an<lb/> Oven in an earthen pot, let the Oven by very<lb/> moderately hot, + let them stand all night<lb/> let the Juice drain thro' a coarse hair Sieve<lb/> + to every gallon of liquor add one quarter of Elder<lb/> Juice. stir it well together. work it with<lb/> a toast spread with three spoonfuls of good<lb/> thick 'new yeast', the liquor being just warm<lb/> let it work, a day or two before you turn it; <lb/> + work a few Days in the Vessel filling it<lb/> up as you do Beer - to ten Gallons add<lb/> one quart of Brandy. close your Vessel<lb/> + let it stand a year before bottled. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/104|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
92<lb/> To make Raspberry Wine<lb/> gather the Raspberries when full ripe + quite<lb/> dry, crush them directly, + mix them with<lb/> sugar; it will preserve the flavor which<lb/> they would lose in two Hour; to every quart<lb/> of Raspberries put a pound of fine Lisbon<lb/> Sugar. - when you have got the quantity<lb/> you intend to make, to every quart of<lb/> Raspberries add two pounds more Sugar,<lb/> + one Gallon of water that has been boil'd <lb/> + stood till it is cold. - stir it well up<lb/> together + let it stand three days to ferment<lb/> stirring five or six times in a Day. + taking<lb/> off the dross as it rises. put it into the<lb/> Cask, + to every Gallon put in two whole<lb/> Eggs taking care not to break the shells<lb/> in putting them in. close the Vessel<lb/> down well let it stand 3 months<lb/> + then bottle it, for us. put in a quart of<lb/> Brandy to ten Gallon.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/105|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
98<lb/> To make red Currant Wine - or Mixed Currants<lb/> Gather the Currants when full ripe, take an equal<lb/> quantity of red + white, bake them an Hour in<lb/> a moderate oven, <sic>squeese</sic> them thro' a coarse<lb/> strainer to get all the Juice. - have boiling<lb/> water ready, + to every Gallon of Water, put<lb/> one quart of Currant Juice, + three pounds<lb/> of good moist Sugar; boil it well a quarter<lb/> of an Hour, scum it + put it into a Tub, +<lb/> when cool set it to work with a Toast<lb/> spread on both sides with a little <unclear>piceken</unclear><lb/> yeast. let it work three Days before you<lb/> Jar it. - then add to every ten Gallons<lb/> two quarts of Brandy, + the whites of<lb/> ten Eggs well beaten. - close the Cask well<lb/> let it stand three months then bottle it.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/106|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
94<lb/> To make strong Mead<lb/> To every Gallon of water put five pounds<lb/> of Honey, the whites two Eggs to every Gallon<lb/> well whisked to a froth, boil three quarters of<lb/> an Hour skimming it all the time; to every<lb/> Gallon add one 1/4 of an Oz of Hops. let them<lb/> boil a 1/4 of an Hour; pour this Hot upon the<lb/> rinds of 2 Lemons to a Gallon. + let the Juice<lb/> be put in after it has done working. - <lb/> which must be done with a Toast spread<lb/> with nice new yeast, + worked as you do<lb/> Beer. - to ten Gallons add one pint of<lb/> Brandy, + the Lemon Syrrup. - close the<lb/> Vessel down well, as soon as the fermentation<lb/> is over. - you may bottle it in six Months<lb/> but it be good to keep it in the Vessel<lb/> a year. put a little brandy into each<lb/> Bottle to prevent its fermenting + bursting<lb/> the Bottles.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/107|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
95<lb/> To make Sherbet.<lb/> Take nine Seville Oranges, + three Lemons<lb/> grate off all the yellow off the Rinds. put the<lb/> rapsings into a Gallon of water, + three pounds<lb/> of Loaf Sugar. boil it to a candy height, then<lb/> put in the Juice of the Lemons, + Oranges<lb/> Just heat them over the fire; then keep<lb/> them stirring till almost cold. then put<lb/> it into Jars for use. - <lb/> <pb/> Elder Flower Wine<lb/> Take the flowers of Elder, be careful not to put in<lb/> any stalks. boil 3 lbs of Loaf Sugar to one Gallon<lb/> of water, + one quart of Elder flowers. this is<lb/> the proportion. - pour the liquor boiling hot upon<lb/> the flowers having well skimmed it. set it<lb/> to work three Days with a Toast spread with<lb/> Barm. then strain it through a Hair sieve <unclear>tun</unclear><lb/> it. to every ten Gallons add one ox of Isinglass<lb/> dissolved in Brandy one quart. put in six whole<lb/> Eggs close the Vessel. let it stand six month
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/108|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
96<lb/> To make Mushroom Powder.<lb/> Take the thickest large buttons you can get<lb/> peel them, cut off the root end, but do not<lb/> wash them, spread the separately on pewter<lb/> dishes, or Tins, set them in a slow oven to<lb/> to dry. till you find they will powder,<lb/> then beat them in marble mortar, <lb/> + sift them thro' a fine sieve. put to it a<lb/> little Chyan pepper, + a little beaten mace<lb/> bottle it + keep it in a dry Closet. -<lb/> <pb/> Raisin Wine.<lb/> Boil ten Gallons of water one Hour, when<lb/> it is nearly cool pour it to sixty lb: of Malay<lb/> Raisins, clean picked, + chopped. stir it well<lb/> twice a Day for ten Days. then strain it thro'<lb/> a Hair Sieve, squeezing the Raisins well, <lb/> put the Liquor into your Vessel. bung it<lb/> close, + let it stand three months.<lb/> a quart of Brandy should be put in before<lb/> you bung it down.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/109|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
97<lb/> To make Lemon Wine that will drink<lb/> like Citron Water - <lb/> pare five Dozen Lemons very thin, put the peels<lb/> into one Gallon of French Brandy, let it stand<lb/> 14 Days make the Juice of the Lemons into<lb/> a Syrrup with three lb: of fine sugar. boil<lb/> fifteen Gallons of water, + forty lb: of sugar<lb/> half an Hour, skim it well; put it in a large<lb/> Tub + when cool two spoonfuls of new yeast<lb/> work it two Days; then turn it + put in the<lb/> Cask the Brandy, the peels, + the Surup;<lb/> stir it all well together, + in a few days<lb/> close up the Vessel. - let it stand three<lb/> months. then bottle it, it will be a<lb/> beautiful pale colour, + will drink<lb/> like a Cordial. - Orange Wine is good<lb/> made in the same way. - only you may<lb/> put in half an Oz of Cochineal to give it a color<lb/> boil'd in a pint of Water. if Orange Wine. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/110|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
98<lb/> To make Ginger Wine<lb/> To four Gallons of Spring Water put 8 lb of<lb/> moist Sugar boil it a quarter of an Hour<lb/> skimming it well when the liquor is cold<lb/> squeeze in the Juice of four Lemons, boil<lb/> the peels thinly pared in three pints of<lb/> Water, + two ounces of Ginger one Hour, <lb/> when cool put it all together into your<lb/> Vessel with two spoonfuls of good yeast, a<lb/> quarter of an Ounce of Isinglass beaten well<lb/> + two lb: of good Raisins.- close it well<lb/> when it has done working, + let it stand<lb/> two or three Months, then bottle it.-<lb/> the spring is the best time to make it.-<lb/> the more Lemons you put in the plea-<lb/> -santer the wine is. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/111|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
99<lb/> To make ripe Gooseberry Wine<lb/> Take twenty four quarts of ripe pearl Goose<lb/> =berries, bruise them well. then have ready<lb/> six Gallons of Water + eighteen lb: of Sugar<lb/> boil'd + skimmed; when the liquor is cool<lb/> put it to the gooseberries stir it well, + let it<lb/> stand 48 Hours at least stirring it twice a <lb/> Day. - Steep an Oz of Isinglass in a pint<lb/> of Brandy two Day, strain the wine thro'<lb/> a flannel Bag, put it into the Cask; then<lb/> beat the Isinglass in a marble mortar, <lb/> with the whites of five Eggs, for half an<lb/> Hour mix this well in the wine put in<lb/> the Brandy, + when settled a little close<lb/> the Vessel down with Clay. - let it stand<lb/> six months then bottle it. put a lump<lb/> of fine sugar into each Bottle. + 2 good<lb/> Raisins. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/112|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
100<lb/> To pickle Cucumbers<lb/> gather the cucumbers on a dry Day, put them<lb/> in a narrow mouthed stone Jar put to them<lb/> a head of Garlic, some white mustard sliced,<lb/> a few blades of mace, one oz of black<lb/> pepper, one ditto of Ginger, a handful of Salt<lb/> put into the Vinegar; pour it all upon the <lb/> Cucumbers boiling hot. set them by the fire<lb/> + keep them warm for three Days, boiling<lb/> the Vinegar every Day till they are green<lb/> then tye them down with a bladder, + <lb/> keep them in a dry place. you may do<lb/> french Beans the same way. <hi rend="underline">do</hi> Girkins <lb/> the same - <lb/> <pb/> to pickle them in slices, gather them before<lb/> they are much seeded. slice them near an inch<lb/> thick, lay a few shalots between the slices,<lb/> sprinkle some salt upon them let them lay<lb/> 6 Hours drain them on a hair Sieve, then<lb/> pour the pickle on them as on the other Cu-<lb/> -cumbers boiling hot, repeat it till they are tender.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/113|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
101<lb/> To pickle Cauliflowers<lb/> Take the closest + whitest Cauliflowers, pull them<lb/> into bunches, spread them on an earthen Dish<lb/> sprinkle salt over them to draw out the water.<lb/> let them stand three Days. wipe + drain them<lb/> quite dry. pour boiling salt + water upon them<lb/> + let them lay in it all night. then drain<lb/> them on a hair sieve, + put them in small Jars<lb/> or wide mouth'd Bottles. make a pickle of the<lb/> palest + strongest Vinegar you can get, or of<lb/> of distill'd Vinegar with a plenty of Ginger<lb/> white pepper, + Mace. + put the pickle on<lb/> the Cauliflowers. - white Cabbage is good<lb/> done the same way.-<lb/> <pb/> To pickle Barberries<lb/> gather the Barberries before they are quite<lb/> ripe. pick out all the Leaves + dried stalks<lb/> put them into Jars with strong salt + water, a<lb/> little spiced. - if you see any scum come on<lb/> them you must make fresh brine, they are so<lb/> sour they don't require Vinegar. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/114|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
102<lb/> To dress scotch Collops White<lb/> Cut them of a Leg of Veal the size of a Crown piece<lb/> season them a little, put them into your tossing<lb/> pan, + set it over a slow fire or it will discolour<lb/> the Collops, before the pan gets hot, or it will<lb/> discolour the Collops- keep turning them till<lb/> the Butter is a thick white gravy. put this gravy<lb/> + the collops into a Pot, + set them on the Hearth<lb/> to keep warm, whilst you fry more, + so on till<lb/> you have done all you want; then pour<lb/> the Gravy from them in the pan, + put in<lb/> some Lemon Pickle, mushroom Catchup, Caper<lb/> Liquor, mace, pepper, + Cayenne Pepper to y<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">r</hi><lb/> taste, + a little Salt. boil it five minutes, <lb/> then put the yolks of two Eggs well beaten, to<lb/> a tea cup full of rich cream. keep this stirring<lb/> it over the Fire till the Gravy is of a nice<lb/> thickness, then put in the Collops shake them<lb/> about in the Gravy. serve them with <unclear>foce</unclear> meat<lb/> Balls, mushrooms, + garnish with Barberries<lb/> + pickles.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/115|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
103<lb/> To make hot Crust for bakes Custards<lb/> put a 1/4 of the weight of Butter that you have<lb/> of flour, into a saucepan with as much water as<lb/> will make the Crust. - when it boils pour it in<lb/> the flour. work the past well, + when a little cool<lb/> raise your Custards - put a piece of writing paper<lb/> in the inside bake them half enough, take out<lb/> the paper + put in the Custard meat which<lb/> you must have ready made. to make the<lb/> Custard meat take a quart of new milk, + add<lb/> a half pint of Cream. beat the yolks of<lb/> 12 Eggs + the whites of four well strain them<lb/> to the milk sweeten to your taste + grate in<lb/> plenty of Nutmeg. - when the Crusts are<lb/> half baked put in this liquid + let it<lb/> stand in a slow oven till of a proper<lb/> thickness. - if you make the Crust of Suet.<lb/> it must be stirred fine, + boil'd in water +<lb/> strained into the flour. - dripping ought <lb/> always to be boil'd in water before you<lb/> use it. as it takes out the strength. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/116|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
104<lb/> To stew Mushrooms<lb/> Take large or small buttons, or if you cannot<lb/> get them scrape, peel, + clean large Mush<lb/> -rooms, + cut them in pieces, + clean the<lb/> buttons with a bit of flannel. - put them<lb/> in a stew pan with a little water, let<lb/> them stew a 1/4 of an Hour, then put in<lb/> a little pepper + salt, a piece of butter<lb/> braided in Flour, boil it till as thick<lb/> as cream, a few minutes will do; then<lb/> add a little cream + the yolks of<lb/> two Eggs, shake these together one<lb/> minute over the Fire, taking care to<lb/> stir it all the time or it will curd to<lb/> dish it up with small <unclear>sippets</unclear> not <unclear>trasted</unclear><lb/> it is a nice corner dish. - to do them<lb/> brown fry the mushrooms first, + put <lb/> good Gravy instead of Cream, a little Lemon<lb/> Pickle- + braided butter + flour to thicken it
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/117|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
105<lb/> To stew Cucumbers<lb/> peel the outside, + slice the cucumbers pretty<lb/> thick, fry them in butter lay them a draining<lb/> put them in a stew pan, with a little strong<lb/> Gravy, season them to your taste thicken<lb/> the Gravy with butter braided in flour, let<lb/> the Cucumbers just boil five minutes stirring<lb/> all the time, be careful not to break the<lb/> slices, + serve it to table. without any<lb/> sippits. - <lb/> To make Beef Tea<lb/> Take some lean Beef cut it into small<lb/> pieces, put it into a Jar, + to every<lb/> pound of Beef, put one quart of boiling<lb/> water. cover it up close + set it by<lb/> the fire till the goodness is out of the <lb/> Beef. set it by to cool skim off all the<lb/> fat if any rises. this <add>is</add> good for weak<lb/> + sickly people.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/118|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
106<lb/> Cream of Tartar Whey -<lb/> set a pint of skimmed milk over the fire<lb/> when it begins to boil, put in two teaspoonfuls<lb/> of cream of tartar, give it a gentle boil. take<lb/> it off the fire. let it stand till the Curd<lb/> settled to the bottom. then pour of the whey<lb/> + drink it milk warm sweeten it to your<lb/> taste.- <lb/> <pb/> To keep green Peas-<lb/> Gather green Peas on a dry warm Day in the<lb/> afternoon. shell them + put them into dry clean<lb/> Bottles- cork them close, + tye them over with<lb/> a Bladder; keep them in a cool dry place<lb/> boil them rather longer than in the Summer<lb/> <pb/> To keep French Beans<lb/> gather the Beans quite dry, + a midding<lb/> size, but not too old. put a layer of salt, +<lb/> a layer of Beans till the stone Jar is full<lb/> let there be plenty of salt on the Top - tye<lb/> the Jar down with a bladder, + leather. keep them in<lb/> a dry cool place. lay them in warm water, before cooked
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/119|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
1<gap/><lb/> To keep Grapes<lb/> Cut the Bunches with a Joint of the Vine upon<lb/> them. hang them upon a string in a dry Room<lb/> do not let the Bunches touch each other, - lest<lb/> they grow mouldy + rot. - they will keep thus<lb/> for months.-<lb/> <pb/> To pickle small Onions<lb/> peel small Onions put them into salt +<lb/> water for 3 Days changing the water every<lb/> Day - let them drain + if still strong. put<lb/> some salt + Water boiling hot over them<lb/> drain them well, + have a pickle made<lb/> with good Vinegar, + spices. pour it hot<lb/> over them, + repeat this if necessary. -<lb/> if you wish to have them very white<lb/> use distill'd Vinegar, + blanch them<lb/> with more salt + water.- + use white <lb/> Pepper instead of black pepper. + put<lb/> in Ginger sliced, + mace -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/120|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<gap/>8<lb/> Indian Pickle or Picalillo-<lb/> one nice white Cabbage, one Colliflower, a few<lb/> small Cucumbers, Radish Pods, Kidney Beans<lb/> a little Beet Root or any other thing you like<lb/> to pickle lay them on a hair Sieve, sprinkle<lb/> them with Salt, set them before the fire or in<lb/> the Sun is best to drain + dry when all<lb/> the water is drain'd out + they are dry<lb/> put them into a large pickle Jar, a layer<lb/> of each thing, + a handful of Mustard seed<lb/> then take as much good Vinegar as you<lb/> think will cover them, + to every quart<lb/> of Vinegar put a 1/4 of an Oz of turmeric<lb/> 1/4 of an Oz of Mace, 1/4 of an Oz of Ginger<lb/> a few Cloves, + an oz of white Pepper-<lb/> boil the turmeric first in the Vinegar +<lb/> then add the spice pour them all boiling<lb/> hot over the things, let them stand near the<lb/> Fire till they are become of a bright yellow,<lb/> put in some Garlic + Shalots if you<lb/> please. much depends on their being well<lb/> spiced. you may keep adding more things as you<lb/> eat out the pickles
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/121|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
109<lb/> To pickle young Artchokes<lb/> gather the artichokes soon after they are form'd<lb/> scaled them three minutes in strong Salt + water<lb/> lay them on a hair Sieve to drain when cold<lb/> put them into small mouth'd Jars, make as<lb/> much pickle of best white wine Vinegar as<lb/> you think will cover them, with spices as<lb/> for other pickles. pour it hot over them.<lb/> + when cold tye them down close with a Blad-<lb/> -der, + a paper or Leather. - <lb/> <pb/> To pickle red Cabbage<lb/> Take a close hard red Cabbage, cut it in<lb/> slices. lay it in an earthen Dish sprinkle<lb/> salt over it, let it lay three Days to drain<lb/> out the water. then lay it on a Sieve to<lb/> drain - make a pickle with good white<lb/> wine vinegar well spiced. pour it on<lb/> the Cabbage (after you have laid it quite<lb/> close in your pickle Jar) let it be quite<lb/> scalding hot. - let it stand covered till cold, tye<lb/> it down with a Bladder. it will be good in a week
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/122|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
110<lb/> To make Gooseberry Vinegar - <lb/> Take very ripe Gooseberries, + to every pick<lb/> of Gooseberries put two Gallons of water<lb/> having first crushed all the Gooseberries<lb/> well. - let this stand for a week, stirring<lb/> it every Day three times, strain the<lb/> liquor thro a hair sieve, + put to every<lb/> Gallon of this Liquor, three pound of<lb/> coarse moist sugar - turn it into an<lb/> Iron hooped Vessel. - set it in the<lb/> Sun abroad, with a piece of Glass<lb/> over the Bung hold, when the weather<lb/> gets cool take it in, + set it in a warm<lb/> place where the frost cannot get it, <lb/> keep it a year then bottle it for use.<lb/> this is better for pickles than any<lb/> you can buy. - for sugar vinegar put<lb/> the same quantity of sugar to the <lb/> water. but let it boil a 1/4 of an hour<lb/> + <hi rend="underline">work</hi> it four Days in a Tub before<lb/> you can it. in an Iron hoop'd Vessel.<lb/> + proceed as for the other Vinegar
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/123|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
111<lb/> a pice seed or plumb cake<lb/> to make them as Coquels, add only milk enough to<lb/> to make them into Dough - <lb/> Take one pound + half of flour, rub in 6 oz<lb/> of butter. heat three quarters of a pint of <lb/> new milk scalding hot. pour half of it<lb/> into the flour + butter, + stir it up together<lb/> the remainder of the milk. to be put to it<lb/> afterwards<lb/> three large spoonfuls of good new yeast<lb/> when the milk is cool. - mix this into <add>the</add> cake<lb/> + add 6 oz of powdered lump sugar, +<lb/> a little nutmeg + allspice. - add seed<lb/> or Currants well wash'd + dried as<lb/> you please. - bake it about an Hour<lb/> + quarter in a Rice Oven. - <lb/> A Rice Cake<lb/> a quarter of a pound of Rice 1/4 lb of flour six ounces<lb/> of lump sugar powder'd half a pound of butter melted four<lb/> eggs <gap/> rub two wholes heat them all well together<lb/> let the butter be nearly cold before you mix the eggs<lb/> with it. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/124|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
112<lb/> Vinegar<lb/> 1 Gallon of water 4 lb of <unclear>very common</unclear> sugar<lb/> boil them together with the whites of 8 eggs<lb/> well beaten then strain it thro' a sieve<lb/> when cool <unclear>work</unclear> it well yeast spread upon<lb/> a toast. - <lb/> <pb/> To make a good blacking for Shoes<lb/> Six ounces of Ivory Black - one quart<lb/> of Vinegar - two ounces of sugar Candy, <lb/> one pennyworth of Spirits of Vitriol - <lb/> two pennyworth of nutgalls, + one<lb/> Table spoonful of sweet rib. - <lb/> from Stringer of Ruinham -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/125|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">To make a cheap kind of Paint - .</hi><lb/> 20 - Ounces of Cheese Curd,<lb/> 20 Ounces of Paris Whiting,<lb/> 1-Oz of Lime unslaked, <lb/> 8- Grains of blue Black, this makes an excellent<lb/> white paint, the manner of mixing will be mentioned<lb/> Pink Paint, or Peach blossom-<lb/> 20-oz of Cheese Curd<lb/> 20-Oz of Paris Whiting<lb/> 1-Oz of unslaked Lime<lb/> 2-Oz of Rose Pink.<lb/> The Curd must be well beaten in a mortar, the Lime<lb/> fresh from the Kiln, + slaked as you want to use it,<lb/> + ground upon a slab with a mullar; then mix'd with<lb/> the curd well, + stirred together for half an Hour,<lb/> mix the colours + whiting with a little water, + <lb/> grind them well together with the curd + Lime;<lb/> sufficient water to make it a consistence for painting.<lb/> strain it thro' a Hair Sieve + the paint is fit for <lb/> use. - N: B:- the whey must not be entirely taken<lb/> from the Curd, lest it should be too dry. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/126|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">To make Raspberry Jam-</hi><lb/> Gather the Fruit when quite dry + ripe, pick out<lb/> all that are bad; and to every pound of Rasp=<lb/> =berries put three quarters of a pound of Sugar<lb/> nicely pounded; mix all well together, it is<lb/> best to mix the sugar + Raspberries, + let them<lb/> stand all night to dissolve the Sugar well-<lb/> then set them over a clear slow Fire stir them<lb/> well lest they should turn, + after they begin<lb/> to boil they must boil half an Hour. - Just<lb/> before you take them off put in a full Wine<lb/> Glass of Brandy to about six pounds of<lb/> Raspberries, let it simmer a few minutes after<lb/> + then pour it into Jars. - the next Day, put<lb/> papers dipp'd in Brandy over the Tops, + tye<lb/> them down with double Papers.- take care<lb/> to clean the rims of the Jars before you tye<lb/> them down, or else the little drops of Jam will<lb/> be apt to decay the other parts-<lb/> be careful to skim off all the froth, + dress as<lb/> the skim rises. - the better the sugar the Less skim<lb/> + waste there will be.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/127|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">To make Currant Jelly.</hi><lb/> Gather the Fruit when quite ripe, prick<lb/> the currants from off the stalks; put in<lb/> one fourth part of white currants to three<lb/> fourths of Red Currants. - set them over a very<lb/> slow Fire in a sten Pan, cover them close. let<lb/> them boil gently till the skins burst + the<lb/> Juice can run out. - pour them into a sieve<lb/> + drain away all the Juice you can. but you<lb/> must not <unclear>igpeese</unclear> them, only gently press<lb/> them with a spoon.- then measure the Juice<lb/> + to every pint of Juice put one pound +<lb/> quarter of sugar well pounded - set it over<lb/> a clear fire to boil gently, carefully skim=<lb/> -ming it, as the skim rises. let it boil full<lb/> half an Hour after it boils, or the Jelly will<lb/> not keep good. - pour it into Jars + when<lb/> quite cold put papers dipped in Brandy over<lb/> the Jelly- wipe the edges of the Jars quite<lb/> dry + tye them down with double paper. -<lb/> + keep them in a dry place. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/128|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To bottle Gooseberries Green<lb/> Gather the Fruit when quite dry, cut off the<lb/> tops + tails without breaking the Skin of any<lb/> one, rub them on a clean coarse Cloth quite dry<lb/> to get off all dirt; put them into wide Mouth'd<lb/> Bottles, + cork them down tight; when the Bread<lb/> is drawn set them into the Oven, + let them<lb/> stand all night. the next day cut the Corks<lb/> quite close + melt Rosin upon <add>the</add> Tops so as to<lb/> close them from all air. - keep them in a <lb/> dry place. - <lb/> <pb/> Purified Soap for Chapp'd Hands<lb/> Take a quarter of an ounce of Camphor, one Ounce<lb/> of Spermaceta, two Table spoonfuls of Oil of Almonds<lb/> melt these slowly in an earthen pipkin on a Hob<lb/> by the fire. when dissolved put in half a pound of<lb/> best soft Soap, stir it till it is well mixed<lb/> together - as it cools add to spoonfuls of Hangary<lb/> or Lavender Water. and a little essence of Bergam<lb/> put it into small Jars for use. a little piece<lb/> Honey is a good thing to add for chapped Hands<lb/> small pieces of soap cut fine will do where<lb/> soft soap is not to be got.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/129|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
113<lb/> <gap/><hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">ed</hi> Pleasance' receipt to make Currant Wine<lb/> <gap/> every Bushel of Currants well squeezed put<lb/> <gap/> quarts of Water let them stand 12 Hours<lb/> then strain them thro' a hair Sieve, squeeze<lb/> <gap/>Stalks quite dry - to every quart of this<lb/> Liquor add one pound <add>+ 1/4</add> of Lisbon Sugar<lb/> dissolve it well + turn it. - let it stand<lb/> <gap/> or 12 months before you bottle it. - be<lb/> careful not to bung it down till it has<lb/> done hissing. - <lb/> <pb/> Shon Miss Hill<lb/> Take 20 large apples peel'd, cored, + boild to a <lb/> Pulp, 1/2 a pint of good Cream - 3/4 lb of sugar<lb/> finely powdered. 1/4 lb Sweet almonds blanch +<lb/> shred fine, 1/4 lb of candied peel sliced in. mix<lb/> these well together put it in a Dish the Day<lb/> before it is wanted the next morning beat the<lb/> <del><gap/></del> Whites of six Eggs to a high froth + spread<lb/> over the Dish, sift some fine Sugar over it, + <lb/> serve it to Table it will look like fine snow<lb/> a Lemon squeezed in when you beat the apple<lb/> will be an improvent -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/130|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To make a Green Colour for colouring Ro<gap/><lb/> Put 6 Oz of blue bitirol and 1 1/2 lb. of Spanish White<lb/> into an earthen Pipkin with 2 Gallons of Water<lb/> let it boil one hour, stir it all the time -<lb/> then tke it off and put it in a clean Pan<lb/> the next day, then pour the Water from it a<lb/> dry as you can, as fast as it settles; and <add>then</add> make a<lb/> thin Glue size, to mix with it - The size will<lb/> made with half a lb of Glue + 6 pints of Water<lb/> let it boil <add>till</add> it is dissolved, and when cold mix it<lb/> wel with the Colour- The colour must be pre<gap/><lb/> upon the wall or Ppaer the same as paint<lb/> and take care to put it on regularly up and<lb/> down with a Painters Brush. it should be we<gap/><lb/> <pb/> This makes a deep Sea Green - If you wish it a<lb/> paler colour you must add more Spanish white<lb/> If you wish for a <hi rend="underline">Pea</hi> Green you must add some<lb/> Dutch Pink before it is boiled. . -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

