To pickle <hi rend="underline">300 Walnuts</hi>. <lb/> 1 1/2 oz Mace.<lb/> 6 " White Ginger - bruised<lb/> 1 1/2 " Cloves -<lb/> 3 " Allspice - <lb/> 6 " Black pepper. <lb/> 6 " Long pepper. <lb/> 3 " Chillies - <lb/> 4 " Bay Salt - <lb/> 6 " Mustard Seed - <lb/> <pb/> These to be boiled for half an hour<lb/> in four pints of the best Vinegar, <lb/> then put into the jars + simmer<lb/> carefully closed till cold.<lb/> then add 2 large onions 8. shalots<lb/> 6 cloves of Garlic, three good sticks<lb/> of horseradish slices + about 26 <lb/> Bay leaves -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/142|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
The Walnuts sh<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">d</hi> be gathered dry.<lb/> and kept in strong salt + water<lb/> for ten days the brine being<lb/> changed in the fifth day. then<lb/> expose them to the full sun<lb/> + air for one day or till black, <lb/> when they may be put to the spiced<lb/> Vinegar piercing every one with<lb/> a <unclear>broad. awl.</unclear> . About 6 p<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">ts</hi> more<lb/> of the hot Vinegar must then<lb/> be added. Let them stand one<lb/> month in the Jar carefully<lb/> excluding the air + if required<lb/> add more Vinegar -<lb/> <pb/> It is better to add the cloves of<lb/> Garlic first from the plant in<lb/> the month of August -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/143|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To <hi rend="underline">make Milk Punch</hi><lb/> Take 10 oranges + 10 lemons<lb/> large + fine - Pare them<lb/> as thin as possible. Steep<lb/> the peels in one quart of<lb/> Rum. let them lie a<lb/> night + day. then take a<lb/> gallon of spring water - <lb/> put in two pounds of fine<lb/> sugar. squeeze your fruit,<lb/> which must make a full<lb/> quart of juice - put it into<lb/> the water with four quarts<lb/> of Rum, including that in<lb/> which the Peels were steeped<lb/> being well drained from the<lb/> Peels - stir all these together.<lb/> then add one quart of milk
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/144|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
boiling hot. let it stand<lb/> all night. The next day stir<lb/> + pour it through a jelly bag.<lb/> If it should not be clear the<lb/> first time, pour it through<lb/> till it be, being careful<lb/> not to disturb the coat of<lb/> bag occasioned by the curd.<lb/> The thicker that <del>it</del> is, the<lb/> finer it will run. you<lb/> may put an equal <lb/> quantity of Rum + Brandy.<lb/> Bottle it, + cork it well,<lb/> and it will keep several<lb/> years. <lb/> Yarmouth June 24<hi rend="superscript">d</hi><add>1868</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/006/001/145|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
