1<lb/> <head>Apple pudding - (by M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> M<hi rend="superscript">c</hi>Doodle).</head><lb/> <pb/> 1/ Pare 12, stew them on a slow fire, add the peel of<lb/> 6 Lemons, sweeten to your liking, put in an egg, beat into<lb/> a cream, seasoned with Raspberries or any thing your Garden<lb/> affords, add to these some flour or if you have none, a <lb/> dust of any thing else to thicken it. join in two or three<lb/> Spoonfuls of Currants and a lard of Suet, add in a<lb/> Crumb of Bread and a scrap of Nutmeg. - <lb/> The above is an excellent dish and may be depended on<lb/> <pb/> <head>Apple Puffs</head><lb/> 2/ Pare and core your apples. boil them in a little water<lb/> until they are dry. always stirring them. squeeze in some<lb/> Lemon juice when they are again dry. put in a little Rose<lb/> or Orange flavor water to flavor it. put this into a rich <unclear>prest</unclear><lb/> peast, the puff not to be larger than a crown piece. -<lb/> <pb/> <head>An Amlet.</head><lb/> 3/ The yolks of six Eggs, and three whites, beat very well, add a good<lb/> deal of shred parsley, some olives or shallot cut fine. a naggin of cream, <lb/> little pepper salt and nutmeg. fry it without tossing but not so as<lb/> to be tore or too brown though one half our the pot and lay<lb/> the other over it again. so serve it up.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/030|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
2<lb/> <head>Apple Jelly for Sweet Meats</head><lb/> <pb/> 4/ Pare and Core your Apples, and put them into as much<lb/> boiling water as will cover them, let them boil very fast, when<lb/> boiled to mash, add another quart of water, boil this for half an<lb/> hour, then pour the Liquor through a bag, and to every quart <lb/> of this allow two pounds of Sugar, in Summer Codlings are<lb/> best, in Winter Pippins.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Almond Custards,</head><lb/> 5/. Pound as many Almonds as you think sufficient, a few<lb/> bitter among them. add a little orange flower water to flavor<lb/> and also prevent the almond going to oil, boil a quart of<lb/> good cream, beat the yolks of eight eggs to a cream. Sweeten<lb/> to your taste, mix the almonds and Eggs together, add the<lb/> Boiling Mick by a Cup at a time for fear of Curdling the<lb/> Eggs, when all is mixed and seasoned to your liking put<lb/> it on a slow fire and keep stirring one way for a few minutes<lb/> until it begins to thicken. take great care least it should get<lb/> too much heat, as it would instantly curdle, do not fill your<lb/> cups until pretty cool, else it would settle.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/031|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
3<lb/> <head>Apple Froth. Supper Dish.</head><lb/> <pb/> 6/ Half a pound of pulp of wasted apples, Sweeten to your taste.<lb/> with pounded Sugar, beat well the whites of three Eggs, mix<lb/> all together, and beat them up light. dish this and speck it once<lb/> with Currant jelly or throw coloured comfits over it, if you should<lb/> dish it too soon before you use it. the pock will fall flat.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Almond Cheese Cakes with Butter.</head><lb/> 7/ Beat ten yolks and two whites of Eggs very well. have a<lb/> pound of Sugar ready pounded and sifted, which you are<lb/> to put in by a Spoonful at a time; until all be mixed,<lb/> pound half a pound of sweet almonds, a few bitter add<lb/> as you pound, some Orange flour water half a quarter of a pound<lb/> of butter fresh it must be, and deserve this must be the last thing<lb/> to go in, mix all very well together and fill your Pattys which<lb/> are to have a rich peast at the bottom. the pattys when<lb/> filled must not stand out of the oven. else the Cheese would<lb/> settle. half an hour bakes them. this quantity makes Eighteen.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/032|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
4<lb/> <head>Mrs Jervases Receipt for Almond Cheese Cakes</head> <lb/> <head>very Good.</head> <lb/> <pb/> 8/ Half a pound of Sweet Almonds a few bitter among them. pound.<lb/> but not fine Blanch). have half a pound of pounded Sugar<lb/> sifted, the whites of three Eggs beat to a solid and mix<lb/> this with the Almond have a rich paste in your Pattys.<lb/> fill them soak them well when done. they will burst at<lb/> the top, this is esteemed better than those, that have butter<lb/> in them.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Almond Pudding.</head><lb/> 9/ Beat 12 yolks of Eggs to a cream. add to it one quart of<lb/> Cream a quarter of pound of pounded sugar, the like of<lb/> Sweet Almonds + bitter. pounded not too fine, half a pound<lb/> of grated Naple bisquit, half a pound of fresh butter melted<lb/> season with little brown nutmeg, a rich paste at bottom of<lb/> the dish half an hour bakes it.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Almond Flumery</head><lb/> 10/ Put down two Calves feet with two quarts of Soft water,<lb/> boil it down to one, with a blade of Mace, or a bit of<lb/> Cinamon, it must simmer over a slow fire, until reduced<lb/> to one half, have ready a quarter of a pound of Sweet Almonds<lb/> a few bitter fine pounded, add to the above. give it one <add>scald</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/033|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
5<lb/> scald and strain it through a cloth, put it into Cups, add<lb/> half a pint of rich Cream. Sugar to your taste. when cold<lb/> pour it out speck it with Almonds cut fine. <hi rend="underline">A genteel</hi> Dish<lb/> <hi rend="underline">for Supper.</hi> <lb/> <pb/> Spanish Flumery.<lb/> 11/ An ounce of Isinglass, dissolve it in half pint of baking<lb/> water, when it cools. put in half pint of white wine. the yolks<lb/> of four Eggs well beat together, the rind of a Lemon, also the<lb/> juice. boil the rind at first with the Isinglass to get all the<lb/> flavor.- add all these together. sweeten to your taste, give<lb/> all a boil together run it through a fine towel into a bason,<lb/> stir it. and when cool fill your Cups or shape pist diped in water - Supper Dish.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Apricots</head><lb/> 12/ Weigh your apricots and allow an equal weight of Sugar,<lb/> pounded and sifted. pan the Ap<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. take out the Stone. crack it<lb/> take out the kernel carefully which you are to put into<lb/> the apricot again and close it, as you pare the fruit cover<lb/> them with Sugar to preserve the colour. let them by twelve hours. then<lb/> boil them very slow a quarter of an hour then lay them by for a day<lb/> then boil them a third time in this manner, then put in small<lb/> dry Pots, in a day or two cover them with brown paper -<lb/> <hi rend="underline">This is a very good receipt</hi> -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/034|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
6<lb/> <head>Artichoke bottons a Fricasy</head><lb/> <pb/> 13/ Take the buttons of as many Artichokes as you choose for<lb/> a Dish either green or Day, if dry steep them over night<lb/> in a little hot water, have ready a little good cream add<lb/> to it when boiling a bit of butter rolled in flour a little<lb/> suet, a little mace. stir this on the fire one way. colour a<lb/> proper thickness and taste add the artichoke to which you<lb/> may first give a oil soft water and milk. so send<lb/> up to Table. -<lb/> <pb/> <head>Apple Pudding. - Mrs Donaldson.</head><lb/> 14. Take some large codlings so as to give you half a pound of pulp,<lb/> put them down in Cold water, let them boil very slow, until<lb/> very tender press them through a hair si<add>e</add>ve so as to get out<lb/> all the pulp, add to this the Rind of a Lemon. beat to cream<lb/> the yolk of seven Eggs. Sugar to your taste. Meet a quarter<lb/> of a pound of Butter to Oil, let it Coate it little, and also<lb/> be the last thing put into the apples. a little Lemon juice<lb/> mix all well. put it into your dish for baking with a<lb/> rich paste at bottom half an hour does it. <lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/035|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
7<lb/> <head>Anchovy Catsup. - Miss Boileau.</head><lb/> <pb/> 15/ One pound of anchovy. half an ounce of mace quarter oz<lb/> Cloaves. a little bruised ginger. the rind of a Lemon a few Shallots<lb/> a small faggot of sweet herbs. such as thyme Savoy + dish<lb/> add one quart of port. half a pint of Vinegar, give this a<lb/> boil and bottle it for use.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Almond Custard baked -</head> very good.<lb/> 16/ Two ounces of Butter melted to oil a quarter of a pound of<lb/> Sugar pounded and sifted the like quantity of Almond.<lb/> Blanched and pounded, but not too fine, a few bitter an <unclear>cong</unclear><lb/> <unclear>threw</unclear>, five yolks of Eggs, and one white well beat to<lb/> a cream, add the Sugar to it, add a little Orange flavour<lb/> Water to the Almond to flavor them and also to keep<lb/> the Almond from going to oil during the pounding, <lb/> the Butter not to be cream when going in to the<lb/> ingredients, now to be put in until just going in to the<lb/> oven which must be cool. the above to be put into <unclear>Ayes</unclear>.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/036|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
8<lb/> <head>Angelico Stalks Curded.</head><lb/> <pb/> 17/ Take young Stalks, free from Specks cut them into<lb/> proper lengths, lay them in water for one night. put them<lb/> down next day in hard water, which must be boiling<lb/> when Boiling so as to peel easy. dry them in a Towel,<lb/> then weight them and allow double their weight in<lb/> Sugar, and to every 2 lb + half of Sugar allow one pint<lb/> of water. Set the sugar and water on the fire until it<lb/> melts, and when ready to Boil keep them close <unclear>couned</unclear>, <lb/> until the are given and sufficiently tender, so put by<lb/> for use as usual, small pots and Brandy, papers if you<lb/> choose any dry.- do as fallacces when given and tender<lb/> enough take them out of the Syrup and lay them<lb/> on Earthen dishes, let the Syrup boil until it comes<lb/> to a Curdy Right, put in the stalks again and let them<lb/> boil until almost Dry. keeping them stirring all the <lb/> while for fear of burning. when so far done take them<lb/> out as fast as you Can. one by one, twist or hi them<lb/> up as you fancy, when cold they until be dry.<lb/> a good and pretty Sweet meat.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/037|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
9<lb/> <head>Almond Cream Supper</head><lb/> <pb/> 18/ Boil a pint of cream. heat a handful of Garden almonds<lb/> some butter also to flavor, heat them fine, add them to the<lb/> cream sugar to your taste, and a little orange flower water,<lb/> give all a boil on a clear and slow flower, until this<lb/> grows pretty thick. then Strain it and put it into Cups.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Browning for all made and savory Dishes very useful</head><lb/> 19/ Pound fine 4 ounces of double refined sugar. put it into<lb/> a clean frying pan, with one oz: of butter, set it over a very<lb/> clear and slow fire, mix it very well all the time and<lb/> keep stirring of it, when it begins to froth, the Sugar is then<lb/> Melting, hold it high over the fire, or stew hole, have ready<lb/> a pint of red wine, and when the Sugar and butter<lb/> is of a days brown, pour in a little of the wine. stir it<lb/> well together. - then add more wine. and keep stirring it as<lb/> before, put in half an ounce of Jamaca pepper, quarter of<lb/> an oz of cloves, six shallots peeled, a few blades of Mace,<lb/> three spoonfuls of Catseep, a little Salt, the rind of a Lemon<lb/> Boil all. slowly for ten minutes pour it into a Bason to<lb/> Cool, when cold <sic>scim</sic> off all <unclear>fiace</unclear> or Scum. very clean,<lb/> to Bottle for use, very little of this sufficient to colour any<lb/> Gravy and is very good. <lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/038|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
10<lb/> <del>Blomange.</del> <head>Blanchemange</head><lb/> 20/ Take two ounces of Isinglass, pound it well and open it out<lb/> into Small Shreds, pour on it half a pint of boiling water, and<lb/> keep it over a slow fire. until dissolved, then boil a quart of<lb/> good cream, sugar as you like, add this to the Isinglass<lb/> also bitter Almonds finely pounded, give all a boil. Strain<lb/> through a Napkin. these season it with Orange flavour<lb/> Water if boiled in the cream + would loose the flavour,<lb/> Stir it after and when cool, fill your Cups or Shapes<lb/> by so doing it will not bitter, turn it out and if you<lb/> Chose stick it with out almonds.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Bread Sauce very good.</head> M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Donaldson.<lb/> 21 Cut the pick of Stale Bread very thin. pour a little boiling<lb/> water on it and let it stand for some brine to soften<lb/> then bruise it fine with a spoon. put it down with<lb/> a little rich cream in a Saucepan. a blade or two<lb/> of Mace, a shallot, a little Anchovy Liquor, boil this<lb/> smooth. <unclear>Duck</unclear> and white, when going to table take out<lb/> the Onion or Shallot.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/039|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
11<lb/> <head>Breast of Veal Stewed, very good.</head><lb/> <pb/> 22/ Spit your Veal, take very little pepper, Salt, Mace. Nutmeg pounded<lb/> fine. rub it over the meat. put it to the fire, dust it with flour<lb/> thick. do not beast it, if the veal be fat. least you take of the<lb/> Spices, or flour which serves to thicken the Sauce. make Gravy<lb/> as follows for it, peel in the bottom of your toss pan. a few slices<lb/> of Bacon cut very thin, some rich and very strong gravy<lb/> that has been made of a variety of meats such as beef,<lb/> Mutton, veal, or fowl as may happen to be convenient.-<lb/> one or two onions. two carrots cut long but first scra<hi rend="underline">pe</hi>d very<lb/> clean, a few blades of mace, a little thyme. cover this close<lb/> and set it on a slow fire to stew but take care. least<lb/> it should burn or singe when brown and properly seasoned<lb/> take out the Bacon. Onion + c Your veal being well<lb/> done and pretty Brown. put it side the Gravy and let it<lb/> stew for half an hour or better as you find it tender and<lb/> the sauce thickened. add Oysters scalded in their own Liquor<lb/> and a little of the Liquor, also Mushroom, grated Lemon, a little<lb/> Lemon juice or pickle, a little Catsup; in <unclear>sleort</unclear> any Savory<lb/> that you would add. - to scotch Callops, so send up garnished<lb/> add it to the gravy or more thickening add a bit of butter bruised in <lb/> flour. <hi rend="underline">This is a good and Genteel head Dish.</hi><lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/040|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
12<lb/> <head>Sweet Breads. Roasted.</head><lb/> <pb/> 23/ parboil the sweet breads, take out of the water. rub them over<lb/> with melted butter slake over this Crumms of Bread very fine<lb/> and a little nutmeg, lay them on this before the fire, or in an<lb/> oven to brown, when a good colour froth them up with butter<lb/> either lay them over your Ragout, or on a Dish by themselves<lb/> with melted Butter under them.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Bread Dumplings.-</head><lb/> 24/ A pound of Grated Bread half a pound of Sliced Suit<lb/> a quarter of a pound of Carrots 4 Eggs whites and yolks<lb/> two spoonfuls of Sugar, a little Nutmeg a little Brandy<lb/> make this into four Dumplings, drudge the laurels and<lb/> tie them up, put them down in boiling water, and turn<lb/> them often least they should settle, when done enough<lb/> they will rise to the top, <hi rend="underline">Sauce</hi> Sugar brandy and<lb/> a little white wine. - Melted Butter you may bake the<lb/> above in Cups first buttered and turned out.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/041|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
13<lb/> <head>Beef potted.</head><lb/> <pb/> 25/ Cut the inside of the Sirloyne, or part of the rump of Beef in<lb/> small cuts face from fat as you can, the fillets answers very<lb/> well, season this with pepper and salt, let it lie so, for two<lb/> or three days. add very little salt petre, then put it down<lb/> in a pot or pan with beef suet cut very small at top <unclear>Hattorn</unclear><lb/> put it on a slow fire or oven. as it answers best. for some<lb/> hours, cover it very close and keep in the steam, when<lb/> when very tender. take up the meat free from strings or fat,<lb/> full as quick as you can to pieces, have a person ready with<lb/> a large Bowl and <sic>pestile</sic> and pound while the meal is<lb/> hot, as fast as you can, cutting it by a spoonful at a<lb/> time with the warm grace it was <unclear>blaned</unclear> in and which<lb/> must be kept very warm all the time, when pounded enough<lb/> rub the small pots, with grace and fill them and press<lb/> the pounded meat down hand in them.- next day cover the<lb/> pots with melted butter, so lay by for use this will keep<lb/> a long time. -<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/042|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
14<lb/> <head>Brocoli and Eggs- for a Supper.</head><lb/> <pb/> 26/ Boil the <sic>Brockla</sic> in hard water and salt very <unclear>gum</unclear>, keep<lb/> one large bunch for the idle, lay a piece toast in your Dish,<lb/> put melted butter over it and then lay on some buttered Eggs<lb/> done as <unclear>Pollaws</unclear>. Take 8 or ten Eggs as you may have occasion<lb/> for beat them very well, yolks and whites, put them down <lb/> in a Copper Saucepan, with a bit of Mace and a little<lb/> butter and Salt. on a clean good fire, keep stirring one<lb/> way all the time, until they begin to thicken. the heat of<lb/> the Saucepan will do them enough put the large bunch<lb/> of <sic>Brockla</sic> in the middle and garnish round with the <lb/> smaller pieces.- <lb/> <pb/> <head>Comport Brolie.</head> supper<lb/> 27/ Take twelve apples Golden pippins are best. quarter them<lb/> and pare them. put them down in a pan with half a<lb/> pound of Clarified Sugar and some preserved Orange<lb/> Let them on a slow fire, ,cover them with a sheet of paper<lb/> when near done mash them with a spoon and Squeeze<lb/> in the juice of a Lemon. save it cold and garnish it<lb/> with preserved orange, or <unclear>litrow</unclear>.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/043|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
15<lb/> <head>Burnt Cream. for Supper.</head><lb/> <pb/> 28/ Boil a quart of rich Cream, with a stick of Cinamon in it<lb/> and some Lemon rind when properly flavoured. take out the<lb/> Cinamon and Lemon. add the yolks of Seven Eggs well<lb/> beat, Sugar and Orange flavor water to your table, set<lb/> it on a slow fire until it becomes as thick as a Custard<lb/> then put it on the Dish you intend for table, and let it<lb/> stand until next day as it must be perfectly cold, take<lb/> a quarter of a pound of Sugar sifted. Stew it over the<lb/> Custard heat a Salamander very hot, and hold it over the<lb/> Sugar a little before it goes to Table, if it were done too soon<lb/> the Sugar would lose its orispress, the Sugar will Melt +<lb/> have a fine brown, were not the Custard quite cold<lb/> the Salamander would Curdle it. -<lb/> <pb/> <head>Rump of Beef Stewed. very good. -</head><lb/> 29/ Choose a fat rump of Beef, the size you like season<lb/> it with pepper, salt, pounded cloves, and Mace of these not<lb/> not too much, let it lie by for three days, rubbing Stewing<lb/> in this seasoning, put it then down to Stew with as<lb/> much water as will be level with the Beef, the less the<lb/> better, add to this <unclear>tivo</unclear> or three Anchoveys. two or three Carrots <lb/> Scraped
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/044|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
16<lb/> Scraped down and cut length ways, a Lemon cut in four<lb/> two or three onions or Shallots let this Stew for some time<lb/> then turn the Beef. that all parts may be alike done, when<lb/> near ready for Dinner take up some of its own Gravy scim<lb/> it clean from fat, add more Seasoning should it require it<lb/> also a little Cabbage thicken it with a little flour, have<lb/>ready currants, Turnips and cellery cut it in small<lb/> pieces. boil these in the Gravy and pour over your beef<lb/> when dished four <unclear>meat rolls</unclear> is very good if roots cannot<lb/> be had - His Beef may be Stewed the day before you<lb/> you want it for Dinner<lb/> <pb/> <head>Brisket of Beef Stewed.</head><lb/> 30/ Rub the edges of the Beef the day before, or just as<lb/> you put it down with grounded mace, stick about<lb/> twenty cloves in the fat side of it, put down the beef<lb/> on the fat side with two quarts of water, Six onions<lb/> sliced, two Table spoonfuls of of whale pepper, a<lb/> spoonful of allspice and very little salt, cover it<lb/> very close and set an slow fire for some hours, when<lb/> near done turn the meat and let it stew until quite<lb/> tender. then take some of its own Gravy serve it clean<lb/> from fat. thicken it with flour properly add Catsup,<lb/> have
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/045|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
17<lb/> have roots Boiled and Cut in small bits, give these<lb/> a Boil in the Savory gravey before you put it over<lb/> the Beef, some slices of lean Bacon put down into<lb/> the Bap improves it in the flavor. M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Stewart Grange<lb/> <pb/> <head>Dutch Beef</head> <lb/> 1/ Take a good fat round of beef, pound two ounces of<lb/> Salt Petre fine, rub it well into the Beef let it stand for<lb/> a day, then season it with pepper. Salt, pounded Mace,<lb/> Cloves, Allspice, very well, all pounded very well fine,<lb/> let it stand ten days, or better rubbing in the Pickle this<lb/> <unclear>beats</unclear> and turning it once or twice in that <unclear>thine</unclear>, then put<lb/> down in a deep Oven or Crack the shape of itself, to<lb/> bake with a good quantity of Beef suet out small, and<lb/> laid at top of Bottom keep in the Steam, it will<lb/> take six hours to Bake it or more on a very slow<lb/> fire sometimes <unclear>least</unclear> it with the Gravy that comes<lb/> from the baking it will keep a long time. -<lb/> <pb/> <head>Beef Pickled.</head> very good- Mrs Truin.<lb/> 32/ Make a pickle so strong of (Salt and water) as to <unclear>hear</unclear> an<lb/> Egg, laid it and skim it very clear when stone cold from it<lb/> over yr. Beef <unclear>obsene</unclear> you are not to press down the pieces as if salted in the<lb/> Common way let the Pickle gget between them and turn them once in the fortnight<lb/> cut your beef in such pieces as will ans<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. y<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. own table best. this will keep<lb/> a long time and has been tried with great success.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/046|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
18<lb/> <head>Breast of Veal Coloured</head><lb/> <pb/> 33/ Row a small breast of veal, lay it in water soft<lb/> it must be for three or four hours to soak and whiten<lb/> then wipe it very dry, and Season it with white pepper<lb/> Mace Nutmeg and Salt finely pounded, tie it up<lb/> very tight and boil it very well in soft water<lb/> and nick when cool take off the bandage and keep<lb/> the Color an a Sauce of lean and water strained.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Breast of Lamb Ragout.</head><lb/> 34/ Have ready some very strong good Gravy, chop<lb/> fine two or three Anchoveys beast your Lamb breast<lb/> or Neck, pretty <unclear>sucart</unclear> and brown, lay it in the Stew<lb/> pan with the Gravy Anchovy, some sweet breads<lb/> that has been boiled or fryed, and cut in Small<lb/> pieces, some Oysters a little of their liquor, a few bits of lean<lb/> Bacon and Onion stuck with Cloves. let all stew until<lb/> of Butter and flour, grated Lemon a little juice so send<lb/> it up.- <lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/047|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
19<lb/> <head>Butter milk Hung. - a Scotch Cream</head><lb/> <pb/> 35/ Take the Corner or thick part that rises on the top of the<lb/> Butter mick in Summer. put it into a clean Towel, hang it<lb/> up. let all whey run off from it. lay it in a Dish, Sweeten<lb/> it to your taste, with pounded Sugar. after this put into<lb/> a Shape a fine wet Cloth. lay the Curd in this when settled<lb/> and the whey run off turn it out on your dish it is to be<lb/> Served up an grate over this a good deal of Lemon rind<lb/> as it gives it a fine flavor. Sweeten some rich Cream, add<lb/> a little Orange flour water, pour it round the Curd in <lb/> the Dish. this is very near as good as whip Cheese.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Bath receipt for Mutton hash.-</head><lb/> 36/ Roast a Shoulder until near done, cut off the blade very<lb/> neat score it. and Shake on this a Little pepper and Salt<lb/> and broil it nice. Cut the rest of your mate in thin small slices,<lb/> have some very strong Gravy <unclear>fue</unclear> from grace, mince a few<lb/> Shallots fine, some pickled <unclear>cumlice</unclear>, a little catsup, a dust of<lb/> <sic>Cayane</sic> pepper, give all a boil thicken it with flour. let the <unclear>hashot</unclear><lb/> meat get a smart heat and so send up the black hour laid<lb/> on it. a little vinegar.-<lb/> <pb/> Beet
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/048|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
20<lb/> <head>Beet Roots pickled.</head><lb/> <pb/> 37/ Wash beet Roots but do not scrape or cut them. Boil them<lb/> as you would Carrots, but not too much rub the outside skin<lb/> off with a coarse cloth, then slice them, a proper thickness, put<lb/> them into Cold Vinegar, with whole pepper, ginger, a little suet,<lb/> so keep for use.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To Bake Mushrooms.</head><lb/> 38/ Take small bottoms of Mushrooms, wipe them very clean<lb/> put a piece of white pepper in the bottom of the Dish you<lb/> intend to serve them up in, but it may well- put in a<lb/> layer of Mushrooms with the Stalks uppermost season them<lb/> with pepper and Salt, a little nutmeg and cover this with<lb/> crumbled Bread. some bits of butter here and there.<lb/> so continue until your dish is full. Bake in a moderate<lb/> oven. if not brown enough on the top, use a Salamander.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Beef or a Mutton Broth</head><lb/> 39/ Cut five or six Turnips into dice but clean them, two or<lb/> three Carrots in small bits 4 or 5 Onions a head or two of<lb/> <sic>Sellery</sic>, put all into a pipkin cover close and let it boil,<lb/> some grains of whole pepper, and a small cup of Barley,<lb/> add to this a little water and let it stew on a very slow <add>fire</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/049|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
21<lb/> fire until tender, but do not let them go to Mash, boil the<lb/> Broth, (boil the broth as Mrs <unclear>Silun</unclear> did) that is twenty minutes<lb/> from the time the broth begins to boil. the meat to be put<lb/> down in cold water, and when going up to table put<lb/> your roots to your broth and send it up in your <sic>Turreen</sic>.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Calves head.-</head><lb/> 40/ Divide the head, lay it in soft water to soak, some time,<lb/> then put it down in a very clean pot, either in a black<lb/> tied or at large, boil it until tender first taking out<lb/> the brains, dust them with flour, tie them in a rag and so<lb/> boil them. when the head is tender that your finger will<lb/> go through it, take <add>it</add> out of the water let it cool as it will<lb/> not eat handsome until cold. cut it then in small bits<lb/> about an Inch thick, but longer let one side of the head<lb/> remain whole and to <add>be</add> laid in the middle of the Dish<lb/> put down the hashed meat, with some strong good Gravy<lb/> a little white wine. a little pounded mace, nutmeg<lb/> both grated <del>nutmeg</del> Lemon Catsup Oyster Liquor, Stew<lb/> all this together and when near done thicken until little<lb/> flour rolled in Batter. add Oysters Mushrooms, pickled<lb/> or otherwise - what quantity you judge proper cut the Eye<lb/> in thin slices. take the whole side of the head. rub it<lb/> over with the yolk of an Egg. Shake over it some <add>crumbs</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/050|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
22<lb/> Crumbs of bread minced parsley, Grated Lemon Nutmeg Salt<lb/> all very fine, lay it before the fire on a tie or in an Oven.<lb/> to brown beast it with Butter before a quick fire when<lb/> brown enough lay this in the middle of your dish. the <lb/> hash pound it, garnish with four meat balls, fryed brains <lb/> in very Small fryters. Lemon + + - <lb/> To make the fyters, when the brains are boiled bruise them <unclear>first</unclear><lb/> add to them salt, nutmeg. fine sliced parsley, flour yolk of an<lb/> Egg, heat all up together and fry in very small nice Cakes<lb/> Garnish the head with them- <lb/> <pb/> <head>Ryans receipt for Calfs head like Mock Turtle. very good</head><lb/> 41/ Scald the head very clean from hair, soak it in soft water<lb/> Boil it pretty tender. cut it in thin pieces, an Inch thick some<lb/> what longer put it down in a stew pan. with two quarts<lb/> of good rich Gravy a little Salt Mace, pounded nutmeg<lb/> Grated Lemon very little Cayenne pepper, a pint of alladeria<lb/> or strong white wine, truffles a few artichoke buttons, cut in<lb/> bits first boiled, stew all this for some time when the Meat<lb/> looks clear and the Gravy strong. squeeze in a little Lemon juice<lb/> a little blended flour and butter should it <unclear>meopine</unclear> to the<lb/> thicker in the Sauce, add four meat balls, hard boiled<lb/> Eggs. the Yolks pounded, with butter, and made into <lb/> small balls oysters and their Liquor - taken from the<lb/> Grand Magazine-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/051|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
25<lb/> <head>Creampan Cakes.</head><lb/> <pb/> <gap/>/A pint of Cream or Stuppings, beast Six Eggs very well.<lb/> with two or three spoonfuls of flour a little Brandy,<lb/> Sugar to your Taste, a nutmeg, a quarter of a pound of<lb/> fresh butter melted, fry them with out turning, <unclear>ahseme</unclear> to<lb/> put but very little in the pan at once, or they will not do<lb/> right you are to put butter into the pan only for the<lb/> first pan cake that is fryed.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Scotch Collops.- very good.-</head><lb/> 43/ Cut your veal into thin Collops with the grain of the meat, <lb/> pound them well with a rolling pin, dredge them with<lb/> flour and fry them on a clear smart fire, a nice clean pot<lb/> and plenty of boiling butter, the quicker you fry them the tenderer<lb/> they will be, let them be a fine even brown, the top of the veal<lb/> to be kept for a Color, and be put in the middle of the dish<lb/> have ready some very good strong Gravy, put it down in <lb/> your Stew pan add a little salt, pounded Mace Nutmeg<lb/> grated Lemon rind Oyster Liquor, a little catsup when the Gravy<lb/> is good and strong add the Catsup to it. if not thick enough<lb/> add a little Butter bruised in flour, but this is seldom<lb/> wanted as the flour on the Collops generally thickens it<lb/> enough as well as to brown it. fill the Cup if you choose <add>it</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/052|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
24<lb/> it with force meat roll it up and sew it very tight. <lb/> it may either be fryed in the Pan, or baked in the oven<lb/> the oven is best.- and the most even in the Colour. this<lb/> for the middle of the Dish standing as a Cooler and the<lb/> has round it. <unclear>observe</unclear> an Onion stuck with Cloves<lb/> may be boiled in the Gravy, and taken <del>up</del> <add>out</add> when going up<lb/> to table, add Oysters. Scalded Mushrooms or any Savory<lb/> good thing. that you may have, a little Lemon juice<lb/> when going to dish, Catsup, Garnish with Lemon and<lb/> four meat balls.- a glass of good strong white wine<lb/> added is also very good.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Collared Eels. very good.</head><lb/> 44/ Skin the Eels and up them down the belly, spread<lb/> them flat, and take out the back bone. cut off the heads<lb/> and tails, wipe them very well (dry) season them very <lb/> high, with pepper, salt, pounded mace + cloves. let them<lb/> lie for a day or two. to take the seasoning, then roll them<lb/> up as close and tight as possible and tie them in a Cloth.<lb/> put them down in soft water and Salt to boil, when<lb/> boiling add a little vinegar. some whole pepper and <lb/> bay leaves when the Eels are boiled tender take them out<lb/> of the Liquor. and when cold put them back in this<lb/> Liquor to keep for use.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/053|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
25<lb/> <head>Curd Pudding. very good</head><lb/> <pb/> 45./ One quart of sweet milk made into a tender Curd<lb/> with butter milk, the yolks of nine Eggs well beat, + one white<lb/> a whole nutmeg grated, half a naggin of Brandy, six ozs<lb/> of Butter melted with a little cream, four oz of Sugar half<lb/> an Oz of Sweet Almonds the like of bitter d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. pounded coarsly<lb/> mix all together butter small Cups. fill them with the above<lb/> Bake them in a moderate oven, when done. turn them<lb/> out on a dish the Sauce White Wine, Sugar and little<lb/> Brandy mixt and put under them.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Plumb Cake.</head><lb/> 46/ Work one pound of fresh butter, with your hand, to a cream<lb/> have one pound of Sugar pounded and sifted. one pound<lb/> of fine flour, well dryed before the fire, beat with a whisk the<lb/> yolks of 12 Eggs, also whisk up the whites to a solid Curd<lb/> when the yolks of Eggs, one beat to light cream, then add the<lb/> Sugar first by a spoonful at a time. then the white <unclear>bend</unclear><lb/> then the flour all by degrees. still continuing to beat with<lb/> your hand until all be mixed, you are to beat the above<lb/> for one hour. add one nutmeg grated. sliced citron a<lb/> glass of good brandy, a pound of Currants. well washed<lb/> and picked. two ozs of Sweet almonds blanched and cut <add>small</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/054|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
26/<lb/> small, butter double papers and lice the bottom + sides<lb/> of the pan you intend baking your Cake in, add a few<lb/> pounded Cloves. the Oven must be Smart but take care<lb/> you do not burn it. when you think it baked that is<lb/> one hour and half try it by putting a knife into it. and if<lb/> it comes out free from any part of the Cake, it is done<lb/> enough. but if not must remain some time longer to soak.<lb/> do not put in the Currants. Citron + + + until<lb/> just going into the oven, else it might settle.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Currant Jelly.</head><lb/> 47/ Pick the best and ripest Currants from the Stack, you<lb/> may mix red and white it improves the Colour put them<lb/> into a clean pitcher. Cover it close so as to keep in the<lb/> Steem, set this in a pot or Kettle of Cold water on the fire<lb/> and let it boil for some hours, until all juice be extruded<lb/> from the fruit Strain it through a fine Sieve and Set<lb/> it by until next day. take care it be not sliced for fear<lb/> of Mudding the Jelly then Skim if off very well from the Sediment<lb/> at bottom, pour into your pressing pan, and to every pint<lb/> of this Liquor allow one pound of Lump Sugar put this<lb/> on a clear <del>slow</del><add>fast 15 minutes</add> fire skim any dirt that may arise when<lb/> it has boiled so as to jelly in the spoon. put it into small glasses<lb/> and Bladders and keep them in a dry place. - <add>good</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/055|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
27<lb/> <head>Lemon Custard</head><lb/> <pb/> 40/ Grate the rind of three Lemons, also the juice boil this to<lb/> a rich syrup, let it cool. boil a pint of good cream. let<lb/> also cool, heat until the yolks of six Eggs, mix them all<lb/> together and stir it one way over a slow fire, until thick<lb/> enough, then pour it into Cups as usual.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lemon Cheese Cakes.</head><lb/> 49/ Beat the yolks of Eight Eggs and four Whites separately<lb/> grate the rind of two large Lemons, also the juice, half a<lb/> nutmeg, a quarter of a pound of Sugar a little Brandy<lb/> mix all together, and when going into the oven, add<lb/> six ounces of fresh Butter melted.- and pour into the<lb/> other Materials very slowly for fear of Curdling, fill the<lb/> pattys with rich paste, so put in the Cheese Cake<lb/> what heat does the paste is sufficient for the rest.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cucumbers sliced. a good pickle.</head><lb/> 50./ Take large Cucumbers pour and slice them rather thick<lb/> they eat the Crisper for being thick, put them into a Crock<lb/> and slake Salt between every layer of them, let them<lb/> stand until next day, put them into a hair Sieve <lb/> to drain for some time, then lay them regular in your <add>Pots</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/056|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
28/<lb/> Pots or Crock and over each layer put Slices of onion<lb/> Cut thin add plenty of whole pepper and Ginger <del>upon</del>when<lb/> the pots are full pour on them boiling Vinegar +<lb/> cover close from Air. this is good common Pickle<lb/> the vinegar should be boiled in a copper vessel or<lb/> will blacken the Pickles tin or pewter would also<lb/> hurt the colour of them.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Chicken Fricasied.</head> <lb/> 51/ Take one two Chickens, as you may have occasion<lb/> for, slice the night before. so as to be very tender. or<lb/> very early in the morning. when scalded and <unclear>Seccured</unclear><lb/> put them down to boil. as this makes these cut<lb/> firm and better. when nice boiled take them up.<lb/> and cut them in small bits as is usual for a<lb/> friccasy and take off the Skin. you may lay this<lb/> by until you have an occasion to make use of<lb/> it then put it down in a little milk and water<lb/> to boil. at same time have ready in your toss<lb/> pan some rich good cream. a little salt. a little<lb/> mace. when boiling you may thicken it with a little<lb/> blended flour or the yolk of an Egg well beat<lb/> it with the latter. take care after the Egg goes in<lb/> or it will Curdle, a smart heat is all it <add>requires</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/057|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
29<lb/> requires with an Egg threw in the Chicken that has<lb/> been boiling in the Milk and water give one smart<lb/> heat <del>fire</del> to dish, a little Lemon grated or some fine<lb/> minced parsley some times one added.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cellery Sauce.</head><lb/> 52/ Wash and clean your Sellery very well, cut it into<lb/> pieces, an Inch long. put it down in Soft water and<lb/> a little Milk much milk would Curdle it, when<lb/> boiled tender strain it from the water, let it cool and<lb/> then add a little good Cream to keep it to its colour<lb/> have ready some very good rich Gravy, add the Sellery to it<lb/> and let it stew, on a slow fire, take care to stir it<lb/> or it will burn, rub a bit of butter in flour.- dissolve<lb/> it in the Gravy as it stews, stir it one way, you may<lb/> add more cream should it want it, also a little oyster Liquor<lb/> have ready some Oysters which must be scalded + just<lb/> heated up in the Sauce, a little Mace, a little Nutmeg,<lb/> Grate a little Lemon peel. it should stew to be thick<lb/> and smooth, when just going to dish add a little Catsup<lb/> this sauce if good will be very white from the Several<lb/> ingredients Oysters and Catsup being the Chief thing in it.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/058|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
30/<lb/> Cucumbers and French beans pickled<lb/> <pb/> 53/ Pull the Cucumbers in a dry day. take off stalks and<lb/> Blossoms. boil hard water and salt a good pickle pour it on<lb/> the cucumbers in a crock. cover it up. Keep it in a warm<lb/> place let them stand three or four days. then boil your pickle<lb/> every day untill the cucumbers are as green as you would choose<lb/> observe the pickle must be boiled in a bell mettle Skillet<lb/> when your enough wipe them very dry. out of this pickle and<lb/> throw them into salt and water to take off the<lb/> least change this a second time. then boil vinegar and<lb/> + pour it on the pickles. add to this salt. <unclear>cohole</unclear> pepper.<lb/> Finger. a clove of Garlick. a little white mustard Seed.<lb/> the quantity according to the cucumbers cover it with a<lb/> bladder and in a month you may use the pickles you<lb/> may pickle astertion seed with the above.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Codling Rye and Custard.</head><lb/> 54/ put down the Codlings in soft water in a very clean<lb/> <unclear>Trow</unclear> pot, and on a very slow fire, let them continue so<lb/> until you can take off the Skin with ease, cut off<lb/> blossoms and tops, put the fruit then down in a Ball<lb/> Kettle Skillet in hard water, which may be warm, Cover<lb/> them with their own Leaves, and Skins that was <add>picked</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/059|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
31<lb/> picked off, keep them on a slow fire, until they look<lb/> green to please the eye, then lay them in your dish<lb/> with plenty of pounded <del>Butter</del> Sugar over them. this<lb/> improves the Colour. Cover the dish with <del>the</del> light paste<lb/> and bake it. when cold, quarter the pye, and have ready<lb/> a common Custard, also cold and pour over the apples, <lb/> Garnish it with neat bits of the Cut out paste or<lb/> apple Bisket.- This is a very good receipt.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cream pan Cakes</head><lb/> 55/ Take a pint of Cream, or Stippings beat six Eggs very well.<lb/> with this or three spoonfuls of flour, a little Brandy, a nutmeg<lb/> Sweeten to your liken, a quarter of a pound of fresh Butter melted<lb/> to oil fry them without burning, but you must to put very little<lb/> in the pan at once, else they will not do right you an only<lb/> to put in butter for frying of the first pan Cake.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cellery Stewed with Oysters.</head><lb/> 56/ Cut the Cellery in quarters length ways, tie in lunches, and<lb/> boil it tender in soft water have gravy ready seasoned in your<lb/> toss pan thickened with a bit of butter blended in flour add<lb/> a little Catsup have Oysters ready scalded, and put them and<lb/> the Cellery into the Gravy, give all a good head, so dish it<lb/> for Table.-<lb/> <pb/> Stewed
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/060|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
32/<lb/> <head>Stewed Cellery.</head><lb/> <pb/> 57/ Cut your Cellary handsomely wash it very clean. boil it in<lb/> soft water and salt until tender, then strain it off, stew it<lb/> then, for a quarter of an hour in some very strong good Gravy<lb/> thicken it with the size of a nut of butter rolled in flour<lb/> add a little Nutmeg, Salt, pepper and some Catsup.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cutlets of a Neck of Veal.-</head><lb/> 58/ Cut your veal in small ribbs. Grate Nutmeg on each<lb/> side, beat up the yolk of an Egg, rub this on the veal<lb/> shake over this fine Crumbs of Bread grated Lemon<lb/> a little Suet. pin each rib in a piece of white paper very<lb/> Close. and put them into a Smart Oven on a<lb/> broiling <unclear>Tron</unclear> or tin. - Sheets - half an hour bakes them<lb/> when done, take off the papers dish them and put melted<lb/> Butter under them Garnish with Lemon.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cabbage pickled, white</head><lb/> 59/ Cut the hardest and best white Cabbage in quarters<lb/> put them down in boiling water. which must be coloured<lb/> with Tumerick, so high as you think proper give the<lb/> Cabbage a good scald. take them out and lay them<lb/> upon Dishes to Drain. shake salt over them and <add>let</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/061|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
33<lb/> let them lie for some days turn them and slake salt<lb/> again, and so let them lie for light or ten days, turning<lb/> them two or three times during this space, you may add<lb/> to this Cellery but in quarters, Onions small, or cut in slices<lb/> Collyflowers, and a little Garlick, they are to get a scald<lb/> in the Boiling water and Tumerick, as the Colour grows pale<lb/> add more tumerick and all to drain as above, when<lb/> ready to put up, lay a layer of Cabbage + + and<lb/> between each layer put long pepper, sliced ginger, white<lb/> Mustard, when the vessell is full, pour boiling Vinegar<lb/> over the Cabbage. + + and cover up close. this is a<lb/> good receipt. <hi rend="underline">got from M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Giddons the primates Cook.</hi><lb/> <pb/> <head>Chickens white Sauce for same.</head><lb/> 60/ Melt butter pretty thick, with a little good gravy, put to<lb/> it a Naggin of good cream, a little salt, give it one<lb/> boil Squeeze the juice of a Lemon, just as you are going<lb/> to use it, stewing it well. N: B: This sauce is good with<lb/> boiled Turkey adding Pickled Cucumbers cut very small<lb/> instead of Lemon.-<lb/> <pb/> Cabbage
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/062|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
34<lb/> <head>Cabbage pickled Scarlet</head><lb/> <pb/> 61/ Cut down two or three red Cabbages, as free from the<lb/> white Stalk as you can, do not cut it too fine else it<lb/> will lose its Crispness, shake a god quantity of Salt<lb/> over it, and let it lie, for two or three days, so as to drain<lb/> off all the water the salt will produce. then put into<lb/> well glazed Crock. and pour boiling Vinegar over<lb/> it, and cover it close. add a good many small<lb/> Onions, whole pepper. Ginger a good quantity, you may<lb/> use it in a few days.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Codlings to keep all the year</head><lb/> 62/ Take any quantity of Codling apples you choose<lb/> lay apple Leaves at bottom and Top of the pan. under<lb/> and over each layer of apples. cover them with water<lb/> put them on a stew hearth. on a slow fire, until they<lb/> change colour. but not so as to let them grow soft<lb/> take them out of the water and spread them on<lb/> a Dish to Cool, strain the Liquor off the apple leaves<lb/> and when the apple and Liquor are cold, put the fruit<lb/> into a well glazed Crock and pour the Liquor over <lb/> them. and Cover close from air. either with wet<lb/> Bladders or Sheep suit <unclear>undered</unclear>.- M<sic>rs</sic> Richardson.-<lb/> A
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/063|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
35<lb/> <head>A Custard pudding to Boil.</head><lb/> <pb/> 63/ Eight yolks, and whites of Eggs, well beat up, a little<lb/> flour, to bind it. Brandy Sugar and Nutmeg, to your Taste,<lb/> a quart of good milk half an hour boils it a small bit of Ising=<lb/> glass Melted and put to the above prevents its breaking or<lb/> running to Curds.- half an hour is too much to boil the<lb/> puddings.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cods head to Boil.</head><lb/> 64/ Wash and Clean the head very well, put a faggot of sweet<lb/> herbs in the mouth. put it on your Fish plate, and put<lb/> it down in Cold hard water. a good handful of salt,<lb/> a piece of horse Radish, and some Vinegar. let it boil<lb/> on a slow fire the longer you keep it back. the finer<lb/> the fish will be it must boil until the fins draw easily<lb/> out, when you think it done, take the Kettle off the fire<lb/> raise up the fish plate so as to keep it over the hot steam<lb/> of water. cover it up close with a Cloth, to keep work<lb/> this drys up all Damp and makes the fish eat Curdly and<lb/> dry, garnish it with red Cabbage or any kind of<lb/> pickle also its own fry.-<lb/> Curd and
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/064|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
36<lb/> <head>Curd and Custard. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Jervase</head><lb/> <pb/> 65/ One quart of Cream the like quantity of new milk<lb/> heat well the yolks and whites of Eight Eggs, add them<lb/> to the Cream and Milk. put them into a toss pan,<lb/> over a slow fire. keep stirring constantly, until it<lb/> becomes a hard Curd. then lay it in a Sieve to<lb/> drain being dry, then put it in a Mortar with a <lb/> quantity of Sugar, as you choose pound it fine<lb/> wet a fine Cloth in Orange flower water lay it in the<lb/> shape, you intend to make use of, then put the Curd<lb/> into it, and press it so, as that it may <del>not</del> take<lb/> the impression, turn it out on your Dish stick<lb/> it with almonds. blanched and Cut fine, send it to<lb/> Table with a light Custard poured round it, and season<lb/> as usual.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cakes Macaroon. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Davidson</head><lb/> 66/ Take four oz of sweet almonds. two D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi> of butter, pound<lb/> them fine. Six Oz: of Sugar pounded and sifted. the whites<lb/> of four Eggs beat to a Curd a spoonful of Brandy. when<lb/> all are mixed very well together. drop the size of a large<lb/> Nutmeg, an double papers and beak in a slow oven, for<lb/> ten or twelve Minutes.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/065|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
37<lb/> <head>Cream of Ice</head><lb/> <pb/> 67/ Mix your Cream to your fancy, either Strawberry. Raspberry<lb/> or any flavour you choose. then take a Mould or Shapes to<lb/> answer your Cream, fill them with the cream, take Ice<lb/> pound it small and mix it with some common Salt.<lb/> Set your Moulds in this, and keep gently stirring until it begins<lb/> to grow thick, then fill the shapes you intend for table<lb/> and cover it up with Ice and Salt. when you want to<lb/> make use of them. Dip in warm water and to turn out<lb/> <gap/>ish with an pretty leaves snow is better than Ice with<lb/> <gap/>act.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cucumbers stewed. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Obins.</head><lb/> Cut some large Cucumbers, also some large Onions<lb/> pretty thick round slices, shake a little salt on them to<lb/> <gap/>ge off the water, put a little fresh butter in your frying<lb/> <gap/> drudge in a little flour, when smartly warm, put in<lb/> the cucubmers +. and fry them brown. but do not<lb/> <gap/> <gap/> them into a toss pan. with a little<lb/> <gap/> <gap/> little pepper, salt nutmeg. little catsup and<lb/> <gap/> <gap/> a little chop of Vinegar.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/066|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
39<lb/> <head>A Whipt Cheese.</head><lb/> 69/ Take a quart of rich cream or more as you may have<lb/> occasion for, sweeten it to your taste, put <add>to</add> it the juice<lb/> of three of four Lemons, a large glass of white wine, some<lb/> orange flower water, whip all up, let it stand for a<lb/> little time take off the Curd, put it into a fine hair Sieve<lb/> so as to Drain all the whey from it, let this stand until<lb/> next day, then turn it out on your Dish, and garnish<lb/> the top with Coloured <unclear>Camfits</unclear> Candid Orange, or any<lb/> sweet meat you fancy.- the more juice of Lemon, the <lb/> thicker the Cream will grow you should put a bit<lb/> of fine old Cloth between the sieve and the Cream<lb/> to keep to turn it out- this dish for Supper.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Catchup.- by Mrs Waring.</head><lb/> 70/ Pick the Mushroom very clean. break them down into<lb/> a Crock, and shake a good quantity of Salt over them<lb/> and let them stand for a few days, so as to get out all<lb/> the juice. then put all down in a toss pan to boil, then<lb/> strain them through a Cullender. and get out all the<lb/> Liquor. let this stand until the next day. then skim<lb/> it off free from the grounds put this down to boil, and add<lb/> to it whole black pepper, Ginger. Mace. Cloves allspice. of<lb/> each an oz or you have a quantity of Catsup. one smart boil is suff<hi rend="superscript">t</hi><lb/> as the spices lose their flavour should they boil much when cold.<lb/> Bottle it. put in the spices. Cork the Bottle then keep in a dry place<lb/> Put a clove of Garlick in each Bottle.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/067|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
39<lb/> <head>Bake a Pike</head><lb/> 71/ Scrape the Fish very clean, rub it once with a bit of<lb/> butter. Dust on some flour pretty thick, put it on this to<lb/> bake it in the Oven, you may put a pudding in the belly<lb/> made in the usual manner. Bread fine crumbled mixed with<lb/> Onion, parsley, salt and pepper. mixed suit Lemon grated, and<lb/> met with the Yolk of an Egg.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Very good Fish Sauce.</head><lb/> 72/ Two or three Shalots, two Tea spoonfuls of Cayenne<lb/> pepper, one spoonful of Indian Sag. or good Catchup, the<lb/> like of walnut pickle, mix all into a pint of Vinegar<lb/> Colour it with a little Cocheneal. one large spoonful<lb/> of this is sufficient for very Savory Dish. the like quantity<lb/> in a boat of Melted Butter for fish sauce. This will<lb/> keep a long time- <hi rend="underline">Mrs Donaldson</hi>.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Preserved Damsons M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Donaldson</head><lb/> 73/ Pull your Damsons with the Stalks on, very carefully. for<lb/> fear of rubbing off the blue, or bruising them, split them down<lb/> the picked side skin deep, take their weight in Sugar, best<lb/> Lump, put it down with as much water, as will melt it<lb/> and boil it until quite clear, then take two pewter dishes<lb/> Cover the bottom of one with some of the Syrup. lay a <add>layer</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/068|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
40<lb/> layer of Damsons on the Cut side, and cover with the<lb/> other dish, put them on a slow fire, lifting them up after<lb/> and turning them gently. keep them covered with the Syrup<lb/> until done. let them lie on the pewter Dish for two days.<lb/> giving them a gentle heat every two days. at the end of ten<lb/> days put them in well clayed Crocks when cold put them in<lb/> Brandy papers. they will never work up, but they may open<lb/> dry them renew the Syrup as before mentioned, these are<lb/> remarkably nice and good for whole Damsons.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Damson Jam</head><lb/> 74/ Wipe the Damsons very dry, weigh them pick on the open<lb/> side. put them down in your preserving Pan, on a slow<lb/> fire. stir constantly for fear of burning, as the stones come<lb/> down from the fruit. pick them out. then allow for each<lb/> pound of Damsons one of Sugar boil (pounded or good<lb/> Brown will do for this purpose) and scum clean when<lb/> it looks clear, put it into well aired pots. and cover<lb/> as usual with Brandy proper and Bladders.- Plumbs may<lb/> be done in like manner only quarter Degree of boiling<lb/> the above answers for Dumplings or Saucers for Supper.<lb/><pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/069|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
41<lb/> <head>Duck Stewed.- Tame or Wild.</head><lb/> 75/ Cut the Duck in pieces as for a frigacy, lay it in a<lb/> Dispatcher with some good gravy, add to it a little<lb/> bit of <del>gravy</del> butter but no flour a shallot minced fine,<lb/> a little pepper salt. Catsup. a glass of Claret or port,<lb/> do it over a smart fire, this sauce answers for turned up<lb/> Venison ( got from Major Lehart)<lb/> <pb/> <head>76/ Dumplings of black currants.</head><lb/> Pick the Currants clean from stalks and blossoms.<lb/> and allow an equal weigh of Sugar, brown will<lb/> answer add a very little water. boil all together. until<lb/> the Liquor looks rich and clear. the more they are<lb/> bake the better, a little red Currant juice improves<lb/> it much, when boiled sufficient, put up in pots<lb/> as usual Brandy papers<lb/> <pb/> <head>Dobe Duck. Miss Boileau</head><lb/> 77/ Half Roast, the Duck or if any left from the former<lb/> day, put it down with some good Gravy or<lb/> Onion stuck with Cloves. Carrots and turnips<lb/> cut in small Dice, also some Cellery. let all <lb/> stew for some time, until the roots grow<lb/> tender. cover the toss pan close to keep in the <add>steam</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/070|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
42<lb/> Steam, should it not be thick enough add a bit<lb/> of butter blended in flour, the Duck should be stuffed<lb/> with forced meat, or a preparation of Bread, Suet, parsley<lb/> Lemon rind, pepper salt, Nutmegg. met with an Egg<lb/> This is a very Savory good dish.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>An Olio Pye- very good- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> OBins</head><lb/> 78/ Take Oysters. take off the fins, set them, of Sweet Breads<lb/> artichoke bottoms, asparagus, tops if they can be had,<lb/> Pullets, yolks of hard Boiled Eggs an equal quantity<lb/> Season them with Nutmeg pounded Mace little pepper<lb/> and salt to your taste. fry brown some force meat<lb/> Balls, which are also to be added a little minced<lb/> parsley and Onion very fine, stew all together in<lb/> some very strong Gravy, a little Oyster Liquor, a little<lb/> Catsup and some bits of veal cut thin and small<lb/> thicken this with a bit of butter rolled in flour, when<lb/> Stewed for some time let it then cool. have ready a<lb/> raised Crust if you choose it. if not a dish in the<lb/> Common way will do. <unclear>abscue</unclear> to boil the Pullets and<lb/> Sweet Breads + + + must be boiled as for a <sic>ragoot</sic><lb/> before you put them into the Pye should it want Gravy<lb/> on com.g out of the oven. after the pye by cutting the lid all round<lb/> and pour in what Gravy you choose, also you may add some<lb/> if you have them all sort of Savory things <unclear>imporous</unclear><lb/> this Dish. raised <unclear>neast</unclear> is the <unclear>Gerelteelest</unclear> cover for this Dish-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/071|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
43<lb/> <head>A remarkable receipt for making Walnut</head><lb/> <head>Catchup and esteemed very good.- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Trucin</unclear></head><lb/> <pb/> 79/ Three of four large handfuls of fresh walnuts leaves<lb/> the younger the better, half a pound of white mustard<lb/> seed, half d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. of horse Radish sliced, a quarter of a pound<lb/> of Ginger sliced, for six weeks keep it close covered and<lb/> then strain it off add to this liquor, one quarter of a <lb/> pound of Anchovy. Cloves. whole pepper, allspice<lb/> Mace according as you wish to make it strong and <lb/> Savory. boil the spices smartly in the liquor least the<lb/> Evaporate. for one quarter of an hour, when stone cold<lb/> bottle Cork and raisin, as usual, observe this Catsup<lb/> can be made at any Season when the Walnuts or<lb/> Mushrooms cannot be had. and is very Savory and<lb/> good aand equal to Cherakee Sauces. N. B:-<lb/> add to the above one or two handfuls of bay salt, of<lb/> the spices one oz: + half of each kind for the above<lb/> quantity is sufficient.-<lb/> <pb/> Walnuts
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/072|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
44<lb/> <head>Walnuts pickled Green. Mrs Kelly.</head><lb/> <pb/> 80/ When the Walnuts are full size but yet so young <lb/> that a pin will run into them. throw them into a Crock<lb/> and pour over them, a very strong pickle of boiling salt<lb/> and water. and cover close from air. keep the joint<lb/> under the Pickle by laying a plate over them, let<lb/> them stand for five days thus, at the end of this, boil<lb/> fresh salt and water, and heat as before for three<lb/> times, small standing fifteen days in pickle, then take<lb/> one Gallon of good Vinegar. boil it in the bell mettle<lb/> Skillet and pour it over the walnuts the spices as <lb/> follow. one ounce of white pepper, the same of Cloves<lb/> Mace, Nutmeg<del>g</del> four ounces of Ginger. Cut small<lb/> or bruised, the other spices grasely pounded. put the<lb/> walnuts in your Crock. so as to be always under<lb/> the Liquor, throw the spices among them and add<lb/> a handful of Salt, a few Cloves of Garlick,<lb/> quarter of a pound of Mustard seed, the vinegar<lb/> to be Boiling hot, when you are to put on the Walnuts<lb/> and Spice.- this will keep a long time July is the month.<lb/><pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/073|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
45<lb/> <head>To make remarkable good Ginger Bread</head><lb/> M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Field<lb/> <pb/> 81/ Two pounds of Flour, one pound of Butter, do:<lb/> Brown Sugar. one ounce of Ginger. pour two ounces<lb/> of Caraway seeds, twelve Eggs. only half the whites<lb/> mix the flour. sugar, Ginger and seeds, well together<lb/> beat the Eggs to a Cream. put in the flour by Degrees as you<lb/> would into a Seed Cake. until it grows thick. then<lb/> put in the Butter. which must be melted. then, the<lb/> rest of the flour, work it as you would any other<lb/> kind of Dough, and make it, into Cakes. bake it in<lb/> a slow Oven; this is a very good receipt for any person<lb/> who may have wind in their Stomach.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Common Gingerbread, but good. Miss Read.</head><lb/> 82/ Take one pound of Treacle. make it nick warm, then<lb/> Strain it, put to it one pound of Fresh Butter. work them<lb/> together until they look smooth and of a light brown<lb/> colour. then add a pound of brown Sugar 1 1/2 ounce<lb/> of ginger pounded. the like quantity of Carraway seeds,<lb/> a little candid orange pounded very fine. mix all well<lb/> together and add as much flour as will make it into a paste<lb/> roll it out into Cakes or bottoms as you choose + bake it as usual<lb/> should you make the above into Bottoms, you may put a small<lb/> bit of Candid Orange or Lemon into each hot, it improves them more.<lb/><pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/074|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
46<lb/> <head>Stewed Sole</head><lb/> <pb/> 83/ Wash and Scrape your Sole, and thin them, do<lb/> not take off the fins, make them very dry in the following<lb/> manner, when washed and drained, dust flour on them<lb/> put them in a dry Cloth, after changing the Cloth. so<lb/> as to soak out all damp, let them remain so until<lb/> you all going to fry them. let the Dipping which is<lb/> the last thing to do then with (or any fryed fish) be so<lb/> hot as to boil. dredge the fish and put them into the<lb/> Boiling pan. with the Bellies down, do not turn<lb/> the fish until that side be a fine even Browne<lb/> and perfectly done. then turn them, and do the back<lb/> in like manner, when done take them out to drain,<lb/> observe you must have ready some very good Gravy<lb/> a little whole pepper. two Anchoveys mixed fine. Lemon<lb/> grated, little Catsup a bit of Mace, a few Cloves. let these<lb/> boil, so as to flavor the Gravy then Strain it.- and<lb/> put as much as you shall have occasion for, <lb/> into your stew pan lay your fish in it and stew it<lb/> gently on a slow fire. but take care or the first will break<lb/> should it want a little thicken.g dust in a little flour,<lb/> when just going to dish, add a glass of Claret, garnish<lb/> the Dish with Lemon.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/075|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
47<lb/> <head>Pickled Mushrooms brown very good.-</head><lb/> <pb/> 84/ Take large and small bottoms, not much <unclear>blown</unclear>, <lb/> wipe them very clean. Cut off the dirty end of the stalks<lb/> do not let them take water or it will spoil them, put them<lb/> into a stew pan with whole pepper mace and cloves, sliced<lb/> nutmeg to a pan full of Mushrooms, put a handful of Salt, <lb/> put them on a slow fire and let them stew gently until<lb/> sufficiently done for eating. take them off the fire. and strain<lb/> from the Liquor, put them into small pots. Cover them with<lb/> an equal quantity of thin <unclear>acon</unclear> liquor and Vinegar<lb/> the Liquor must be quite cold, going on the Mushrooms<lb/> cover the pots with wet Bladders. and keep in a dry<lb/> place. should they grow dry add a little vinegar and<lb/> spice, if not kept covered with Liquor. you might put<lb/> Catchsup down at first with the Mushrooms to boil,-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/076|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
40/<lb/> <head>To Dress Goose Giblets. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Marrets</head><lb/> <pb/> 85/ Scald and clean the Giblets very well, put them down<lb/> in a Stew pan, with some good gravy, a bunch of<lb/> Sweet herbs, let them boil on a very Slow fire, when<lb/> done add a little white wine and vinegar, pepper, and <lb/> salt to your taste (then take the blood of your Goose,<lb/> Strain it and put it to your Giblets keeping it stirring<lb/> on a slow fire. do not let it boil else it would run<lb/> together, you may use it either Cold or hot, you may<lb/> use a goose in this manner if not too fat. Cut it<lb/> in pieces stew as before mentioned.-<lb/> To keep the blood from thickening. take some vinegar and<lb/> salt, let the blood run into it. keep stirring with<lb/> a spoon, else it will run together.-<lb/> NB. Mince a small Onion fine and some parsley add<lb/> to the Giblets they will be very good without the Blood<lb/> <pb/> <head>A fashionable Dish- A Vegitable Pye.</head><lb/> 86/ Boil the following Vegitables.- Peas, Turnips, Carrots,<lb/> Collyflowers, small onions, artichoke Bottoms, Cellery,<lb/> when the above are all separately Boiled, and Cut into<lb/> small bits, and prepared. have ready some very good Gravy<lb/> throw in the Vegitables but let them stew on a slow fire <add>when</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/077|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
49<lb/> when going to be served up, add a little Catchup they<lb/> above may be put into raised <unclear>peast</unclear> or a <unclear>Conceed</unclear> dish,<lb/> to keep warm a little salt and pepper, the Vegitable<lb/> to be Cut fine. but in particulars the Carrot very fine.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Pulled Fowl. Fashionable Dish</head><lb/> 87/ Take any kind of boiled or roast fowl or Chicken<lb/> that has been left, pull off the Skin. Cut the meat into<lb/> long thin slices or pieces, or <unclear>Sluceds</unclear> have ready some<lb/> very good <add>Gravy</add> and a little whole pepper, Grated Nutmeg,<lb/> a bit of butter rolled in flour, a little good cream, little salt<lb/> Grated Lemon, toss all together, to serve it up Garnish<lb/> with Sippets or Lemon. Cold veal is very good. done<lb/> in this manner, the Gravy should be white <unclear>calamid</unclear>.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Eggs, a La Frape</head><lb/> 88/ Make strong Gravy, season high to your taste, boil<lb/> some. Eggs hard, beat them in two, set them on the Dish<lb/> with the white side uppermost, and pour the hot<lb/> Gravy over them, you may ad a very little thickening<lb/> to the Gravy if you choose it but let it be Savory.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

