50<lb/> <head>A White Onion Soup very good M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Richardson</head><lb/> 89/ Three pound of Beef, the like quantity of Mutton, cut into small<lb/> pieces, put down in a stew pan, with Six quarts of water,<lb/> a little allace and Cloves, whole pepper and little salt. let<lb/> this boil on a slow fire, down to three quarts take a Dozen<lb/> large onions, or more as you may occasion, mince them<lb/> small, as for a Duck or Goose take half a pound of<lb/> fresh butter, put it and the onions down on a slow<lb/> fire in a stew pan to stew, also a bit of hung beef<lb/> or ham and a few cloves take care to keep it constantly<lb/> stirring for fear it should burn or grow brown observe<lb/> the onions are to be quite soft, and stewed to a pulp<lb/> and must be white coming out of the butter, pick out<lb/> the Cloves and Beef and skim off the grace very clean<lb/> then add to it the Gravy give it a Boil and when<lb/> going to be served add half a pint of good rich cream<lb/> sippets of white Bread, or toasted, so served up,<lb/> N.B. any strong gravy will answer for this soup,<lb/> but let it be rather white colour.-<lb/> <pb/> Horse
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/079|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
51<lb/> <head>Horse Radish Sauce</head><lb/> <pb/> 90/ Grate the Roots put this into melted Butter, and Gravy<lb/> Season with pepper. salt. a little Lemon juice a little of<lb/> the rind. give all a heat together so cover it over boiled<lb/> goose or Ducks, as you would Onion Sauce.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Sweet Paste</head><lb/> 91/ The yolks of two Eggs. Three spoonfuls of Cream, one<lb/> spoonful of white sugar pounded fine, heat all together<lb/> add as much flour as it will wet, the paste must<lb/> be very little worked, and rolled out very thin.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Baked Rice Pudding</head><lb/> 92/ put down three ounces of pounded rice, mix pint<lb/> of Stippings, stir it on the fire 'till it boils and begins<lb/> to thicken, then take it off, put to it four ounces of butter<lb/> Cover it close when cold add to it the yolks of six<lb/> Eggs the whites of four, six ounces of Sugar. 30-<lb/> little almonds blanched, pounded or cut small, and<lb/> a little Brandy the above quantity makes but a small<lb/> pudding half as much rice makes one a good size<lb/> N:B: Melt the butter to oil it improves the colour add the<lb/> Nutmeg.- Mrs Stewart Grange.<lb/> preserve
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/080|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
52<lb/> <head>Preserve Lemons. Mrs Richardson.</head><lb/> 93/ Take the longest and cleanest coloured Lemons. grate<lb/> them very smooth, rub them with salt for some Minutes<lb/> then throw them into Spring water for four or five days<lb/> changing the water each day. cut a small hole in the top<lb/> take out the pulp. then put them down in Soft water to<lb/> boil until a straw will pierce them. according to the<lb/> quantity get Sugar and water. boil to a thin Syrup,<lb/> put in your Lemons boil them for three quarters of an <lb/> hour then take them off. let them stew in this for two days<lb/> boil them again and add a little more Sug<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. so proceed<lb/> for ten days there are to be two days between each boiling<lb/> the last boil be sure have them to look clear, then<lb/> have ready some rich Syrup, and some scraped Ginger<lb/> take care the Syrup be quite cold before the Lemons go into<lb/> it cover as usual brandy papers + + + +<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lemon Jelly</head><lb/> 94/ A pint of spring water, half a pound of Sugar, boil<lb/> them well, and let them stand until cool, put three large<lb/> Lemons very thin squeeze the juice over the Rind and let it<lb/> infuse, beat the whites of six Eggs, put them by, degrees<lb/> to the Lemons juice, take out the rind run the rest through a Cloth<lb/> set it on a slow fire. stir it until it boils half an ounce of Isinglass<lb/> dissolved makes it of a proper stiffness. put it into your shapes-<lb/> Dutch
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/081|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
53<lb/> <head>Dutch Beef. By the famous <unclear>Mornetts</unclear></head><lb/> <pb/> 95/ Put a round of Beef in Salt for ten days, if large<lb/> it would require more. Steep it in water for one night<lb/> then rub it very well with salt petre. coarse brown sugar<lb/> let it be well rubbed morning and night in the Liquid<lb/> this produces then take half an ounce of Nutmeg. d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.<lb/> of Cloves. D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. of Mae two ounces of all spice, pound<lb/> all these together until fine and rub the beef with the<lb/> spice and Liquor the sugar and salt petre has made,<lb/> lay it bye for two or three days more, still turning it once<lb/> a day in the Liquor and rub it round the sides and all<lb/> over for fear of mould or getting a bad taste. then bake<lb/> Beef in a Crock or pot as may answer best with a<lb/> good quantity of Beef Suet. cut fine at top and bottom,<lb/> be sure tie up your Beef as round and as neat as possible<lb/> before it goes down to bake. cover it very close to keep<lb/> in the Steam, it will take five or six hours to Bake-<lb/> you are <hi rend="underline">not to take the Beef out of the vessel</hi> it was baked<lb/> in until next day preserve the Liquor, it is good in all<lb/> Savory dishes, and you should often wash the Dutch Beef<lb/> with it, to keep from moulding- This Beef will keep for<lb/> two months.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/082|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
54<lb/> <head>A French Soup,- very good M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Donaldson</head><lb/> 96/ Three quarts of good Gravy, take a handful of Lettuce<lb/> cut off the head and tops. <unclear>french sonell</unclear>, Green Cut,<lb/> parsley and <unclear>Chewill</unclear>, as much of each as of the Lettuce. Steam<lb/> them in Clarified Butter well <unclear>Scumed</unclear> when Stewed<lb/> tender. lay them before the fire to drain. take a few young<lb/> Onions cut off the tops and blossoms. do as above lay them<lb/> also before the fire. toast a round of the loaf. pretty brown<lb/> pour some Clarified Butter on the toast, until it is<lb/> <del>little <gap/></del> soaked quite in. put the toast into a little<lb/> warm water until it becomes a pulp, then take the gravy<lb/> herbs, onions + pulp stew all together add a little<lb/> Catsup. you may boil some young peas and through<lb/> into it when going to table a little pepper and salt.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Soup Meagre by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi></head><lb/> 97/ Take Lettuce <unclear>french Sonell</unclear>, green beet Spinnage of<lb/> each an equal quantity pick and cut very small<lb/> slice Onion very small, take of thyme. put all <unclear>angiron</unclear><lb/> parsley and Savory. so much as will be sufficient<lb/> to give a flavor. put all down in your pan with<lb/> a sufficient quantity of fresh butter to stew them. Cut<lb/> half a slice of <del>butter</del> Bread very thin and in small <add>dice</add>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/083|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
55<lb/> dice, stew it with the herbs. take a few small Carrots<lb/> one turnip, slice them very thin, let them lye in water<lb/> whilst the herbs are stewing, pour boiling water on your<lb/> pan. to the quantity of soup you wish for when the<lb/> Pan comes to the Boil put in the roots with a quart of<lb/> green Peas.- which you are to have ready boiled and a<lb/> little before you serve up your soup. heat up the Yolks<lb/> of two Eggs and put to the Soup, and observe to Stir<lb/> one way for fear it would Curdle.<lb/> <pb/> <head>To preserve Mellons. by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi></head><lb/> 98/ Scrape off the rind very nice and carefully lay the<lb/> Mellon in Cold water for 24 hours. make a thin Syrup<lb/> Boil the Mellon in it until tender. then throw it into<lb/> hot water, let it stand all night. next day make<lb/> a rich Syrup, boil and skim it well, put in your<lb/> Peel cut mace and thin some ginger scraped and<lb/> cut thin and the juice of a Lemon, when cold put<lb/> into small pots, and cover with Brandy papers.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

