60<lb/> A green Peas. Soup<lb/> <pb/> 106/ Put down a couple of quarts of old peas. and some<lb/> allspice. and a little whole pepper, pounded, a bunch of<lb/> Mint boil these well. and then strain off the soup. if<lb/> not green enough, or thick enough. bruise the peas with<lb/> the back of your Spoon. in a Collinder and wet it with<lb/> a quart of the Soup so as to get out all the pulp<lb/> you wish for mix a bit of butter in flour add it to<lb/> the soup to present it separating, add some minced parsley<lb/> and Cellery sliced fine. little salt. have ready a quart of<lb/> Gravy, peas ready Boiled. and some very small young<lb/> Onions which are also to be ready boiled. throw into your<lb/> soup, give it a boil so send it up. a bit of ham boiled<lb/> it fast with the soup is a good thing, and then strained off.<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Ragut of Pullets</head><lb/> 107/ Boil the pullets very tender. Cut them in thin slices,<lb/> the the cross very to look like Cocks Combs. take strong<lb/> Gravy brown it. boil in it an onion, little mace,<lb/> a few Cloaves, an anchovy cut small, strain it and<lb/> thicken it with a bit of butter in flour, then put in the<lb/> Pullets give them a boil add a little bit of Bacon cut small. Lemon grated<lb/> and some oysters, ready set a little of their Liquor or Catchup, add force<lb/> Meat balls, so serve it up.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/089|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
61<lb/> <head>Lobster Sauce. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Kelly.</head><lb/> 108/ Pound the Pea. either the inside or seed with a little butter<lb/> pound it very fine, put it into a Stew pan, with some<lb/> good Gravy or Oyster liquor. mix it well together. then<lb/> rub it through a sieve then heat it up in your Stew<lb/> pan again one anchovy and the tail of Lobster<lb/> break into very small shreads. add to this melted butter<lb/> so send it to table.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Oyster Sauces</head><lb/> 109/ Set your Oysters pretty well, in their own Liquor. let<lb/> it then settle and strain it off and strain through an<lb/> old Cloth, so as to be free from grit, season some good<lb/> Gravy with a little mace and nutmeg, give it a scale<lb/> so as to get a flavour of the spices. then strain it. add to <lb/> it a bit of butter rolled in flour. some of the Oyster Liquor<lb/> stir this one way on a slow fire, until it looks smooth<lb/> and thick enough a little Lemon juice and White Wine.<lb/> but not until it is going up, for fear it should Curdle<lb/> put down the oysters again and make them smart<lb/> hot, but must not boil else they would grow hard and<lb/> tough, add two or three spooonfuls of good rich Cream<lb/> stir all one way. put this over <unclear>fazul</unclear>. or in a boat for fish. take Care<lb/> you do not put too much flour else it will be too thick.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/090|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
62<lb/> <head>White Fricacy of Mushrooms</head><lb/> <pb/> 110/ Wash the Mushrooms, cut off the Stalks boil them<lb/> in milk and water until tender, then take as much of<lb/> this liquor as you have occasion for, thicken it with the<lb/> yolk of an Egg, a little sweet cream, that is rich, add<lb/> a little salt. mace, nutmeg, and a Drop of White Wine<lb/> when going up-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Seed Cake, or Ralifea Cake</head><lb/> 111/ Take 12 Eggs, only half of the whites beat them Separately<lb/> take on pound of white sugar pounded very fine. beat<lb/> the yolks until the become cream, add the Sugar by<lb/> degrees, a spoonful at a time. beat these well pound fine<lb/> half a pound of bitter almonds ready blanched, dry three<lb/> quarters of a pound of flour. stir it by degrees into the<lb/> Eggs and Sugar, and also put in a Spoonful of the<lb/> Whites, which you are to have ready beat to a Solid Curd<lb/> turn about the flour and whites of Eggs, lastly put in<lb/> the almonds and a glass of Brandy, add a Nutmeg,<lb/> butter paper and line your pan at bottom and sides<lb/> bake it. it should rise high in the pan.- when<lb/> done the pan will feel light and you may put<lb/> the point of knife into the cake if done enough <lb/> it
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/091|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
63<lb/> it will come out quite clean <hi rend="underline">NB</hi> a Lemon Cake is made<lb/> in this way only to add the rind of Lemon and also the juice<lb/> a Seed Cake made as the above only to add Caraway seeds<lb/> in the place of Lemon this Cake takes on hour to beat it,<lb/> and about three quarters of an hour to bake it.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Hare Pye</head><lb/> 112/ Cut the hare in pieces as for a fricacy season it with<lb/> pepper and salt, a few Cloves and a little mace pounded<lb/> put these into a stew pan close covered to stew on a slow<lb/> fire until tender, else add some thin slices of ham or<lb/> lean Bacon add water, you might put the Hare into<lb/> the oven to bake, if it answered your <unclear>connesuime</unclear> best, in<lb/> a sap dish or Crock and Coarse paste over it, it will<lb/> take two hours to bake it, when done so take out the season?<lb/> Hare, lay it on the Dish you intend for Table. and Cover<lb/> it with good rich paste. add some hard Eggs and more<lb/> Gravy, should it want it. Artichoke Bottoms is also good.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Demontinorge. Receipt for Mutton Chops,</head> <lb/> 113/ Take a Leg of Mutton, cut it into neat chops, take<lb/> some lean veal, the weight of it, in suet, two anchoveys<lb/> some parsley thyme. <sic>Marjorum</sic> <sic>shread</sic> very small and<lb/> mix them well together. season them with salt. pepper.<lb/> nutmeg<del>g</del>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/092|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
64<lb/> nutmeg, grated Bread Lemon rind make up this into force meat<lb/> season your Chops a little run a knife in the best part of<lb/> each Chop and stuff some of the force meat into it.- butter<lb/> papers, and lay a Chop in each half sheet. make it up very<lb/> neat and fold it so. that no Gravy shall get out lay them<lb/> on this and bake in a moderate oven not too hot for fear<lb/> of burning the papers dish and serve it up very warm,<lb/> when the papers are opened, they will be full of Gravy<lb/> which makes them very Savory and good. but should<lb/> they be very dry. a little thin melted butter. garnish<lb/> with Lemon - NB Veal Cutlets will answer in this<lb/> manner rather better then Mutton.- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> OBins<lb/> <pb/> <head>To Sauce any kind of Fish in jelly</head><lb/> 114/ Wash and Scrap the fish very clean and gut them<lb/> make a pickle of white wine water and vinegar<lb/> some whole pepper mace, Lemon peel, and a few<lb/> bay Leaves let this Boil a little and as it boils pop<lb/> in the Fish on a fish Plate with their bellies uppermost<lb/> dip them in two or three times to prevent their breaking<lb/> and then let them continue in the water until<lb/> dry an boiled enough. then put your fish on the<lb/> Dish they are to be served on. take the Liquor the fish<lb/> has boiled in put a couple of Calves fat. boil it <lb/> down
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/093|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
65<lb/> down until it jelly's clear this with the whites of Eggs in <lb/> the usual way run it through a jelly bag and when<lb/> near Cold cover the fish it must be so warm as to <lb/> run equally smooth all over the fish- this is a genteel<lb/> Dish for Balls or great Entertainments and will keep<lb/> along time, it is seldom used but for shew.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lemon or Orange mead - very good, M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Kelly</head><lb/> 115/ To one quart of honey, put four quarts of water, let<lb/> it boil an hour, from its first beginning to boil, it must<lb/> be quite cold before you turn it, to ten Gallons of this<lb/> put the juice of 14 Oranges and the rinds of 7 and one<lb/> quart of Brandy do not close your vessel for one<lb/> month or five weeks, the Lemon mead, is made in <lb/> like manner the older this is the better before you use<lb/> it, one year at best before you open it. observe<lb/> to scum it very clean whilst Boiling.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Harico- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Kelly</head><lb/> 116/ Take a leg or neck of good Mutton, cut it in<lb/> veal Chops. take off the skin. fry it a clear cream but<lb/> take care not to burn it. have ready in your<lb/> Stew pan some very strong good Gravy, boil<lb/> Turnips. Carrots. Cellery. Cut them into small<lb/> Dice
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/094|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
66<lb/> Dice. drain the Chops from Grease, put it into the<lb/> Stew pan of Gravy, add the roots and some Catsup,<lb/> dust in a little flour if not thick enough, <lb/> give all a slow boil. add Mushrooms if you<lb/> have them if not Savory enough, add a little<lb/> pounded <unclear>Cloves</unclear> and Mace Nutmeg, little Salt<lb/> do serve it up. it should not stew too long,<lb/> else it will grow hard and loose the Colour<lb/> <pb/> <head>Pickle Lemons. Marrets</head><lb/> 117/ Score the Lemons but not too deep least the juice should<lb/> come out. pound bay salt fine. rub on the Lemons<lb/> and press it into the nicks. put in the Lemons lay<lb/> the Lemons on a Dish, and put them into a Cool oven<lb/> or before a smart fire to crust and dry. then put<lb/> them into a Crock and pour boiling Vinegar so as<lb/> to cover them. the spices you are to add or as follows<lb/> Mace, Nutmeg, whole white pepper. white mustard seed<lb/> some allspice all but the mustard to be pounded<lb/> very little of this serves for any Savory Dish, or fish<lb/> Sauce add a Teaspoonful of <sic>Cayeinne</sic> pepper, some<lb/> horse Radish sliced. a few pads of <sic>Capsicon</sic>. as the Liquor<lb/> wastes. add more boiling vinegar it will keep a long time the<lb/> spices should be of the white kind-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/095|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
67<lb/> <head>Oranges Preserved. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Waring</head><lb/> <gap/>/ Choose large rough Oranges rasp them with a grater<lb/> very smooth throw them into soft water and good<lb/> quantity of Salt, this takes off the bitter. let them lye<lb/> for two or three days then tie them loose in a Cloth<lb/> bag let them have plenty of room, put them down in<lb/> a large and very clean pot in soft water let them<lb/> boil on a slow fire. until so tender that a straw<lb/> will run through them then take them out. dry these<lb/> cut them in quarters those you intend for such. scrape<lb/> out with a spoon all the pulp, and Strings very clean<lb/> and even. those you choose to keep whole cut a bit out<lb/> of the Stalk and, and with a spoon carefully take<lb/> out all the inside pulp + + lay them a part slice<lb/> some of the oranges a proper thickness carefully prick<lb/> out the pipkins such as are sliced should not be<lb/> rasped as the variety of colours makes the sliced<lb/> Orange much prettier then take the pulp as free from<lb/> pippins as you can. and all the bits of orange<lb/> cut out of the whole Orange pound the latter in<lb/> a Mortar before you add it to the pulp. Boil<lb/> all together on a slow fire with an Equal<lb/> weight of Sugar this Marmilet answers for <lb/> sweet
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/096|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
68<lb/> sweet or for pucchlings then weigh your Oranges and<lb/> allow their weight of Sugar. when the Sugar is broken<lb/> in pieces. give it a dip in Water and lay it in the<lb/> pan to melt. the day before, boil it and Serve it clean<lb/> put in the oranges, let them boil jointly in a quarter of an<lb/> hour, lay them in Basons to Cool do not cover them<lb/> until a day or two, boil them again, and so on for three<lb/> Boilings until the look clear and the Syrup rich and Clear<lb/> also put them in small pots cover with Brandy paper<lb/> and Bladder three of four slices of the sliced Oranges<lb/> makes a pretty supper dish, garnished with some of<lb/> the grated orange and some Syrup.<lb/> You are to allow a pound and a half of Sugar, to<lb/> each pound of Orange.- some allow double weighd.<lb/> <pb/> <head>A plain Flour Pudding</head><lb/> 119/<lb/> Baste the yolks and whites of Eight Eggs well<lb/> Sweeten to your Taste, mix three of four Spoonfuls<lb/> of Flour. some nutmeg a pint of milk, a little<lb/> Brandy tie it up in a thick Cloth and boil it.<lb/> Melted Butter. little Brandy and Sugar for sweet<lb/> and hour boils it.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/097|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
69<lb/> <head>Lady Clanbrissells baked Custard</head><lb/> 120./ Let a Quart of Cream just to the Boil, let it<lb/> then bad, add to it the yolks of 9 Eggs. well beat, a little<lb/> Nutmeg and sugar fill your patty pans with light paste,<lb/> put them into the oven. but observe put proper filled with<lb/> bran to prevent their blistering or having up too much when<lb/> they begin to colour take out the papers and bran, and<lb/> fill the custard as fast as you can into the paste. let<lb/> them stand in the oven until the Colour all over<lb/> the Cream and Eggs must be strained after they are mixed<lb/> and made just warm in a sauce pan stirring all the time<lb/> till put in the paste-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Potted Hare- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Waring.</head><lb/> 121/ Cut the Hare in pieces season with pepper and salt, let<lb/> it lye 24 hours put them into a Crock with a good quantity<lb/> of Beef Suet cut small Bake it well. pour off all the <lb/> gravy, and pound it in a wooden Bowl. and take out<lb/> all strings and hard bits and keep the fat and Gravy you<lb/> drain off, warm and add it to the meat as you pound it<lb/> when pounded short and fine. put it into small pots<lb/> and press it down hard. when cold. cover as usual with<lb/> Butter melted to oil.-<lb/> N:B: a Beef heart prepared as above eats just as well as hare.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/098|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
70<lb/> <head>Minced Pies - very good. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Kelly</head><lb/> <pb/> 122/ One pound of pippins sliced small, half a pound<lb/> of Beef Suet, minced, one pound of Currants half a pound<lb/> of Raisins stoned and Cut small a quarter of a Pound<lb/> of Sugar pounded, a quarter of a pound of Sweet almonds<lb/> blanched and cut length ways. the rind of three Lemons<lb/> juice of three, little Salt, mince Nutmeg, <sic>Cinamon</sic> a few<lb/> cloves all pounded fine in a mortar, a little port and<lb/> a good deal of Brandy. mix all well together. this<lb/> will keep a long time in a well glazed Crock close<lb/> covered from the air. when you make your Stock<lb/> for winter. you may just double this quantity, it keeps<lb/> three months, by adding a little fresh brandy, when making<lb/> of your pyes add Candid Orange and Citron cut this<lb/> and long and laid on the top your Paste to be very <lb/> rich, and at each set you make add a little fresh<lb/> Brandy half an hour bakes them. for this quantity or<lb/> quarter of an Oz of each spice will answer.<lb/> <pb/> <unclear>Parapikelits</unclear>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/099|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
71<lb/> <head><unclear>Paropikelits</unclear>. by same.</head><lb/> 125/ To six quarts of fine Flour, take six Eggs well beat<lb/> and three Spoonfuls of fresh Barm. three pints of new Milk<lb/> almost boiling mix all together stir you flour with<lb/> a spoon. until all be well mixed. then then work it<lb/> well with your hand for a quarter of an hour, cover<lb/> it with a clean Towel and set it before the fire<lb/> to rise for half an hour, then roll it out in what<lb/> size you like. bake it in a slow oven 'tis best.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Pickle for keeping Cold Salmon.</head><lb/> 124/ put down salt and water. some <sic>fennell</sic> and<lb/> a little allspice some vinegar boil it. let it cool. next<lb/> day pour it over your cold Salmon it will keep<lb/> a long time thus.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To Keep French Beans all the Year.</head><lb/> 125/ Pull the Beans in a dry warm day. let them be pretty<lb/> large. cut them in the usual manner as for table, get a well<lb/> glazed Crock narrow at the mouth lay a layer of salt, then of<lb/> Beans, so on 'til you fill the <sic>vessell</sic> so cover it, and keep it in<lb/> dry place. when you want to use the Beans. the night before<lb/> put them into Spring water. and change into a third water the next<lb/> day. before you boil them as would any other green <sic>vegitable</sic><lb/> hard water + + -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/100|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
72/<lb/> <head>Eggs a La Trappe</head><lb/> <pb/> 126/ Make strong Gravy very high, boil your<lb/> Eggs hard as for Salad, cut them in two. serve<lb/> them up with the white uppermost, a supper dish<lb/> a little flour to thicken, add some Catsup or any Sauce<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lambs puddings. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Kelly</head><lb/> 127/<lb/> Stir the Blood as it comes from the Lamb<lb/> and throw in some salt. mix it well with<lb/> your hand break all the Lumps and strain<lb/> it through a <sic>Calunder rince</sic> the Strings and<lb/> what remains with the water or milk: <lb/> stir in some flour. add to it some sweet milk<lb/> Grate plenty of Sliced suet. some mint, put <unclear>Marjornne</unclear><lb/> Allspice, Onions. <unclear>winda</unclear> Savory. pepper and Salt. Chop<lb/> all very fine scald your bag very well and Clean, put<lb/> in the above mixture. sew it close up. put into a pot<lb/> of Boiling water let it boil very Slow, after it goes in<lb/> + turn it after to prevent <unclear>Sculing</unclear> when some time boiling<lb/> take it up and run a pin into it here and there to prevent<lb/> it bursting. which Lambs puddings are apt to do.-<lb/> A sheep may be made in like manner- lay this pudding<lb/> by and use them as you may have occasion.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/101|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
73<lb/> <head>Eels Savory. by same</head><lb/> <pb/> 128/ Skin the Eels, gut them and wash in different waters<lb/> and salt in it, then cut them in small pieces an Inch<lb/> and half long, put them down to boil on a slow<lb/> fire when done take them out of the water they were<lb/> boiled in. which will be strong and greasy. have<lb/> ready a little good gravy a little mace some minced<lb/> parsley a little pepper salt nutmeg, a little <sic>Seves</sic> give<lb/> the Eels a small heat. add a bit of butter rolled in<lb/> flour to thicken the Sauce, you may add any Savory<lb/> herb to them. but do not put in the parsley too<lb/> soon else it will loose its colour. this is a good<lb/> method of Dressing Eels.<lb/> <pb/> <head>To preserve Currants whole</head><lb/> 129/ Two pounds of Lump Sugar, put to it as much Currant<lb/> Juice as will melt it. set it on a slow fire. and let it<lb/> boil a little time. then put into it, two pounds of pickled<lb/> Currants. let them boil until the Jelly looks clear so<lb/> put them into aired pots and Cover as usual with<lb/> Brandy papers in a day or two.-<lb/> <pb/> To
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/102|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
74<lb/> <head>To Pickle Sturgeon.</head><lb/> <pb/> 130/ To one quart of Vinegar add two of water. and<lb/> so on in proportion to a less or greater quantity of fish,<lb/> add Mace. whole pepper, allspice, little Cloves, some bay<lb/> leaves, cut the Sturgeon into pieces such as you chose,<lb/> bind each piece with Tape, put all down to boil. when<lb/> enough done take it out of the pickle to Cool. and<lb/> when both are Cold Crock it and Cover it over-<lb/> <pb/> <head>For Ditto by Momett</head><lb/> 131/ Sprinkle your Sturgeon with Salt and let<lb/> lie by twenty four hours, then boil it thoroughly, let it<lb/> then drain on a Cloth 24 hours, keep it in a Sauce<lb/> of and water some vinegar and whole pepper.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Macaroon Cakes.- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Donadlson</head><lb/> 132/ 4 Ounces of Almonds pounded fine. 3 Ounces of<lb/> Sugar pounded and sifted. the whites of four Eggs<lb/> beat to a Curd. one spoonful of Brandy. mix all<lb/> well together and drop the size of a large Nutmeg<lb/> on papers which must be dusted with Sugar and bake<lb/> them on a Slow oven some bitter almonds among them<lb/> to flavor.<lb/><pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/103|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
75<lb/> <head>Marrow. Pasty's</head><lb/> 133/ Sliced a pound of Marrow, which should be<lb/> rendered and strained for fear of Splinters. Cut small<lb/> a pound of apples, add some pounded mace, and<lb/> Cinamon. half a pound of Sugar nutmeg + Brandy<lb/> one pound of Currants washed and picked clean, Mix<lb/> all together. have ready rich puff paste, which you are<lb/> to fill with the minced meat and make into the <lb/> shape of Pasties so bake them.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Epinards alo- Cream.</head><lb/> 134/ Take Spinage boil it well. drain it off from<lb/> the water it was boiled in, put it into Cold water<lb/> strain it from that also. squeeze the Spinage very<lb/> dry between your hands and then Chop it fine. put<lb/> it down in a stew pan with a little bit of fresh<lb/> butter, an Onion, stuck with Cloves and little pepper<lb/> and slat. stew it for some time with the butter which<lb/> you must keep turning, then shake in a little flour<lb/> let it stew a little longer. then add half a pint<lb/> of Cream if your dish will hold so much, it <lb/> must stew until the cream and spinage do not<lb/> Separate. cut small sipets 3 Comer ways fry<lb/> them a light brown and stick up in the spinage<lb/> so serve it up.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/104|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
76<lb/> <head>Stewed Eels- Major Lahunt.</head><lb/> 135/ Skin the eels, wipe them dry, fry them brown in<lb/> clarified butter. take them out and drain them before<lb/> the fire, take a little of this butter, thicken it with<lb/> flour add some very strong gravy (Savory or<lb/> good Catsup) little pounded Cloves Mace. Nutmeg<lb/> Salt, Lemon juice anchovy bruised fine. Claret<lb/> or port put in the Eels give a good heat and so<lb/> serve them up. this receipt was got from a famous<lb/> cook in London who dress this dish remarkably well<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lemon Cream.</head><lb/> 136/ Pare two Lemons very thin, pour on it a pint of<lb/> Boiling water let it stand close covered, until<lb/> quite cold then heat the whites of four Eggs. but<lb/> not to a froth and strain on to the water and Lemon<lb/> also strain as much juice of Lemon as will make<lb/> it very tart. then add Sugar, to make very short,<lb/> mix them well together and season on a slow fire,<lb/> and observe to stir one way, until it grows thick<lb/> and then fill your glasses.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/105|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
77<lb/> <head>To preserve Plumbs of any kind.</head><lb/> <pb/> 137/ Weigh your Plumbs and allow an Equal weight<lb/> of Sugar pounded fine, pare your plumbs and lay<lb/> them on a large dish that is Deep, Strew the Sugar<lb/> over them as you <del>please</del><add>hare</add>, then to keep them to a colour<lb/> when all are done, let them stand until the Sugar.<lb/> is in Syrup about them, put them in a preserving pan<lb/> over a slow fire and let them gently boil for a little<lb/> time. take them off and sit them by until next day<lb/> put a sheet of paper over them to keep them under<lb/> the Syrup. then boil them very slow until they<lb/> look clear put them into well oiled pots as usual<lb/> Brandy papers and Bladders as usual.-<lb/> Some throw the Plumbs into cold water as you<lb/> pare them, rather than Sugar. but must not lie in<lb/> it log for fear of loosing the colour. at same time<lb/> have the Syrup preparing on the fire, as you take out<lb/> the Plumbs dry them very carefully and put them<lb/> into the Syrup and give a slow boil rich on the<lb/> marked side.-<lb/> <pb/> Preserve
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/106|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
78<lb/> <head>Preserve Peaches</head><lb/> <pb/> 138/ Scald the peaches in in scalding water until the<lb/> skin carries easily off them, boil their weight in Sugar<lb/> to a rich Syrup, put in the Peaches. have Sugar ready<lb/> pounded to throw over the fruit as you pour them.<lb/> boil very slow until clear and tender. keep them<lb/> covered all the time. when done put them up as usual<lb/> <pb/> <head>Plumb Pudding- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Kelly</head><lb/> <pb/> 139/ Half a pound of suet cut large, the like quantity <lb/> of grated Bread, d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi> of Currants d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi> of raisins stoned<lb/> and cut small, a nutmegg. little salt. good handful 1/4<gap/><lb/> <add>1/4 b</add> <del>of</del> Sugar. a little flour. four Eggs beat with the flour<lb/> a glass of Brandy mix all well together tie close on<lb/> a thick Towell. one hour and half boils it. sauce<lb/> method Butter and Brandy.- 5 Tablespoonfulls Flour<lb/> 1/2 of Citron <add>Ginger</add> 1/2 of Orange Peel candid- Tiny the Cloth out of Cold water + flour<lb/> a little milk it will<lb/> put down at 3 of. for<gap/><lb/> <pb/> <head>Jelly T</head><lb/> 140./ Take the pith of a light roll slice thin boil<lb/> a pint of sweet milk on Strippings pour on the Bread<lb/> as much as will wet it. blanch two ounces of<lb/> almonds with orange flour water six Eggs,<lb/> half
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/107|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Glass of Whiskey<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Plum Pudding</hi><lb/> 1/2 lb <unclear>Oncca</unclear> Raisins picked =<lb/> rubbed in cloth<lb/> 1/2 lb Currants washed in Cullder<lb/> + dried <unclear>sped</unclear>.<lb/> Suet chopped fine<lb/> Sugar 1/4 lb<lb/> Bread Crumbs<lb/> Flour on 5 Table spoonfuls<lb/> Tea spoonful Salt. half as<lb/> much ground ginger d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi> nutmeg<lb/> 1/4 <hi rend="superscript">lb</hi>. (or ounce) Citron mixed -<lb/> 4 Eggs beaten. mix with them<lb/> <unclear>the</unclear> Flour + enough milk<lb/> to make it a proper.<lb/> Consistency. rather stiff or<lb/> the fruit will. slink-<lb/> <gap/>ing the Pudding Cloth out of<lb/> Cold water, Flour well. Tie<lb/> the pudding tightly, leaving room<lb/> or swelling. boil 3 hours <unclear>puttey</unclear> down<lb/> in boiling water
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

