'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/109|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
79<lb/> half the whites, half a pint of cream, make it pretty<lb/> sweet mix all together. colour it with the juice of<lb/> <del>Lemons</del> Spinage. but pull of the Stalks and wipe<lb/> it dry before you pound it, butter your dish and<lb/> Bake in a slow oven, if you choose it to eat like<lb/> Tavos pound a sprig of it with the Spinage-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Mushrooms pickled white,</head><lb/> 141/ Rub the Mushrooms very clean with a piece of flannell<lb/> throw them into Spring water and very little milk<lb/> when all are cleaned put them down in a bell kettle<lb/> skillet, on a slow fire in milk and water and<lb/> a little salt. when they get a good boil take them<lb/> off lay them on a Sieve to drain. and then put them<lb/> on a piece of Flannel to dry quite, when cold put<lb/> them into wide mouthed Bottles. add best white wine<lb/> Vinegar, whole white pepper, nutmeg a good deal of mace<lb/> some ginger bruised cover the butter. and keep bladders<lb/> over them and put them in a dry place.-<lb/> <pb/> Mushrooms
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/110|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
80<lb/> <head>Mushrooms Fricasied white - M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Trevion</unclear></head><lb/> <pb/> 142/ Wash the Mushrooms, cut off the Stalks. boil them<lb/> in milk and water. little salt, until they are tender<lb/> take as much of this Liquor as will answer for sauce<lb/> thicken it with the yolks of two Eggs. well beat,<lb/> a little sweet cream a little mace. nutmeg and a little<lb/> white wine to serve it up.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Harico.</head><lb/> 143/ Take a Loyn of Mutton cut into neat chops not<lb/> too thick. cut off the Skin beat the chops with the rolling<lb/> pin fry them on a quick fire a light brown, put them<lb/> into a tacks pan. which must have very strong rich<lb/> Gravy. add the following Roots which must be boiled<lb/> ready and Cut into small dice. Carrots Turnips,<lb/> little Cellery you may dust in a little flour, but take<lb/> care the sauce be not too thick. season with a<lb/> little mace, nutmeg. Cloves, pounded together little salt<lb/> little grated Lemon Mushrooms if you have it. Artichoke<lb/> bottoms boiled and cut in small bits should you<lb/> like it, give all a small boil to send it up. if you<lb/> have Catsup throw in a little.-<lb/> <pb/> Potatoe
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/111|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
81<lb/> <head>Potatoe Pudding. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Waring.</head><lb/> <pb/> 144/ Half a pound of good Potatoes. when boiled and<lb/> skinned, pounded fine in a mortar beat half a pound<lb/> of fresh butter with your hand, as for a plumb Cake<lb/> the Yolks of Eight Eggs, beat very well with half a<lb/> pound of Sugar pounded mix all well, add Nutmeg<lb/> and Brandy, three quarters of an hour bakes it. a rich<lb/> <sic>Poast</sic> at bottom and sides of the dish.-<lb/> <pb/> N:B: you may make a Carrot or Potatoe pudding<lb/> in like manner as M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Irving Ree<hi rend="superscript">t</hi> for an Orange Pudding<lb/> only observe the orange to be left out, for the Carrot<lb/> pound you one to boil the Currants very tender. and<lb/> not to put any of the inside cow in then, these puddings<lb/> are to be made just as the Orange and will take<lb/> the some Baking.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Tongues Pickled</head><lb/> 145/ Take the best tongues you can get. cut out the bones<lb/> salt them with Salt petre hot salt and brown Sugar,<lb/> the latter makes them cut short, pack them close in a crock<lb/> threw all the <unclear>Scallered</unclear> salt over them and cover close, those<lb/> you intend for hanging take out at three weeks end. and hang up<lb/> by the root you may add a little bay salt if you can get it<lb/> to the hot salt if not. the hot salt will do-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/112|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
82<lb/> <head>Raspberry Jam.</head><lb/> <pb/> 146/ Pull your Raspberries in a dry day allow an<lb/> equal weight of Sugar. dip your Sugar in water<lb/> and Set it by to melt next day put all down in<lb/> your preserving pan or a clear but slow fire, bruise<lb/> them as little as you can, as they look better to be<lb/> whole. and too many seeds spoils the look. scum off<lb/> any dirt. that may arise to every pound of Raspberry<lb/> allow one pint of Currant juice red and white <lb/> mixed, answers best and to each pint of juice allow<lb/> one pound of Sugar boil all together for some time<lb/> take it off and boil it in a day or two often<lb/> until it looks clear and Jellys on the Spoon,<lb/> put it in to small pots and cover as usual<lb/> Brandy papers.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Scarlet Gooseberries.</head><lb/> 147/ Take the Gooseberries when full ripe, pick them<lb/> from Stalks, + + to each pound of fruit allow<lb/> one pound and half of Sugar. allow half a pint<lb/> of Currant juice put the sugar to the juice to melt<lb/> give it a boil. scum it clean. then put in the gooseberries<lb/> and let them boil quick. until they look clear and<lb/> begin to jelly so put them up as usual.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/113|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
83<lb/> <head>Venision Pasty made of Mutton.</head><lb/> <pb/> 148/ Choose a fat Shoulder of Mutton. bone it, but do not<lb/> cut the Skin on the outside season it with pepper<lb/> salt and pounded cloves. put it in a pan Crock with<lb/> the cut side down. but rub the outside also with the spices<lb/> from Celaret over it and let it lie by for two days,<lb/> then put it down in a port with the outside<lb/> uppermost make it as round in the pot as you can,<lb/> add a little more Claret and cover it very close,<lb/> so that the Steam may get out with coarse paste<lb/> and Stew it over a slow fire or in an oven for three<lb/> hours. before you cover the paste add plenty of beef<lb/> Suet on the top of the meat crock the bones. and let<lb/> them stew with the meat also, when stewed enough set<lb/> it by to settle, then put it into the Dish you mean<lb/> to scum it it, should it want any more spice or gravy<lb/> add it, then cover the pye with a rich paste. the<lb/> baking of the paste, will do the meat enough.-<lb/> <pb/> Plumb
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/114|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
84<lb/> <head>Plumb Dumplings very good.</head><lb/> <pb/> 149/ Half a pound of raisins stoned and Cut small,<lb/> six ounces of suet cut large, two spoonfuls of Flour<lb/> two of Sugar. two of cream. two of brandy two yolks<lb/> of Eggs half a nutmeg. little salt mix all well<lb/> together tie in one or two Dumplings some Brandy<lb/> Sugar butter.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Half Cake. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Clewlaw.</head><lb/> 150/ 2 lb of Flour 1/2 lb Currants 1/2 lb Sugar 1/2 do Butter<lb/> melted in a pint of new milk not quite 1/2 a pint<lb/> of yeast the yolks of 4 Eggs beat up and mixed<lb/> with the Bann.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Ginger Wine, very good M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Radeliff.</head><lb/> 154/ To ten Gallons of water, put ten pounds of Lump<lb/> Sugar and three ounces of ginger sliced. boil it one<lb/> hour. take off the Scum as it rises then empty it into<lb/> a tub let it stand until cold then put it into the<lb/> vessels you intend it for, with the Peel and juice of<lb/> Twelve Lemons and three pints of Brandy put half<lb/> a spoonful of Yeast on the top, and stop it close in a<lb/> fortnight it will be fit to bottle, in a fortnight more it<lb/> will be fit to drink the Lemons are to be peeled very thin and the<lb/> juice strained.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/115|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
85<lb/> <head>Tallelon. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Cope.</head><lb/> 152/ To 2 pound of flour, rub in a quarter of a pound of<lb/> Butter then wet it with a pint of milk (cream) made<lb/> cream, three spoonfuls of fresh bann, and the yolks of<lb/> Six Eggs well beat, mix all together as you would any<lb/> other Bread set it before the fire to rise a cloth<lb/> Covering <del>of</del> it, make it up into a Cake, and bake it<lb/> in a quick oven on a tin plate add a little <del><gap/></del><add>salt</add><lb/> to the Dough.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Savory Patty's easily made.</head><lb/> 153/ Mince fine any cold fowl or veal. two ounces of fat<lb/> Bacon, the like quantity of Beef suet boil these for<lb/> half an hour. then choped fine mix it with the<lb/> mince meat season this with grated Lemon nutmeg<lb/> parsley finely minced salt pepper juice of Lemon<lb/> Two spoonfuls of cream put this into patty pans<lb/> with rich paste under them.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To preserve Cherries.</head><lb/> 154/ Cut the stalks of the Cherries of a right length<lb/> weigh them and allow them an equal weight of <lb/> Sugar dip the Sugar in water. when melted<lb/> Set it on the fire. boil it to rich Syrup. Scum<lb/> it
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/116|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
86<lb/> it clean stone the Cherries and when the Syrup<lb/> is cold put in the Cherries. set them on the fire<lb/> give them a boil take them off put over them a sheet<lb/> of paper to keep them tender the Syrup let them stand<lb/> a little so put them on again and in this manner mem<gap/><lb/> them, until the fruit looks clear and tender boiling very<lb/> slow for fear of bursting the fruit lift them up one<lb/> by one lay them in well lined pots have ready some<lb/> Currant juice. to each pound of Sugar allow half<lb/> a pint of Currant juice. also allow to each pint of<lb/> Juice one pound of Sugar. when this is melted give<lb/> all the Syrup and Juice of Currants a boil<lb/> until they become a light jelly pour this over the<lb/> Cherries. papers as usual keep them in a dry place<lb/> <pb/> <head>Carrots or Potatoe pudding.</head><lb/> 155/ May be made according to the Receipt in this<lb/> book. for an Orange pudding only observe the orange<lb/> must be left out, half a pound of potatoes or Currants<lb/> to be boiled and pounded they must be boiled tender<lb/> the inside case of the Carrot is not to be put in<lb/> you are in all respects to do as directed in the<lb/> Orange pudding. brandy nutmeg + + and the<lb/> same time to bake it.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/117|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
87<lb/> <head>Lemon pudding- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Jervase</head><lb/> <pb/> 156/ Grate the rind of three Lemons into half a pound<lb/> of pounded Sugar, add three ounces of melted Butter<lb/> heat the yolks of Six Eggs very well. the Juice of the<lb/> Lemons two ounces of Sweet almonds half a pound of<lb/> bitter blanch and pounded. little Brandy nutmeg mix<lb/> all the ingredients together put a rich paste at bottom<lb/> and sides - three quarters of an hour bakes it.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A <unclear>fricassel</unclear> of Oysters.</head><lb/> 157/ Beard the Oysters wash them in their own Liquor<lb/> Strain that very free from grit, put it down on the <lb/> fire, give the oysters a scald in their Liquor, season <lb/> this with a little mace have ready two yolks of<lb/> Eggs well beat add a little good rich cream to the<lb/> Eggs just before you dish put them to the Oysters a smart<lb/> heat will do, take great care for fear the Eggs Curdles<lb/> a little nutmeg, you may put in a drop of white wine<lb/> if you choose when dishing.-<lb/> <pb/> A Maccaroni
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/118|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
88<lb/> <head>Macarony Miss Boileau.</head><lb/> <pb/> 158/ Boil a quarter of a pound of Macaroni until tender<lb/> drain if from the water. take half a pint of strong good<lb/> Gravy a little fresh butter thrown into it to melt, have ready<lb/> some fine grated bread, also some Gloucester Cheese grated<lb/> put the Macaroni on a Slow fire with the Gravy to<lb/> Stew, and Swell, then butter a proper dish. lay on some<lb/> Crumbs of Bread. add the Cheese and then lay a layer<lb/> of Macaroni. continue so until your dish be filled as<lb/> you Chose cover it with crumbs of Bread and<lb/> cheese set it on a slow fire or an oven to brown<lb/> if not an oven use a Salamander, it must be brown at<lb/> top if done proper.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Quince Marmalet,</head><lb/> 159/ Pare the quince and put them down in a large<lb/> Vessel to boil. when very tender take them off and pulp<lb/> them in a Dish. cut the cones and Kernells into small<lb/> bits. put them back into the water the quince were boiled<lb/> in, until the water be consumed to one quart or less<lb/> according to the quantity you do, then stain it. and<lb/> to every pound of quince allow one pound of sugar<lb/> and the like to every pint of juice put all down in y<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. preserving <lb/> Pan + boil them very well they take vast boiling a little of this is excellent in<lb/> apple Pyes. or on Sauces for Sweet meats.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/119|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
89<lb/> <head>To fry Parsley remarkably pretty.</head><lb/> <pb/> 160/ pick the parsley from the Stalks lay it on a tin sheet<lb/> before the fire which must be smart. or in an oven rather<lb/> cool, when very crisp and without <unclear>wateting</unclear>, baste it with<lb/> a little Butter. this may be laid by. until you are ready to<lb/> Garnish, then hold it to the fire for a minute or two it<lb/> will be of a beautiful green so cover your Dish. this<lb/> is a better Colour than by frying of it.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Stuffing for Veal or Turkey</head><lb/> 161/ Suet, grated bread, an equal quantity, little salt,<lb/> pepper. Nutmeg. Lemon rind little onion minced very<lb/> small, wet these with the yolk of an Egg or a<lb/> little cream, some minced parsley and a little Lemon<lb/> thyme is very good in it.-<lb/> This stuffing answers also for Hare, you are to make<lb/> the inside very dry before you put in the stuffing<lb/> and you may put that in without any sort,<lb/> wetting should you choose it. some think it right<lb/> to do so.-<lb/> <pb/> Oysters
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/120|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
90<lb/> <head>Oysters in Shells</head><lb/> <pb/> 162/ Set the oysters in their oven Liquor, be <unclear>sine</unclear> wash<lb/> them very clean from Grit in their own Liquor, butter<lb/> Shells or a small dish lay some Crumbs of bread<lb/> then lay on some Oysters. same Grated Nutmeg and<lb/> bits of butter, continue this until your Dish be<lb/> properly filed so take them, let them be brown at<lb/> top, a little of their own liquor so as to moisten the<lb/> Bread is not amiss.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To pot white meat.</head><lb/> 163/ Take any sort of white meat that has been dressed<lb/> pound it. and season it. Salt. pepper and mace. boil<lb/> a red Tongue pound it very well, lay a layer of the<lb/> pounded meat then of Tongue and so on until you<lb/> have filled your pots. press down very hard, pour melted<lb/> butter as usual over this, it will Keep a long time, when<lb/> you use it, cut it out in slices, and lay it on your<lb/> dish for Supper.-<lb/> <pb/> Pickle
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/121|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
91<lb/> <head>Pickle Salmon. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Stewart.</head><lb/> <pb/> 164/ Cut the fish in small round pieces, wash it well<lb/> and dry it with a Cloth, make a pickle strong enough<lb/> to bear an Egg, boil and skin it well. then put<lb/> in the Salmon, and boil it until done sufficient<lb/> when near Cold stick six or Eight Cloves in<lb/> each piece to twelve pounds of Salmon. allow<lb/> half an ounce of black pepper the same of allspice<lb/> d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. mace, d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. Nutmeg well pounded season each piece<lb/> of fish with the pounded spice. and pack it in a<lb/> close Crock when the pickle it was boiled in is<lb/> Cold put to each pint of it. three pints of vinegar, and<lb/> one pint of white wine it must be covered with<lb/> the Pickle made in this manner. keep it in a dry<lb/> cool place.- Take care to skin off the oil as it<lb/> arises,- on the Cold Salmon.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Strawberries preserved very good. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Donaldson</head><lb/> 165/ pull the Strawberries in a dry warm Day. take care not<lb/> to bruise them, pick off the blossoms and Stalks, weigh<lb/> them, allow an equal weight of Sugar, break the<lb/> Sugar in small pieces, dip it in boiling water and let<lb/> it dissolve, then set it on the fire to boil, skin it clean<lb/> take
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/122|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
92<lb/> Take it off the fire. let it cool. pretty well put in the<lb/> fruit. Cover them with a sheet of paper to keep them<lb/> Under the Syrup, put them on the fire let them get a slow<lb/> Boil. then take them off, let them stand a while, put them<lb/> on again. repeat this until the fruit looks clear. to every<lb/> pound of Strawberries allow a pint of red Currant juice<lb/> to each pint of juice allow one pound of Sugar boil, <lb/> this and scum it clean and strain if the Syrup pan<lb/> the Strawberries into the Currant juice give all a boil<lb/> before you throw in the fruit give that a boil also-<lb/> but very slow for fear of bursting the fruit, have ready<lb/> well and pots to put it in, the smaller the better as<lb/> they loose the colour when opened, as usual after two<lb/> days paper, with Brandy papers and Bladders, observe<lb/> the Currant juice seldom answers the Strawberries at first<lb/> but when you do get the currant juice <unclear>be sure</unclear> give the<lb/> Strawberries a Gentle Boil in it. keep them in a dry<lb/> place. N:B: The Indian Straberries answer best<lb/> and have the highest flavor.<lb/> <pb/> To pot.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/123|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
93<lb/> <head>To pot Herrings</head><lb/> <pb/> 166/ Gut scale and wash the Herrings very clean,<lb/> cut off the heads and tails. dry them, and season them<lb/> with pepper and salt, lay them in a flat bottomed Crock<lb/> a tin patty. between each layer of herrings. put a few<lb/> bay leaves and some sliced onions, when the number<lb/> you wish to have is completed fill up the vessel<lb/> with small beer and vinegar an equal quantity<lb/> or some Cider will do as well. cover the vessel with<lb/> a Coarse paste of Meat; and bake it in an Oven<lb/> four or five hours, when done the back bone will cut<lb/> short and Crisp.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lemon Flumery</head><lb/> 167/ Two ounces of Isinglass dissolved in a pint of<lb/> water, when dissolved strain it. add to it a pint of<lb/> white wine. the juice of two Lemons also the rinds<lb/> a little Cinnamon, Eight Yolks of eggs well beat.<lb/> Sweeten to your taste. Strain it thicken it over the fire<lb/> a little and then put it into Cups.- when Cold turn<lb/> it out. stick it with blanched almonds cut small + lay<lb/><pb/> Mutton
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/124|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
94<lb/> <head>Mutton Hash as dressed at Bath</head><lb/> <pb/> 168/ Roast a should of Mutton until near done. but<lb/> cut the blade bone very neat. Score it and season it<lb/> with pepper and Salt. Slice the rest of the meat thin<lb/> and neat, put it down in a Stew pan, with some<lb/> good Gravy. two or three Shallots minced very small<lb/> a Cucumber minced small. little salt and pepper, let it<lb/> get a smart heat, but not to boil. you may dust in<lb/> a little flour when going up a drop of <sic>Catchsup</sic>, broil<lb/> the blade bone. on a clear fire free from <sic>Smoak</sic> lay it<lb/> on the top of the hash. Garnish with sippets of bread<lb/> or Lemon. you may grate a little Lemon Rind and the<lb/> least pinch of <sic>Cayinne</sic> pepper, any hashed meat by boiling<lb/> grows hard, you may add any Savory thing to this<lb/> such as Mushrooms.- +c +c +c<lb/> <pb/> <head>To Stew Tench - Lady Longford</head><lb/> 169/ <unclear>saue</unclear> the blood, wash and scale the fish let them lye<lb/> by in Salt and water put as much spring water<lb/> as will cover your fish with half a pint of vinegar<lb/> into your stew pan add a little salt stew this<lb/> on a slow fire one hour then take a pint of strong<lb/> gravy on Onion minced very fine two or three<lb/> anchovys
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/125|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
95<lb/> Anchoveys minced fine, a little mace pounded fine, half<lb/> a pound of fresh butter bruised in flour. add the blood<lb/> and thicken the sauce as you choose with flour.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To Stew Tench- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Obins.</head><lb/> 170/ Rub your Tench with salt, cut this throats, let them<lb/> let them blood into a vessel with Salt and Vinegar<lb/> you are to stir the blood as it drops into the above<lb/> and it will not turn into Lumps.- Wash them<lb/> very well out and inside, rub them down in a stew pan<lb/> cover them with Claret add a little pounded Mace<lb/> cloves, salt a Dust of pepper, an anchovy when one<lb/> side is done turn the other, when done enough take<lb/> <unclear>turn</unclear> out, add to the Liquor they <unclear>wen</unclear> Stewed in, the<lb/> White wine and blood, add a little fine grated bread,<lb/> Grate Lemmon Mushrooms a little Catchsup and<lb/> more spices should it require it. put in the fish, let<lb/> it stew a little more. to serve it up. garnish with<lb/> Lemon-<lb/> <pb/> Stew
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/126|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
96/<lb/> <head>Stew Cucumbers to eat with Roast Mutton or Beef</head><lb/> <pb/> 171/ Peel and slie thick fine or six large Cucumbers, and<lb/> as many Onions shake salt on them, lay them on a<lb/> Cloth to drain all the water out of them, dust flour<lb/> on them. stew them in good gravy with some whole white<lb/> pepper, little salt. and any other spices you may choose,<lb/> when tender toss them up with a bit of flour and butter<lb/> blended, add a little Catsup.-<lb/> You may fry in fresh butter the Cucumber and Onions<lb/> dredging in flour so as to make them brown. but take<lb/> care they do not burn, and then put them to the gravy<lb/> and spices to stew, a little drop of Vinegar also<lb/> <pb/> <head>To keep Codlings all the Year.-</head><lb/> 172/ Take any quantity of Codlings you choose, put apple<lb/> halves at top and bottom of the pan. under and over every<lb/> layers of Codlin. cover them with water put them on a Stew<lb/> hole on a slow fire, until they change colour but not<lb/> so as to grow soft. take them out of the water. spread<lb/> them on a Table to cool. strain off the apple Leaves all<lb/> the Liquor and when the fruit and liquor are both cold<lb/> put the fruit into a narrow necked crock, and cover them with<lb/> the liquor. and then cover that with sheep suet melted,<lb/> cover all very close and keep in a dry cool place.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/127|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
97<lb/> <head>Oysters Pickled.</head><lb/> <pb/> 173/ Take half an hundred of good large Oysters, wash them<lb/> very well in their own liquor put them in a toss pan. strain<lb/> their liquor through a thin cloth. over the oysters very free<lb/> from Grit or <unclear>Sechinent</unclear> give them a smart scald so as to <lb/> Plump them, but take care for fear they should get to<lb/> hard, then take them out and add to the liquor they<lb/> <unclear>wine</unclear> scalded in half a pint of good strong white wine<lb/> Sherry answers very well, as much vinegar, mace nutmeg<lb/> whole white pepper, half an ounce of each, little salt. give<lb/> this a boil, put your Oysters in a Pickling pot and<lb/> pour the boiling liquor over them cover the crock, or<lb/> pot. and when cold cover it. with bladders +c +c +c<lb/> <pb/> <head>Fish Sauce for present use.</head><lb/> 174/ Take a little good Gravy, an Anchovey choped very fine,<lb/> some Oyster Liquor, a little Catchsup, a pit of mace, a<lb/> large Onion, a little Lemon rind, a little white wine, two<lb/> or three Cloves. give all a boil then strain. it, and either<lb/> put melted butter, or bruise a good piece of fresh butter in<lb/> flour and add to the Savory Gra<add>v</add>y. give all a scald, and<lb/> throw in some oysters if you have them if you should<lb/> put in the butter rolled in <del>butter</del> flour observe. to keep it stirring<lb/> one way. until it gets a boil and grows of a proper thickness.-<lb/> This a very good.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/128|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
98<lb/> <head>A green Soup Megre. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Waring</head><lb/> <pb/> 175/ Boil a quart or better of old Peas in 3 quarts of water<lb/> with a quarter of a pound of lean Bacon or bit of ham,<lb/> some whole pepper a little allspice. boil them until very<lb/> tender, strain this through a Cullender and then through<lb/> a fine old Towel, take as follows. one handful of<lb/> Onions the younger the better. Lettuces, Cellery, turnips<lb/> Carrots and <unclear>Endiff</unclear>. Cut them best not too small<lb/> stew them in Butter until very tender, then drain them<lb/> from the butter very well. and give them a boil in<lb/> the Liquor you had prepared of the Peas before, if<lb/> any grease arises. skin it off, so serve it up some<lb/> minced parsley.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Savory Toasts. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Obins.</head><lb/> 176/ Mince fine two spoonfuls of lean Hare or bacon,<lb/> choose the tender part, six artichoke bottoms, a little<lb/> parsley, an onion, little nutmeg, little salt, three or four<lb/> spoonfuls of good gravy a small bit of butter. when<lb/> all are minced as fine as you think proper. put all<lb/> down to heat in a toss pan.- have toasts ready on which<lb/> you are to spread the above. or out a french roll across scrap<lb/> out the pith and fill it with the above ingredients having first<lb/> toasted them, the hare or bacon to be boiled before you mince it.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/129|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
99<lb/> <head>Lobster fricacasseed, very good M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Donaldson</head><lb/> <pb/> 177/ Boil your Lobsters as usual. break open the tail part.<lb/> take out the meat as whole as you can, take out the<lb/> Sand Bag. four or five Boils makes a dish, when going<lb/> to use it. throw the Lobster into cream water. to heat it<lb/> thoroughly make ready a little very rich sweet cream,<lb/> add a little mace and salt, put it on to boil, mix<lb/> into it a bit of butter bruised in flour. keep it stirring<lb/> until it looks smooth as any other white sauce<lb/> should do, throw in the Lobsters. let it get as smart heat<lb/> in this, so serve it up and when going to dish it<lb/> squeeze in a little Lemon juice none but the tail part<lb/> and that quite whole should go in a genteel Dish<lb/> for second course- or supper.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Raspberry Vinegar</head><lb/> 178./ To one quart of vinegar, add two pounds of<lb/> Raspberries, let them lie by for 24 hours in the<lb/> vinegar, then add two pounds more let them lie by<lb/> 24 hours longer then strain the vinegar add one pound<lb/> of sugar, let this lie in a Tureen or pan, and Skim<lb/> off the fermentation as it rises in three days it will <lb/> be
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/130|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
100./<lb/> be over their Bottle and Cork it close<hi rend="superscript">x</hi> for use, a little<lb/> of this Liquor gives a fine flavor to punch, also add water<lb/> Boiled and added again to a little of the above, it makes<lb/> a most pleasing Cool Drink, and answers the purpose<lb/> of Lemonaid +c +c +c p M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Dean Hamilton<lb/> <pb/> <head>To make very good Mould Candles. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Dean Hamilton</head><lb/> 179/ Make the necks of fine Cotton, or mixture of Cotton and<lb/> Bristol. yarn, sixteen threads to a half pound Candle<lb/> and so in proportion for a candle of small size, twist your<lb/> tuck hand, in order to clean and pick it, which must<lb/> be done very carefully, after you have put the weeks into<lb/> the Moulds take the twist entirely out before you put<lb/> in your Tallow.-<lb/> How to prepare the Tallow. Take Sheep suet <unclear>unrendered</unclear><lb/> not more than four days old. Chop in fine down, put<lb/> in into a deep large Pot over a Slow fire. first putting a<lb/> small quantity of clear water into the bottom of the Pot. so<lb/> seen as the Pot boils, put in a mixture prepared in the<lb/> following manner to half a pound of pearl or Barritor pot<lb/> ashes, put a pint + half of Boiling water; let it stand until<lb/> the sediment subsides, then pour of the clear water and<lb/> bottle it for use. when your Tallow Boils as before mentioned<lb/> to every pound of Tallow. put in a Tea Spoonful of<lb/> the
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/131|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
101<lb/> the mixture, and also one pinch of the salt of Tarter, when<lb/> the froth subsides, which the insection of the mixture will<lb/> occasion take <del>off</del> the tallow off the fire, and let it remain until<lb/> <del>it be</del> sufficiently cool to put into your moulds. let be as <lb/> cool as it will remain Liquid or about milk warm.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Lettuce Soup - by M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Dean Hamilton.</head><lb/> 180/ Take four Lettuces, the green part of six onions. cut and<lb/> wash them, and lay them in a Cloth to dry. then clean a<lb/> quarter of a pound of butter as for Callops. when brown,<lb/> put in your herbs. stirring it constantly. until the butter be<lb/> soaked up, then put in half a naggin of Catchsup stirring<lb/> as before. cover the pan very close. and stew the herbs until<lb/> tender which will be in a quarter of an hour. stew a<lb/> peas and Collyflowers, with them, when all are properly<lb/> stewed, pour in boiling water. keep it stirring on the fire<lb/> until it comes to a consistnace of cream then put in a<lb/> Curst of Bread, and let it steam as long as before mentioned<lb/> so serve it up with the Bread in it.-<lb/> <pb/> A Soup
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/132|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
102<lb/> <head>A Soup, by Miss Johnston- very good</head><lb/> 181/ Take of Lettuce, Cellery, Leeks, Cherville, and Cucumber, a handfull<lb/> stew all in butter, except the Cucumber, which must not go in<lb/> until the soup is near done, or until they are very tender, take<lb/> a slice of Bread, fry it in butter, lay it in your tureen, <del><gap/></del><lb/> and fill up with boiling water let it stand whilst the<lb/> herbs are stewing. then rub the bread through a sieve and<lb/> add the water the Bread was in to the herbs season it with<lb/> spices as you choose and salt, add to it some good Gravy<lb/> that a bit of bacon ha been boiled in, boil all together very<lb/> well; this Soup requires to be well scummed.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Cope's Receipt for Curd Puddings</head><lb/> 182/ Boil three quarts of sweet milk, turn it with one quart<lb/> of Buttermilk. Runnet wanted also answer, take the Curd off<lb/> the whey, and squeeze it pretty dry from the whey, pound<lb/> pretty well in your Marble Mortar the Curd, and also a quarter<lb/> of a pound of Sugar pounded and sifted the rind of two or<lb/> three Lemons, the juice of two, a quarter of a pound of fresh butter<lb/> melted either to oil or with a little Cream, the yolks of twelve<lb/> Eggs, the whites of two, a Nutmeg. a glass of Brandy. the pitt<lb/> of a fresh roll finely crumbled, mix all these together very<lb/> well
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/133|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
103<lb/> well, but small Bowls or Cups, and bake them in a moderate<lb/> oven three quarters of an hour then turn them out have some<lb/> in a boat of W. wine, sugar or Brandy, have some sweet<lb/> almonds blanched and Cut long and stick them up in<lb/> the pudding, they should be a light brown and if they soak a<lb/> little, will turn and the . butter, - a most excellent receipt-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Potatoe Fritters</head><lb/> 183/ One pound of Potatoes boiled and pounded very<lb/> fine the yolks and whites of two Eggs, brandy and<lb/> Sugar to your taste. after you have mixed the Sugar<lb/> Brandy and Eggs, put in your potatoes blend them<lb/> together to a fine paste. some grated nutmeg an <unclear>oimee</unclear><lb/> of butter five Spoonfuls of flour. drop them into boiling<lb/> drippings of which these must be a good quantity.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To make Vinegar</head><lb/> 184/ To each Gallon of Boiling water put one pound of coarsest<lb/> kind of Brown Sugar, stir it well and let it stand<lb/> in a Crock 'till cool enough for barm, then put into it a<lb/> toast of bread well soaked in cream, stir it now and then<lb/> as it stands before the fire or some hours then put it into<lb/> the Keg. and cover the bung with Canvas or brown paper<lb/> with holes in it to admit some air. let it stand in a warm<lb/> place 'till to Bottle which will be in about three months-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/134|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
104<lb/> <head>Yorkshire Puddings</head><lb/> <pb/> 185/ Three Eggs to a pint of milk and two large table spoonfuls<lb/> of flour, a little salt and Ginger mixed very well about<lb/> an hour before you bake it, put it into a baking dish<lb/> or a tin pan does better. and set it before the fire in a <lb/> tin Oven about a quarter of an hour that the bottom part<lb/> of it may be done enough or set it over a Choffing<lb/> dish, but dont let the top get too brown, which<lb/> would prevent the dripping pan getting into the preceding<lb/> set it before the fire under the meat for a bout half an<lb/> hour.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Ginger Beer</head><lb/> 186/ 22 quarts of water, 5 pounds of Brown Sugar,<lb/> 5 ozs. of Ginger, well bruised boil it an hour just<lb/> before you take it off the fire. add the whites and<lb/> shells of four Eggs well beaten up. then strain it well<lb/> and when nearly cold add the juice of 8 Lemons +<lb/> the thin rind of four. then stir and add a Table <lb/> spoonful of all yeast. Bung the Cask close let it<lb/> stand a fortnight, then bottle it + in an other fortnight<lb/> it will be fit for use.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/135|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
105<lb/> <head>To Pickle Cucumbers</head><lb/> <pb/> 187/ Put your Cucumbers in any strong cold <add>salt +</add> water for<lb/> one night take them out and put them in a<lb/> preserving Pan with a green or vine leaves under<lb/> them then mix Vinegar and water half and half<lb/> and put as much in the Pan as will cover<lb/> the Cucumbers and put leaves over them then<lb/> put it on the Fire and when they come to a Slight<lb/> boil or scald take it off and empty them on the<lb/> fire again with the same Vinegar and water<lb/> Cut fresh Leaves, they will grown quite yellow<lb/> but in five or six times heating them they will<lb/> be quite green. wipe them dry and put them<lb/> in a Crock, get vinegar. Pepper and Ginger<lb/> and give it a boil and put it on the<lb/> Cucumbers.-<lb/> <pb/> A receipt
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/136|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
106<lb/> <head>A receipt for a large Barnbrack</head><lb/> <pb/> 188/<lb/> Three pounds of flour six Eggs, half a pound of Currants, a<lb/> quarter of a pound of Sugar. d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi> of fresh butter. two Tablespoonfuls<lb/> of barm and three naggings of new milk cover it before<lb/> the fire 'till it rises and then bake it in a quick oven.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>For a haunch of Mutton</head><lb/> 189/ Wipe it perfectly dry for two or three days then rub it<lb/> well with ground ginger. hang it up in a dry place by<lb/> the Knuckle, when it begins to appear moist, let the<lb/> moisture be wiped off. and put fresh ground ginger and<lb/> let it hand ten or twelve days and let it be roasted in<lb/> a paste.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Vegetable Soup.</head><lb/> 190/ Pare and slice very fine a good many turnips, grate any<lb/> equal quantity of Carrots. slice fine a quantity of onions<lb/> a like quantity of Cellery, all of which are to be put down<lb/> in a Stewpan with a pound of good butter or more as required<lb/> put this on a stew hearth, and let it melt and stew, until<lb/> it becomes fine like pulp, have ready at same time good <unclear>th<gap/></unclear><lb/> Gravy warm and some of it to the vegetables which are<lb/> steaming in Butter- let all boil very slow and long until you<lb/> find
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/137|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
107<lb/> find the roots become so tender and soft as to go though a <lb/> hair sieve or fine Cullender which you are to press with the<lb/> back of a spoon. make your rich gravy as thick of this pulp<lb/> as you think proper, it should be rather thick, season with<lb/> salt, cut in small long slices carrots Turnips, onions and the<lb/> long stalk of Cellery, give them a boil so as to be tender lay<lb/> them in the Turreen and throw your boiling soup over them<lb/> pretty thick with the pulped vegetables so serve it up-<lb/> this should be made the day before, stir it often on the <lb/> fire else it will burn while stirring.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To make <unclear>Noyace</unclear>.</head><lb/> 191/ To a quart of Brandy put half a pound of good brown<lb/> Sugar, two ounces of Bitter almonds blanched and Cut in<lb/> long pieces. add six Cloves- let it stand for a fortnight<lb/> shaking the bottle every day. then <unclear>slice</unclear> it off when it is<lb/> fit for use-<lb/> <pb/> 192/ The most approved receipt for pickling Beef, Pork + Tongues.<lb/> To twenty quarts of water add as much salt as will make<lb/> it bear an Egg, boil and scum it, when cold add three pounds<lb/> of Brown Sugar half a pound of Salt Petre and two pounds of<lb/> Bay Salt, Stir all very well and pour it on the Beef, Pork or Tongue,<lb/> which are not to be rubed with salt. the meat will not be fit.<lb/> for use for three days.-<lb/> another
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/138|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
108<lb/> <head>Another Receipt for Pickling Beef.- p I. Thompson</head><lb/> <pb/> 193/ To Eight Gallons of water add fourteen pounds of Salt and<lb/> four ounces of Salt Petre, boil well and Skim it, and<lb/> when perfectly cold pour it on the Beef, by adding 3 1/2 lbs of<lb/> moist Sugar you improve the flavor of the Beef.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Mock Turtle Soup (from Farley.)</head><lb/> 194/ Scald a Calfs head clean. cut off the horney parts into<lb/> into square bits 2 Inches long. wash them and dry them in<lb/> a cloth, put these into a Slow fire with 4 quarts of Gravy<lb/> this take 6 lbs of Beef, Calves foot or two an Onion, 2 Carrots<lb/> a shank of ham, a head of Cellery a few Cloves and whole<lb/> pepper, a bunch of Sweet herbs, a little Lemon Peel a few<lb/> Truffles add 8 quarts of water stew this well on a slow fire<lb/> until reduced one half. then strain it and put it in as<lb/> above directed; then add to it some knotted margoram, a <lb/> little Savory, thyme and parsley. chopped all together, add some<lb/> Cloves mace pounded very little Cayenne pepper some green<lb/> Onions, and a shallot chopped. some Mushrooms chopped<lb/> and half a pint of strong white Wine, stew all together<lb/> gently 'till reduced to 3 quarts. then take a little warm <unclear>So<gap/></unclear> <lb/> stir some flour in it with the yolks of 2 Eggs and keep them<lb/> stirring over a slow fire 'till near boiling. then add this<lb/> to
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/139|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
109.-<lb/> to the soup stirring as you pour in. stew all together for an hour<lb/> or more, when taken off squeeze in half a Lemon and half<lb/> an Orange, thrown in Force meat <unclear>balbs</unclear> and potatoes cut small<lb/> and boiled before very tender.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A mock Turtle soup. (<hi rend="underline">Col. Hall</hi>) </head><lb/> 195/ Boil a leg of Beef with some whole pepper, mace onions<lb/> a little salt to a strong Gravy, that will Jelly when cold<lb/> let it stand until next day. skim off all fat, boil a<lb/> Calfs head vey tender. Cut it when Cold from the bone<lb/> in very thin small bits one inch long or better, add the<lb/> head bone to the gravy to help the strength of it. make<lb/> a <unclear>lear</unclear> of Brown butter in the pan as you would<lb/> for hashed meat colour your Soup a pretty brown<lb/> add all the materials to the Soup, and let them simmer<lb/> over a slow fire, untill it looks well and tastes strong<lb/> add force meat balls small size, and butter them,<lb/> a pint of good Sherry or Madera, a little Catchup a<lb/> little Lemon juice so serve it up.-<lb/> This soup should be made the day before it is is<lb/> wanted and is reconed very genteel.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/140|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
110<lb/> <head>M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Jees's Recipt for Trifle very fine supper Dish.</head><lb/> <pb/> 196/ Half a pound of Naple Biscuit, a quarter of a pound of<lb/> Macaroon Cake. two ounce of Ratafia Drops lay a<lb/> <unclear>lear</unclear> of each in your dish over each other. pour over <lb/> this a pint of white wine. a glass of Brandy. dust over this<lb/> some sifted sugar then put over this a pint of good<lb/> cream Custard. add over this a thin <unclear>lear</unclear> of Raspberry<lb/> Jam. cover this with white of Eggs beat into froth<lb/> and over all strew some small <unclear>calamed</unclear> Confits<lb/> so serve it up. this is an excellent Dish-<lb/> <pb/> M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Jees English Cookery Book - Olives Royal<lb/> 197. Take Potatoes boil and pound them fine so that they will<lb/> go through a Cullender add some flour and butter to<lb/> make it into a paste roll it out in a thin <unclear>lear</unclear> then take<lb/> any Cold meat cut into long thin slices, pepper and salt<lb/> it, spread over some cold butter, lay the cold meat on<lb/> the paste and close it up like any puff or role<lb/> or any form you choose fry them a light brown until<lb/> quite hot through. have ready some good strong warm<lb/> Gravy. not too thick lay it in your dish and lay<lb/> the rolls in it so serve it up.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/141|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
111<lb/> <head>Force Meat Balls by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> <pb/> 198/ Take half a pound of lean part of Leg of veal, the<lb/> like quantity of Beef suet minced fine. pound them together<lb/> in a wooden Bowl. until fine and well mixed spread<lb/> some sweet herbs very fine add to the meat, add a little<lb/> new nutmeg grated Lemon, salt and pepper, as you like<lb/> the yolks of two eggs. mix all very well together. roll<lb/> them in small round balls the size of Walnut and <lb/> some long, roll them in flour and fry them a<lb/> handsome brown, if for white sauce put water in a<lb/> Saucepan and when it boils. put them into it and<lb/> boil them for a few minutes for white sauce they<lb/> should not be fried.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Calfs foot Jelly. D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 199/ Take a set of <del><unclear>Cups</unclear></del> Calves feet (which is four) clean and<lb/> scald them free from hair. put them down in six quarts<lb/> of soft water. let them boil very slow, to one half. scum<lb/> any dross or fat nicely off. set it by to cool, next day<lb/> if any Scum appears take it carefully off, lift it from <lb/> your Pan free from any of the Settling at the bottom<lb/> lay this Jelly in your tosspan. add as much white<lb/> Sugar as pleases your taste, the rind of four or five <lb/> Lemons
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/142|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
112<lb/> Lemons also the juice, put this down to Boil on a<lb/> clean fire eat the whites of Six or Seven Eggs with<lb/> which to a thick froth lift it with a spoon add to the<lb/> Jelly. continue to heat until you get in all the eggs<lb/> in this manner. keep stirring with the whisk one way<lb/> whilst on the fire and to prevent burning. when it comes<lb/> to a boil add near a bottle of white wine. let this all<lb/> Boil gently stirring it, until it begins to break, which it will<lb/> do when boiled enough, you may, <del>you may</del> crumble a few<lb/> of the Egg Shells and throw them in it keep to break it, have<lb/> your Flannell bag fixed near the fire, and when it will<lb/> not be disturbed for fear of Mudding the Jelly, the least<lb/> thing to touch the end of the bag would spoil all- throw the<lb/> Jelly just in the Boil into the Bag, have Basons ready<lb/> to receive it under the Bag, the first and second running<lb/> off, throw back into the bag again until it runs out quite<lb/> clear. then cover the top of the bag with a clean Table<lb/> Cloth in several folds to keep in the heat and get all<lb/> the Jelly off- keep close to the fire. keep the Bag covered all<lb/> round for fear of cooling to fast, and loosing the Jelly,<lb/> you may add a glass of any sort of Brandy, put this into<lb/> Shapes, or break it when cold on dishes for Table.-<lb/> <pb/> Hashed
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/143|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
113<lb/> <head>Hashed Veal, by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi></head><lb/> <pb/> 200/ Cut your cold dressed veal in small round pieces the<lb/> size of half-Crown pieces, put them into your tosspan,<lb/> with some good Gravy add Lemon rind, Nutmeg, a little<lb/> mace, a little Lemon, vinegar or pickle, set it on the fire<lb/> thicken it with Butter and flour, when boiling put in the<lb/> thin slices of veal. heat it well, and just before you dish<lb/> add two or three spoonfulls of cream shake the pan well<lb/> so dish them garnish with <unclear>Sappets</unclear> or Lemon.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Ragout of Pigs Petitoes. by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi></head><lb/> 201/ Let the feet Boil until tender, with the heart, lights and Liver,<lb/> mince those last fine, take up the feet, cut them as tender<lb/> thicken some Gravy with Butter and flour, throw in the<lb/> mince, a spoonful of white wine, a little grated Lemon also<lb/> the juice give this a Boil, an Egg hard mince it small, add<lb/> to the rest a little nutmeg, put in the <unclear>pettytoes</unclear> give a shake<lb/> over the fire, but not to Boil, a little minced parsley and <sic>sive</sic><lb/> to dish Garnish with Sippetts.-<lb/> <pb/> A
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/144|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
114/<lb/> <head>A Fricassee of Sheep's Trotters. D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 202/ Scald them free from hair. boil them tender. take out the<lb/> long bone, and split them in tow, lay them in your stewpan,<lb/> with good Gravy, a little white wine nutmeg and mace and <lb/> Salt, beat the yolks of two Eggs with a little cream, add this<lb/> to the rest, a bit of butter rolled in flour, stir it well so as to be<lb/> of a proper thickness to serve it up with sippets. Calves feet<lb/> may be chessed in like manner.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Lion or Leg of Lamb- by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 203/ Cut your Lamb into nice Chops. rub both sides with yolks<lb/> of Eggs. have ready some fine grated Bread, a little parsley<lb/> thyme. Marjorem Savory and grated Lemon, nutmeg grated<lb/> Lemon. nutmeg pepper salt chopped very fine. rub over the<lb/> Chops, fry them a light and nice brown, in boiling water<lb/> garnish with Lemon stew them a little in good Gravy<lb/> so serve up.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Haunch of Venison to Roast by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 204/ In order to roast a haunch of Venison properly, as<lb/> soon as you have spitted it, you must lay over it a long<lb/> sheet of paper, and then a thin common sheet of paste, with<lb/> another paper over that to preserve the fat, tie this fast<lb/> on to prevent the paste falling off if the haunch be large<lb/> it will take four hours to roast. as soon as it is done<lb/> take
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/145|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
115<lb/> take of the papers and paste, and change it well with<lb/> flour, and baste it well with Butter, as soon as it becomes<lb/> a handsome brown, dish it up with some good brown Gravy<lb/> or Currant jelly sauce and send up some in a Boat.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Cold Venison hashed. D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 205/ When you hash venison, cut it into thin slices, put it with a<lb/> glass of red wine in your Tosspan, with a little Catchup. and<lb/> a little browning of butter an Onion stuck with Cloves an<lb/> Anchovey, minced fine. as soon as this Boils in a little<lb/> good Gravy, put in the Venison and Wine and let boil<lb/> for a few minutes a little grated Lemon. or Lemon Pickle lay<lb/> it in a deep dish and pour the Gravy should not be thick<lb/> Currant Jelly should be on the table if wanted by any.-<lb/> Cold roast Ham may be dressed in like<lb/> manner.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Apple Frittons- p D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 206/ Get large apples fit for baking pan them and Cut out<lb/> the core as clean as you can, cut them in round slices not too<lb/> thin, dip them in butter. made thus, take half a pint of ale. three<lb/> Eggs. beat them in as much flour as will make it rather<lb/> thicker than a common pudding with nutmeg and sugar to your<lb/> taste, let it stand some time to rise, dip y<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. apples in this butter + fry them Crisp<lb/> and serve them up with Sugar sifted over them have for sauce<lb/> in a boat white wine or brandy with sug<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. and a little melted butter.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/146|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
116<lb/> <head>Currant Wine. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Macan.</head><lb/> <pb/> 207/ To Seven Gallons of water, add five Gallons of Currants. brown<lb/> them very well in a clear tub. Stalks and fruit with a wooden<lb/> pistile or hard, let them remain for twelve hours. then pour over<lb/> the water. and let them stand for twelve hours more. then strain<lb/> through a hair sieve. and squeeze all as well as you can<lb/> to get out all the juice, to this add 28 pounds of best brown<lb/> Sugar stir it for some time until the Sugar is quite melted,<lb/> then turn it into your vessel let it remain open in the<lb/> Bung until it quits working which will be a long time, as<lb/> it works out fill up again with what cover remains of the<lb/> Liquor after filling the vessel, and which you are to keep<lb/> in Bottles for this purpose. break in four Eggs, shells + all<lb/> into the vessel. it will waste much, and work for a length<lb/> of time, when done working, put in two quarts of Brandy<lb/> and bung it slightly for some days. then close it very safe<lb/> and well and raisin over the bung and let it stand for<lb/> nine months, then Bottle it close and keep the Bottles on the<lb/> Bottom else it will fly, if well done it should sparkle<lb/> when the Bottle is opened, when bottling as you come near<lb/> the grounds of which there will be great deal, run it off, let it<lb/> settle and then filter through a fine towel.- This is a good<lb/> receipt for the above wine.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/147|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
117<lb/> <head>D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. Sweet Breads dressed Brown.</head><lb/> <pb/> 208/ Scald two or three sweet Breads. cut this you like in slices,<lb/> dip them in the Yolk<del>s</del> of Egg, beat up with some fine grated bread<lb/> pepper, salt. Nutmeg, and a little flour finely mixed and smooth<lb/> fry them a nice brown. thicken a little rich gravy with butter and<lb/> flour boil it smooth, add a little Cachup mushroom powder<lb/> or any Savory thing that you like, a little Lemon rind and<lb/> juice stew the sweet breads in this for a few minutes to<lb/> serve them up. if you like you may add pullets boiled and<lb/> cut fine to this, as for a Ragout, Garnish with fryed parsley<lb/> or Lemon.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Savory Veal Pyes. D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head> 209/ Cut a neck of veal into Chops. season with pepper salt<lb/> and pounded mace and nutmeg. lay the meat in your<lb/> some sweet Breads if you have them, also seasoned hard<lb/> boiled yolks of Eggs some Oysters, some good Gravy, put<lb/> a rich paste round your Dish an Inch thick. then cover<lb/> it with paste equally thick bake it one hour and a quarter in<lb/> a quick oven. you may cut off the bad quarter it and stick it<lb/> round the dish and cover the veal with sliced Lemon if you<lb/> like it.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/148|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
118<lb/> <head>Anchovy Sauce. D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> <pb/> 210/ Cut small two or three anchovy, put them down in a pint<lb/> of good Gravy a quarter of a pound of butter bruised in flour<lb/> stir it altogether until it boils, you may add as you choose<lb/> little catsup, juice of Lemon, glass or red wine, or walnut<lb/> Liquor, add to this melted butter, and as many Savory things<lb/> as you choose. to fish sauce it is the better.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Shrimp Sauce by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 211/ Wash half a pint of Shrimps very clean, put them in the<lb/> stewpan, with a spoonful of anchovy Liquor, and half a<lb/> pound of Butter melted thick. boil it up for five or<lb/> six minutes. squeeze in the juice of half a Lemon toss<lb/> it round, put in your boat and serve it up this is good<lb/> and proper for all flat fish.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Oyster Sauce very good- d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 212/<lb/> Open what quantity of Oysters you choose, wash them very clean<lb/> in their own Liquor, then strain the Liquor through a Cloth,<lb/> have it free from grit or sediment put this and the oysters down<lb/> on a close fire and when a froth comes all they are done enough<lb/> then add a spoonful of an<add>c</add>hovy Liquor a little pounded mace<lb/> and thicken in a bit of butter mixed in flour. a little good Gravy<lb/> a little
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/149|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
119<lb/> little Lemon juice give all a scald together so serve it up-<lb/> you may add some melted butter as you wish to measure the quantity.<lb/> <pb/> <head>Stewed Oysters. good D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> <gap/>/<lb/> Open your oysters wash them in their own Liquor free from<lb/> Grit, strain the Liquor through a Cloth, into the tosspan add<lb/> a little pounded Mace, a little nutmeg, and thicken with a bit of<lb/> butter bruised in flour fine, boil this for three or four minutes add<lb/> to this a spoonful of Cream. then put in your Oysters, shake them<lb/> round, but do not let them boil, else they will grow hard, add<lb/> a little drop of white wine, so serve them up, Toast a bit of bread<lb/> cut it in Cover bits Garnish your Dish, Cockles may be done<lb/> in like manner.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Lobster Sauce by Ditto. - good.-</head><lb/> 214/ Take a little mace and whole pepper boil them together, so as to <lb/> get out the strength of the spice, then strain it off an not as much<lb/> butter in this water, have you Lobsters ready boiled. cut the<lb/> fish into small pieces, add it to the butter, and stir all together<lb/> with an anchovy well minced until tender, or make it as follows-<lb/> bruise the Body of a Lobster into smooth melted butter, cut the flesh<lb/> into small bits, if a pea Lobster bruise the pea fine with the <lb/> point of a Knife, stew all together let it get a boil season<lb/> with a butter fine mace very little pepper and slat- N.B. <unclear>Crea</unclear><lb/> fish sauce may be made in this manner-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/151|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
121 <lb/> <head>Suit Pudding to Bake or boil.</head> <lb/> 217/ Take four spoons fulls of flour half a pound of Beef Suit <lb/> cut fine four eggs, beat well a spoon full of beaten ginger a <lb/> Teaspoonful of salt, a quart of milk, mix the Eggs and flour <lb/> with a pint of the milk pretty thick, add the seasoning, then <lb/> add the rest of the milk, let the butter be pretty thick boil this <lb/> two hours, if baked add Sugar and Brandy to your taste. <lb/> <pb/> <head>Onion Sauce by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head> <lb/> 218/ Boil as many large onions as you may judge wanted, throw <lb/> off the first water as it is too strong. boil them in a second <lb/> water, when tender take them out. let them cool, then cut them <lb/> rather large as they look richer, take some good cream, as you <lb/> may have occasioned for, throw in throw in as many onions as you will <lb/> want, a little salt, add melted butter to this let it stew a little <lb/> so as to be of a proper thickness, and look rich. some add a little <lb/> bit of stewed turnips to this sauce, it helps to thicken + makes <lb/> it sweet and is not to be discovered in the sauce, so cover your <lb/> Rabets or Ducks or any meat you intend it for.- <lb/> <pb/> Celery
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/152|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
122 <lb/> <head>Celary Sauce good by D <hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head> 219/ Wash as many heads of Celary as you may have a occasion for <lb/> very clean, cut the white stalks and solid part in bits about <lb/> one inch long. pare off all stringy and hard or bad parts. boil <lb/> this in soft water until tender enough, then lay it by to cool + <unclear>Drain</unclear> <lb/> then take pint of good rich cream, put in the Celary, add a little <lb/> pounded mace, nutmeg, a pinch of salt, add a bit of butter bruised<lb/> fine in flour, boil this gently until it looks smooth and <unclear>beabls</unclear> <lb/> well, if you have oysters scald some and some of the Liquor <lb/> a little before you dish, a little catsup and grated lemmon <lb/> a little good gravy and small, mushrooms if you have them <lb/> so, cover your Fowl +c +c +c - <lb/> <pb/> <head>Eggs curiously dressed D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 221./ Boil a good many Eggs hard. take out the Yolks, whole<lb/> out of half the number you boil, cut the remainder in quarts<lb/> white and yolks together have ready on the fire some good<lb/> Gravy with a little sliced parsley and thyme. give this a B<gap/><lb/> a little nutmeg. then put in the Eggs. a bit of butter bruised<lb/> fine in flour, a little salt. toss all round and have it a<lb/> proper thickness, you may add artichoke bottoms fryed<lb/> and cut in small bits.-<lb/> You may boil Eggs hard. cut them in two, toss them up<lb/> in white sauce as for a fricassy. serve them up with the<lb/> narrow end up this is a pretty dis for supper.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/153|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
123<lb/> <head>Hare Soup by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 221/ Cut a large old Hare into small bits put into a Jug with<lb/> three or four quarts of soft water some blades of mace a little<lb/> salt a few Onions, a red herring if you have it, if not a few<lb/> anchovy cut in bits, half a pint of red wine, bake this for <lb/> three hours. or stew it on a very slow fire, if in the oven<lb/> it must be quick, then strain off the Liquor into your<lb/> Tosspan have ready boiled three ounces of French Barley<lb/> or Sago, nearly boil the Liver of the Hare bruise it fine<lb/> through a sieve and add to the soup, you may also<lb/> put the Brandy through the sieve. a small bit of butter<lb/> set it on the fire . keep it stirring, but not to boil, if you<lb/> dislike the liver put small sippets of toasted Bread steeped<lb/> in red Wine, so serve it up.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Hashed Hare by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 222/<lb/> <gap/> any cold Hare be left that you wish to make use of Dress it<lb/> as follows,- Cut into small bits if any of the pudding be left, rub<lb/> it small and put to it a glass of red wine. the like quantity of <lb/> Gravy. an anchovy chopped fine an onion stuck with cloves and a good<lb/> bit of butter mixed in flour shake all together over a slow fire until<lb/> <gap/> Hare be thorough hot it is wrong to let any hashed meat come to a boil<lb/> as it hardens it, send the hare up in a deep dish take out the onion and<lb/> lay sippets round the Dish.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/154|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
124<lb/> <head>To Dress a Calfs Pluck by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> <pb/> 223/ Boil the lights and a part of the Liver roast the heart stuff<lb/> as follows, a little suet cut small some bread grated, some fine<lb/> minced parsley thyme or any sweet herbs you choose a little pepper<lb/> and salt, grated Lemon mix this with the yolk of an Egg<lb/> heat up when the Liver and lights are boiled so as to <unclear>mince</unclear> <unclear>some</unclear><lb/> put them into a toss pan. with a bit of butter roled in flour<lb/> a little salt pepper and grate Lemon or Lemon Vinegar<lb/> and Nutmeg, cut the remaining part of the Liver into slices<lb/> also some slices of bacon fry them <gap/> quick and nice brown<lb/> lay the mince in your Dish. the heart in the middle of this<lb/> and the fry round the dish. cover with fryed parsley some<lb/> melted Butter.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Sauce for Carp or Tench by D<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>.</head><lb/> 224/ Save all the blood of your Carp when you take it, have some<lb/> very strong good Gravy, seasoned with pepper, salt and mace<lb/> and onion, boil and strain this off before you stew your fish<lb/> in it, and boil your Carp before you put it down to stew<lb/> in this prepared Gravy be careful not to boil your Gravy<lb/> to much away before you put your fish into it and then<lb/> stew it on a slow fire for a quarter of an hour or better then<lb/> the sauce with butter bruised in flour, or you may make
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/155|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
125<lb/> your sauce this, take the liver of the Carp free from the guts<lb/> two or three anchovy, a little parsley thyme + an onion, mince<lb/> all fine together, take half a pint of Renish Wine four spoonfuls<lb/> of Vinegar and the Blood of the Carp. put all these together<lb/> stew them gently on a slow fire. keep stirring least the blood<lb/> settle add this to the carp, which must be boiled as before<lb/> <unclear>mel.<hi rend="superscript">d</hi></unclear> in salt and water, and a little wine, and take great<lb/> care not to stew the Carp to much or it will break in<lb/> the sauce.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Sauce for a Cods head or any boiled fish you may chose</head><lb/> 225/ Pick out a good Lobster, stick a scewer the vent at the <lb/> tail, and boil it in boiling water and salt until done near<lb/> half an hour, if the Lobster has pea when boiled take it out<lb/> and bruise it fine with the point of a knife have ready some<lb/> melted butter, take the meat out of the Lobster, break it into<lb/> small bits, add it to the butter with a spoonful of Lemon pickles<lb/> the like of Walnut Liquor one or two slices of Horse Radish<lb/> a little mace a little salt a Dust of Cayine pepper bit of Lemon<lb/> rind, give all a boil for three or four minutes then take out the Horse<lb/> Radish and Lemon fill your Small Boats, if Lobster cannot be<lb/> got make use of Oysters or Shrimps in like manner if shell fish cannot<lb/> be head you may then add four or five anchovy chopped fine a spoonful<lb/> of walnut Liquor + an onion stuck with cloves. and when boiled<lb/> take out of the sauce.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/156|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
126<lb/> <head>A Hare Soup-</head><lb/> <pb/> 226/ Take the Hare as it comes down from Table with the Gravy<lb/> that remains in the dish put it into your Soup pan with<lb/> an Onion, a Carrot, some sweet herbs some Gravy or some<lb/> bones that you think will be sufficient for the quantity of<lb/> soup you want stew them 'till they are so tender that<lb/> the Hare will pulp through a Cullender or coarse Sieve<lb/> this adds to it a little butter rolled in flour a spoonful of<lb/> good cream a little anchovy and what seasoning you think<lb/> it requires and then serve it up.- - M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Dam: Kelly-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Beefs Head Soup.-</head><lb/> 227/ Boil the Head down to a strong stock then take out a<lb/> sufficient quantity for your soup brown as much Butter<lb/> and flour as you think will thicken it then mix the soup<lb/> in it, Boiling hot. strain it and add your seasoning as<lb/> you like with Catsup, anchovy and as much wine as you<lb/> think is necessary, cut the nice part of the head in small<lb/> square bits and send it up in the soup first letting it get<lb/> a boil in it if you wish to have it particularly for <unclear>Comfry</unclear><lb/> there ought to be forced meat balls and yolks of Eggs boiled<lb/> hard along with the meat in it.- Mr<hi rend="superscript">s</hi> Dam. Kelly.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/157|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
127<lb/> <head>M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> D: Kelly</head><lb/> <head>Spices for Kitchen use and to be put into any Savory</head><lb/> <head>Dish, or Soup.-</head><lb/> 228/ Nutmegs Maces, Long pepper, common d<hi rend="superscript">o</hi>. Cinamon of all<lb/> an equal quantity. half an ounce of each, Dry these before.<lb/> the fire, then add grated Ginger rather more than above <unclear>menl<hi rend="superscript">d</hi>.</unclear><lb/> some Salt, pound all together very fine in Mortar, put into<lb/> small Bottles, and Cork very close. keep this in a dry place<lb/> and as you have occasion add either to Savory dishes or<lb/> Soup a small quantity of this from a Teaspoonful to a Desert<lb/> spoon, but you may judge from your Taste how much to<lb/> add, it highly improves the flavour of Soups +c +c +c<lb/> <pb/> <head>Browning for Gravy- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> D: Kelly.</head><lb/> 229/ Put one ounce of fresh Butter into a frying Pan on a slow<lb/> fire when the butter boils, add by Degrees a quarter of a pound<lb/> of pounded Sugar, when it is of a deep brown but not<lb/> burnt, put in about half a pint of boiling water mix it well<lb/> but let it get a good boil in the Pan then pour it into a<lb/> Bowl, and when cool scim it, bottle it and keep for use<lb/> either for Gravy or Savory dishes-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/158|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
128<lb/> <head>To Pot the <hi rend="underline">Skin</hi> of Beef. Shin.</head><lb/> <pb/> 230/ Cut the shin small and let it lie in the seasoning for five<lb/> days, which should be pepper, salt, salt petre, little pounded<lb/> clove or allspice or both, the bone is broken and put into the<lb/> potting pot with the meat which should have marrow or<lb/> rinsed suit laid over, and then cover the meat with paste<lb/> to keep in the steam when baked very tender the bone is taken<lb/> out <del>of</del> the marrow mixed with the meat and pounded<lb/> fine, put into the pot again and set it in the oven,<lb/> for a short time. put this into any shape you please<lb/> to cool, cut in slices and send to Table. a little strong Gravy<lb/> put in, is not amiss.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Gooseberry Wine</head><lb/> 231/ To every pound of Gooseberry's when picked and well<lb/> bruised put a pint of cold water let remain three days stirring<lb/> them twice a day then strain off the Liquor and add three<lb/> pounds of Loaf sugar to every Gallon and to every twenty<lb/> quarts put one quart of French Brandy, and a small quantity <lb/> of Isinglass, then put into a Crock which must be closed<lb/> immediately. The fermentation being over and when it<lb/> has stood 6 months it may be bottled-<lb/> Observation- Browne sugar does just as well as white<lb/> and there is no occasion for Spirits-<lb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/159|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
129<lb/> <head>Ginger Cordial- By Miss Louisa</head><lb/> 232/ To a pound of white Currants picked from the stalks<lb/> and bruised a little to let out the juice, put one quart of good<lb/> whisky, let it stand 24 hours then strain it through a hair<lb/> Sieve and press out all the juice. add to this one pound<lb/> of Lump Sugar bake small to melt the sooner and a quarter<lb/> of an ounce of Ginger <del>ground</del> <add>pounded</add> let it stand until the Sugar<lb/> is all dissolved, so bottle it close in a few days you may<lb/> use it as cordial or <unclear>Liure</unclear> after fruit - the rind of two or<lb/> three Lemons will improve it. you make as many quarts as<lb/> you choose after the about manner-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Turnip Soup M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi>. D<hi rend="superscript">n</hi>. Kelly</head><lb/> 233/ Take Eight or nine Turnip peel and slice them, put them<lb/> into a slow fire, with three or four heads of Cellery two or<lb/> three slices of Ham. the Curst of a roll, an Onion, a Carrot<lb/> the size of one Egg of Butter, stew them altogether, strain<lb/> them through a Hair Sieve, add as much good strong Gravy<lb/> as will make it the thickness of pea Soup, have some<lb/> Turnips cut small and boiled in the Gravy, add them to<lb/> the Soup so send to Table. season with a little Cayenne<lb/> pepper and Salt to your taste.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/160|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
130/<lb/> <head>A Potatoe Soup. M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi>. D: Kelly's</head><lb/> 234/ Take six Potatoes, one pound of Mutton, one Onion a<lb/> head of Cellary, some white pepper and slat stew them over<lb/> a slow fire till the Potatoes are dissolved then put them<lb/> through a Cullender or hair sieve, add Gravy to them two<lb/> ozs. of butter rolled in flour let it boil till the butter is dissolved<lb/> then add half a pint of cream and serve it up with cream<lb/> thrown into it- these ingredients ought to make three quarts-<lb/> Some Catsup added and some Cayenne pepper when<lb/> going to Table is an improvement, the potatoes are to be<lb/> pared before they are put down-<lb/> <pb/> <head>English Champaigne of <unclear>minpe</unclear> Gooseberries.-</head><lb/> 235/ To every pound of Gooseberries when well bruised, put a<lb/> pint of Cold water let them remain three days stirring them<lb/> twice a day strain off the Liquor add 3lb of Loaf Sugar to<lb/> every Gallon, and to every 20 quarts one quart of Brandy<lb/> put a little Isinglass into the Cask. close it when the<lb/> fermentation is over- when it stand 6 months it may<lb/> be Bottled, gather the fruit when at full growth before it<lb/> begins to <unclear>upon</unclear>, in measuring use a wine measure.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/161|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
131<lb/> <head>Spruce Beer.-</head><lb/> <pb/> 236/ For a Cask of 20 Gallons. put 7 ounces of the essence of<lb/> Spruce, Boil all the water. and take the spruce and 14 lb<lb/> of Treacle mix it very well in four or five Gallons of the<lb/> water, then add all the water to it and a proper heat put<lb/> a quart of good brown to it and stir it very well with a<lb/> stick till there is a good froth on it, let it lie until next<lb/> day in the Tub, and Skim off the barm, turn it into a half<lb/> Barrel and let it lie without banging for 4 or 5 days to work <add>then bung it close</add><lb/> <add>let it lie a week</add> then bottle it and lay it on the side it will be up in Bottle<lb/> fit to Drink in 10 days or more, when turn the Bottles that<lb/> it may not fly- you may boil in 4 or 5 Gallons of the water<lb/> half a pound of hops for a quarter of an hour and strain it-<lb/> This is an improvement to the Spruce Beer. - from H. Stuart.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>A Green Pea Soup- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi>. Kelly</head><lb/> 237/ Put down three quarts of old Green peas with 3 Onions<lb/> a little cellery tops, a bunch of mint a shank of ham or any bone<lb/> of fresh meat boil all very well and until the peas are so<lb/> soft as to bruise easily and go through a Cullender, <unclear>wet</unclear> then<lb/> the soup till you get all the pulp out and some whole<lb/> pepper in the beginning - when this done if not a fine gren<lb/> pound a little Spinnage and Squeeze the juice in the<lb/> Soup
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/162|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
132<lb/> <unclear>Soup</unclear> till it is a good colour, put it on the fire, and when<lb/> boiling add to it, a bit of Butter bruised in flour and keep<lb/> it stirring until melted this keeps to thicken and prevents the<lb/> Soup from separating, have ready one quart of young peas<lb/> boiled a little fine minced parsley and a few small young onions<lb/> throw all into the soup and serve it up, give the onions<lb/> a scald and take off the skins add a little salt.-<lb/> This is a very good receipt-<lb/> <pb/> <head>To Preserve Carrots</head> <lb/> 238/<lb/> Take large orange Carrots free from worm eaten boil them as<lb/> for the Table and blanch them. then cut them in rings with<lb/> a tin cutter or large thimble take out all the pale inside<lb/> and leave them in hollow rings make a rich Syrup<lb/> with a pint of water to each pound of Sugar, boil and<lb/> skin it well, add a large handful of whole Ginger and<lb/> Lemon Peel. when the Syrup is quite cold put in the<lb/> Carrots and let them remain all night, the day following<lb/> Boil them all together very slowly and do so every day<lb/> till the Carrots are clear and the syrup rich and thick<lb/> if the Syrup reduces (which it often does) make more and<lb/> add a little Lemon juice to take off the richness, one good boil<lb/> each day will be enough.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/163|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
133<lb/> <head>To make Spring Soup</head><lb/> 239./ Take a Knuckle of veal, part of a roasted fowl and<lb/> some slices of Bacon put down these in a Stew pan<lb/> with a little mace. white whole pepper and a few grains<lb/> of allspice also a cup of ground rice, when all are very<lb/> well boiled strain it, and skin the fat entirely off<lb/> when it is coming to the boil, cut small whatever herbs<lb/> or Roots you like Cellery Carrots Collyflower Turnips +c + <lb/> having boiled them separately put them into the soup<lb/> with the yolks of nine Eggs, a pint of cream the Eggs<lb/> and cream must be heated up together first and remember<lb/> the Soup is not to boil after the Eggs are put in-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Carrot Soup- Major Read</head><lb/> 240/ Put some <unclear>beef</unclear> leaves with 4 quarts of the Liquor in which a leg<lb/> of Mutton or Beef has been boiled 2 large Onions, a Turnip, pepper<lb/> and salt into a Saucepan stew them for 3 hours, have ready<lb/> 6 large Carrots scraped and cut thin and stew them until<lb/> soft enough to put through a hair Sieve or coarse cloth. then<lb/> boil the pulp with the soup which is to tie thick as pea soup-<lb/> use two wooden Spoons to rub the Carrots through. make the soup<lb/> the day before it is to be used, add red pepper- purposely the red<lb/> part of the Carrot and not the yellow.-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/164|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
134<lb/> <head>An Almond Soup.</head><lb/> <pb/> 241/ Take some veal Broth that is in jelly strain it and put it down<lb/> again with some mace and pepper and salt to your taste<lb/> pound almonds fine, not them lest they turn to oil, throw them<lb/> into your Soup. half a pint of cream boil it and throw it last<lb/> into the soup with some butter rolled in flour let it boil till<lb/> you think it thick enough beat the yolks of two Eggs to put<lb/> into it and some boiled Celery.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Elder Flower wine</head><lb/> 242/ 6 Gallons of water. 18 lb of Sugar the whites of four Eggs<lb/> beat up, boil and skim the Syrup clear. pour it on three<lb/> pint of the white part of the Elder flowers the rind of 6 <lb/> Lemons paired thin and the juice free from seeds or pulp<lb/> with 6lb of raisins (<unclear>suw</unclear>)stoned and cut small when with<lb/> cream add two spoonfuls of Bann let it work for two days<lb/> keeping it stirred very often. put in Cask. let the Bung remain<lb/> open for a few days Then close it 6 months it will keep<lb/> to Bottle.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/165|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
135<lb/> <head>Rice Blanchemange</head><lb/> <pb/> 243/ Take a quarter of pound of Rice, pounded and sifted very<lb/> nice + fine, put into it three pints of good milk, put it on a<lb/> slow fire stirring it till it is a proper thickness then put it into<lb/> cups and turn it out as Blanchemange with <sic>whiped</sic> cream<lb/> about it. chop it over with Currant Jelly, you must<lb/> Sweeten it to your liking and it will prove good <unclear>Vhanes</unclear> pound<lb/> <pb/> <head>Neat Picking to your Tea</head><lb/> 244/ 2 lb of flour 4 Eggs 1/2 lb butter one spoonful of sweet bann<lb/> and cut it with Butter milk- this is the best thing for breakfast<lb/> ever <unclear>Jeat</unclear>. R.E.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Paste for Tarts.</head><lb/> 245/ 1lb Flour 1/2 lb of Butter work them well together, add 2 ozs<lb/> fine Sugar pounded, 2 Eggs yolks and whites with a drop of<lb/> water beat all together well and roll it out thin.<lb/> it proves good. R.E:<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/166|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
136<lb/> <head>To make a <sic>Safron</sic> Cake</head><lb/> <pb/> 246/ Take a quarter of an ounce of <sic>Safron</sic>, dry it in an oven, or<lb/> before the fire, powder it very fine with the point of a knife<lb/> boil it in half a pint of water or new milk then rub a pound<lb/> of Sugar 3/4 lb fresh butter and an ounce of Caraways and <lb/> two pounds of flour beat up the yolks of eight Eggs and<lb/> the whites of six separately. mix them into a quart of good<lb/> Bann then make a hole in the flour, and pour them all <lb/> into it, <sic>Rince</sic> the vessel you boiled your <sic>Safron</sic> in, with<lb/> half a pint of milk, and pout it into the rest, mix all<lb/> together with your hands like a seed cake add a pound of<lb/> flour to make it stiff paste, let it stand to rise then make<lb/> them into Cakes as large as a good sized Table, to bake them<lb/> in a quick oven.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>247 To Pickle Beef (J Thompson)</head><lb/> <pb/> Six quarts of Water three pounds of Coarse Salt<lb/> two oz's of saltpeter two lbs of Brown Sugar boiled<lb/> together and when perfectly cold pour it on the Beef<lb/> let it be fully covered,-<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/167|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
137<lb/> <head>Hung Beef</head><lb/> <pb/> 248/<lb/> Chine the 2 fore quarters of Beef out of which the prime<lb/> parts are 2 Ribs 2 Briskets and 2 plates- Had the rounds +<lb/> Rumps in the kind quarter- The round is dry and hard<lb/> when hung, but if you wish to hang it you do not separate<lb/> the first and second round beef cut out the bone, then<lb/> salt peter over it. we use half a pound well powdered<lb/> when making the prime parts, shave stated) lay it in a<lb/> vessel for 3 weeks it makes its own pickle, but use often<lb/> make more pickle in the same way and add to it. after<lb/> 3 weeks hang it up to dry and when dry enough put it<lb/> in a vessel and cover it with seeds-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Hung Buttermilk (or scotch cream)</head><lb/> <pb/> <gap/>/ Take 2 quarts of fresh churned Buttermilk or more if required<lb/> tie it up in a linen cloth or Bag, so as not to squeeze it then<lb/> hang it up to drain, change the cloth twice a day let it<lb/> hang until it becomes dry then whip it up adding a Cup of<lb/> fresh cream and some lemon and sugar when thick turn<lb/> into a dish-.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/007/001/168|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
138- Pound Cake, M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Buchan Stone</unclear><lb/> <pb/> 138- Beat a pound of Butter, to a cream<lb/> with a pound of powdered white sugar<lb/> drop in by degrees the yolks of 10 Eggs<lb/> beat the whites of the Eggs to a smooth<lb/> a pound of fruits, consisting of<lb/> Raisins Currants, preserved orange,<lb/> Lemon peel, Citron, pounded clo<gap/><lb/> + Cinnamon + a pound of sifted<lb/> Flour - all these to be added by <lb/> Degrees, to the butter + sugar with <lb/> a Glass of Brandy- + bake for 3 Hours<lb/> <pb/> Black Currant Whiskey <unclear>In Magmoore</unclear><lb/> <pb/> To Every Gallon of Whiskey Four pounds<lb/> Currants, + two pounds Lump Sugar<lb/> the Fruit must be bruised + added<lb/> by degrees half the Whiskey which<lb/> must <unclear>shudin</unclear> on the fruit 48 hours +<lb/> then be strained through a flannel bag <hi rend="underline">over</hi><lb/> the sugar. the half the whiskey to be added<lb/> the same bag. by degrees. Mix them well<lb/> together, + when settled <unclear>lack</unclear> off in Bottles.
