A Syrup to Cure a Cough though a Consumptive one (p Lady Lefford) <lb/> Take Coltsfoot. Hyssop. <unclear>Maidenham</unclear>. <unclear>Growding</unclear>. and Horchannd of<lb/> each a handful, put them down with three Quarts of water. and<lb/> let them boil slowly in a <unclear>sase</unclear> round vessel 'till near two thirds <lb/> of the water are consumed then strain it and add to it a <lb/> pint of honey or more if that quantity does not make it sweet<lb/> enough add also a few slices of Eringo cool and <unclear>Elensapane</unclear>.<lb/> root and give it a Boil or two on a very slow fire taking the<lb/> Scum off as it rises pour it off and when cold bottle and cork<lb/> it close. The Patient is to take a small Tea cupful every<lb/> morning and to lie in bed a little while afterwards the same quantity <lb/> to be taken every night going to two and a Table spoonful to be <lb/> taken two or three times a day if the Cough is troublesome-<lb/> <unclear>When</unclear> Patients Bowels are too weak to have honey it may be sweetened<lb/> with sugar candy and half an ounce of <unclear>Gumaebee</unclear> may be<lb/> clarified in the Syrup.- more than three parts of it must not<lb/> keep well in Summer but a larger quantity of it may be made<lb/> in the winter the herbs are better when well dried than when<lb/> gathered fresh and if any of the herbs cannot be had (except the<lb/> horchound and alysop and Ground Ivy) it will answer without<lb/> <unclear>strew the</unclear> not so well; only the quantity of water in which they are<lb/> boiled must be <unclear>Caplomed</unclear> in proportion so the herbs that are left out-<lb/> <pb/> <unclear>Inpretuet Jones</unclear> it is recommended to give a Tablespoonful of <unclear>Cam</unclear><lb/> every hour or two. also <unclear>back</unclear>. wine and Cordials-
