105<lb/> (in about 2 months) dissolve 1 oz of<lb/> Isinglass + 2 lb of sugar candy, +<lb/> when <hi rend="underline">nearly cool</hi> put it into the<lb/> Cask + then fill it up with Brandy-<lb/> The scum taken off generally leaves<lb/> a <unclear>tacancy</unclear> of 3 pints in an 8 Gallon<lb/> Cask - which is the quantity of Brandy<lb/> to put in.- then paste some<lb/> Bladder + brown paper over the<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Bung hole</hi> it will be fit to Bottle<lb/> the end of Sept, or very good drawn<lb/> from the Cask.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/120|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
106<lb/> Cake (called Tot fait)<lb/> <lb/> 1/4 lb Ham<lb/> 1/4 lb Sugar<lb/> 1/4 lb <hi rend="underline">Butter short measure</hi><lb/> 3 Eggs- + some Lemon peel - <lb/> these ingredients to be well beaten<lb/> together <hi rend="underline">the longer the better</hi> - to be<lb/> baked in a large tin. or <hi rend="underline">in little</hi><lb/> this as you like.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/121|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
107<lb/> Ginger Cakes (M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Baker)<lb/> 1/2 lb moist sugar<lb/> 1/4 lb butter<lb/> 3/4 oz of best Ginger finely powdered<lb/> 2 Table spoonsfull of milk - Simmer<lb/> these Ingredients carefully together<lb/> over a gentle fire and continue<lb/> stirring till they boil. then mix<lb/> in your flour till worked into a<lb/> stiff paste to be rolled out thin +<lb/> cut into cakes which will only<lb/> require about 3 minutes baking<lb/> in a quick oven - as soon as<lb/> coloured take them out- They <lb/> must be rolled out while the paste<lb/> is warm.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/122|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
108<lb/> Laady Coventrys<lb/> To a pint of boiling milk add as<lb/> much Flour to thicken it as will take<lb/> up - Then pound it in a marble<lb/> mortar till quite smooth - add the<lb/> yolks of 8 + whites of 4 Eggs. pound<lb/> altogether- Fry the mixture in<lb/> lumps about the size of Bergamy<lb/> pear, in clear dripping, open the<lb/> lumps at top when nicely done,<lb/> + fill them with hot custard<lb/> sift sugar over them + serve<lb/> them immediately. -
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/123|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
109<lb/> Fish Sauce<lb/> Cockles + Mussels - 1 Pint of each <hi rend="underline">clean</hi><lb/> washed, + put into a covered stew pan<lb/> stew gradually to get out all the <lb/> liquor. mix that liquor with about<lb/> a fill of cream, thicken it with<lb/> flour + butter (properly rubbed)<lb/> add Cayenne + a <hi rend="underline">small quantity</hi><lb/> of essence of anchovy, boil + stir it<lb/> all the time.-<lb/> <pb/> Sauce for Vegetables<lb/> a little <hi rend="underline">thin</hi> cream or new milk, or a<lb/> mixture of cream + old milk - 2 or 3<lb/> spoonsful of good real gravey - Thicken<lb/> with a little flour + Batter - add a <lb/> little pepper + salt, boil + pour it<lb/> over the vegetable.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/124|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
110<lb/> For a Dish of Curry<lb/> Dust a little Curry powder over the<lb/> meat when cut up- Take 3 large onions<lb/> Cut + dust them also + dry both in<lb/> butter, the meat first, then the onions<lb/> in the same butter - put both into<lb/> the stew pan with a little weak<lb/> gravey or water, add you thickening<lb/> (that is butter + flour well rubbed)<lb/> + let all stew gently for 2 hours.-<lb/> adding gravy if it gets too dry.-<lb/> Then Fork out the meat, scum the<lb/> rest thro' a sieve (to clear off the<lb/> onion) put the gravy back into<lb/> the stew pan, adding a tea-<lb/> -spoonful of curry powder<lb/> (Improved Healaugh receipt)
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/125|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
111<lb/> To make Snaps (or Ginger Cakes)<lb/> 1 lb of Treacle<lb/> 1/2 lb of flour<lb/> 3 oz. moist sugar, 3 oz butter,<lb/> 1/4 lb ginger very finely grated + a<lb/> little orange peel, pour these ingredients<lb/> into a Bason (without the flour) +<lb/> melt them either in the oven or<lb/> before the fire, then mix the flour<lb/> with a knife till quite smooth<lb/> + drop in upon this to bake in <lb/> a quick oven a short time-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/127|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
17<lb/> To keep Insects out of wood<lb/> Put it with a solution of vitriol<lb/> to prevent them <unclear>habouring</unclear><lb/> <pb/> To take Iron moulds out of<lb/> Linen.-<lb/> Take Sorell, bruise it well in a<lb/> mortar, squeeze it thro' a cloth- bottle<lb/> it + keep it for use, take a little of<lb/> the above juice in a silver or tin<lb/> sauce pan, boil it over a Lamp, as <lb/> it boils dip in the Iron mould do<lb/> not rub, it but only squeeze it-<lb/> as soon as the Iron mould is out<lb/> throw it into cold water
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/128|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
16<lb/> To fasten knives into their Blades<lb/> one part of Bee's wax 4 of Royin melted<lb/> + mixed up with one part of Brick dust<lb/> finely hardened.-<lb/> <pb/> To stop leaks in Casks<lb/> Suet + wood casks makes the best<lb/> Cement for this<lb/> <pb/> Isinglass <unclear>or</unclear> Vinegar better than<lb/> from Arabic <unclear>+</unclear> water for fastening<lb/> paper <unclear>or</unclear> flap<lb/> <pb/> To darken Mahogany<lb/> 3<hi rend="superscript">d</hi>: Alkanet root, 1 pint linseed oil-<lb/> 3<hi rend="superscript">d</hi> Rose pink put in a pan to stand<lb/> all night - then rub it over the mahogany<lb/> + cut it <unclear>zemon</unclear> 1 hour - then rub it off with a<lb/> linen cloth
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/129|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
15<lb/> Yellow Dye. Miss Fuller<lb/> <pb/> Take some <unclear>Inmmonide</unclear> and put it<lb/> into a small flannel bag squeeze<lb/> it a Boiling Water and soap till<lb/> you have got it to the colour you<lb/> wish it to have. then let the<lb/> Muslin be wash'd it in it with<lb/> Soap till it turns red. take it out<lb/> dissolve a little allum and put it<lb/> into cold water. rinse your Gown<lb/> till it becomes yellow, then hang<lb/> it up to dry<lb/> <pb/> To clean gold or silver Chains <gap/><lb/> 1 oz of aqua Amononiae <unclear>parse</unclear> y y<lb/> calld spirit of Caustic Alkali<lb/> W <unclear>have</unclear>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/130|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
14<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Cement</hi> <lb/> 2 Oz of Gum arabick 1 oz of white<lb/> sugar candy boild in a Pint of water<lb/> take it comes to half a Pint<lb/> <pb/> Cement<lb/> In a two Oz vial of Brandy put in <lb/> as much beaten or pickd Isinglass<lb/> as you can get in. let in stand before<lb/> the Fire till it is melted. and then<lb/> some more may be added. and<lb/> melted as before. This is always<lb/> ready for use by only setting it before<lb/> the fire to melt and is a very strong<lb/> cement and may be made still<lb/> stronger by adding a little Gum<lb/> Mastick.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/131|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
13<lb/> between each layer of Varnish<lb/> always wipe it with a clean<lb/> Flannel. after the fourth coat of<lb/> Varnish you must polish it with<lb/> <unclear>rotten</unclear> stone scraped over it + just<lb/> wet it with a sponge + water. The <lb/> next day you must clean off the<lb/> <unclear>rotten</unclear> stone with Oil and a Flanel<lb/> till the Grease is quite gone or it<lb/> will not take the Varnish again<lb/> if after it has been polish'd, then<lb/> sh<hi rend="superscript">d</hi> remain any lumps in The <unclear>lum<gap/></unclear><lb/> you must polish it over again.<lb/> if The Drawings are not prints you<lb/> must just wash them slightly over<lb/> over with the white of an Egg to prevent<lb/> the colours from running before you Varnish
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/132|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
12<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Varnish</hi><lb/> Wipe whatever you are going to Varnish<lb/> very clean. boil some size + do it over<lb/> while it is hot. take care it is not too<lb/> thick after the border and print are<lb/> cut out they must be Gumm'd with<lb/> very strong gum Water + left to dry<lb/> when the size you put on your Box<lb/> is quite dry stuck on your Print +<lb/> very flat with Paste N when they<lb/> have had sufficient time to dry. put<lb/> on the first Coat of Varnish, which <lb/> must be done all one way with<lb/> a lambs hair <add>Hat</add> brush. six Coats of<lb/> varnish is enough. the longer it<lb/> has to dry between each coat the better
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/133|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
11<lb/> <unclear>Slak</unclear> colour for the Roof of a <add>House</add><lb/> <pb/> The Tiling to be twice done with<lb/> Fools Oil or grounds, which is sold<lb/> in size + one when this is dry <hi rend="underline">quick</hi><lb/> paint it over with slate (dour or<lb/> Dark Hue. it may be easily done<lb/> by any handy cut doors <unclear>Servant</unclear><lb/> <pb/> Tar paint. made a good Green<lb/> by mixing a proper quantity of<lb/> yellow Oaker, and blue with the<lb/> Tar. supposd <hi rend="underline">Coal</hi> Jar<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/134|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
10<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Pink Dye</hi><lb/> Pink some Red Currants and put them<lb/> into a stone Jar with a little Allum<lb/> then pour boiling water enough to<lb/> cover them. when it has stood till it<lb/> is near cold pour off the clear so as not<lb/> to leave any settlement remaining<lb/> If the colours is to deep it must be<lb/> Cover'd with warm water. then dip<lb/> the Muslin or Gauze in and pin it<lb/> out instantly, for if it is not pin'd<lb/> or framd very quickly it will be of<lb/> different Hues. The Dye must be heated<lb/> after is it stram'd in an Earthen vessel<lb/> for the Ribbon<lb/> The colour will wash out of the<lb/> Muslin.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/135|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
9<lb/> sh<hi rend="superscript">d</hi> be starchd a very little. this<lb/> colour will never wash out.<lb/> <pb/> Yellow Dye<lb/> <pb/> Take three penny worth of Gum <unclear>bouge</unclear><lb/> and the same of Turmerick boil these<lb/> in three quarts of Skim and Milk<lb/> + strain it into a Pan large enough<lb/> to hold the Gown. The Gown must<lb/> be first wash'd starch'd + dried<lb/> then dip it into cold Water and<lb/> take care it is wet all over. then using<lb/> it out of the Water + put it in the<lb/> Dye all over at once. it must not<lb/> be wrung out of the Dye but pulld<lb/> out strait + hurry up till is dry<lb/> If it is to be glazed some Wax must<lb/> be put into the Starch-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/008/001/136|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
8<lb/> add starch to make it the stiffness you<lb/> wish Dry it in the Shade and out of<lb/> sight of a Fire and it will become a<lb/> beautiful salmon colour<lb/> <pb/> Lilac Dye<lb/> <pb/> Put a small quantity of Logwood<lb/> into an Earthen Vessell let it boil<lb/> a short time put to it a little allum<lb/> Dip a piece of what you intend to <lb/> Dye to fix the colour to your <unclear>Faney</unclear>. for<lb/> the more it is boild the deeper the colour<lb/> will be, then strain it and put your<lb/> Things into it, as hot as the Dyes can<lb/> bear it. Ribbon sh<hi rend="superscript">d</hi> be Iron'd very hot<lb/> + Muslin or Gauze, pin'd out. They <del>mus</del><lb/> must be quite clear + dry + Muslin
