'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/004|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To a quart of warm water <lb/> a tea cup of this put into<lb/> the fruit before they begin<lb/> to milk, will effectualy move<lb/> any <unclear>ba of</unclear> taste the milk, <del>milk</del><lb/> might require by food, of turnip,<lb/> or leaves that give butter a<lb/> bad taste.<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/005|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Stuff'd Eggs <lb/> <lb/> take six eggs boil them very hard, then cut them <lb/> <unclear>evens</unclear> in two, take not the yolks, part them put them in as mor<lb/> <unclear>than</unclear> with some grated bread crumbs, a little parsley<lb/> shrink very fine, a little onion <unclear>burath</unclear> them well thro-<lb/> gather, season it with pepper salt and mace, then<lb/> cover <gap/> of, with the forcemeat, fill the eggs<lb/> with<gap/> it und<gap/> stick them in the dish, bake them<lb/> in rather slow oven and when enough pour<lb/> in not y<gap/> <gap/>oung over them. -<lb/> N.B. you may wet the bread in <unclear>stais</unclear> and pour <lb/> doing cream over it. - instead of grating it
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/006|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<head>Treacle Beer</head><lb/> Two <del>Six</del> gallons of water two pounds of treacle half <lb/> an ounce of ginger. Boil all twenty minutes<lb/> and when nearly cold put all into a cask with<lb/> a spoonful of yeast. and when it has done<lb/> working it will be fit for to bottle or to drink<lb/> out of the Cask - <lb/> <head>Tincture of Rhubarb</head><lb/> Take two ounces of the best Turkey Rhubarb<lb/> Two Drams of Saffron and six Drams of Car<lb/> damum seeds bruised and <unclear>usfised</unclear> into <lb/> a Quart of the best French Brandy -<lb/> Let it be well shook every day for a fortnight<lb/> when it will be fit for use M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Richardson
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/007|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<head>Cowslip Vinegar</head><lb/> To fifteen Quarts of water five pounds of<lb/> coarse brown sugar - boil them half an hour<lb/> then strain it- The next Day put four large<lb/> bunches of Cowslip-Stalks + alltoghter - three s<lb/> spoonfuls of yeast- When it has worked three<lb/> or four days cover it up close, + let it stand<lb/> by the fire untill Sour - Add a little Isinglass<lb/> to clear it- - Mrs Gowng-<lb/> <pb/> <head><hi rend="underline">Preserved Scherian Crabs</hi></head>.<lb/> <unclear>Puib</unclear> the crabs, + as you do them put<lb/> them overhead in cold water or they will<lb/> lose their colour when that is done<lb/> give them a scald then wipe them quite<lb/> dry, make their own weight of sugar into<lb/> a syrup let them boil in that for <hi rend="underline">three</hi>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/008|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">minutes</hi>. let them stand till they are quite<lb/> cold, + then boil them again till they <lb/> look clear<lb/> <pb/> <head><hi rend="underline">To make Damson</hi> Biscuits I.C</head><lb/> Put your damsons in a jug into the oven<lb/> and when stewed rub them thro an hairsieve<lb/> Boil it 15 or 20 minutes. measure a pint of<lb/> pulp and add to each pint when quite cold<lb/> three quarters of a pound of powdered loaf Sugar<lb/> <unclear>Beech</unclear> them together to a light froth which<lb/> will take at least three hours then put it into<lb/> the biscuits papers + dry them before the fire<lb/> or on a <sic>bourd</sic> - <lb/> <head>Mock Turtle</head><lb/> Take a good calves head dressed with the skin<lb/> on + clear it from the Bone, then put it in <lb/> an Earthen pot with a little mace pepper +<lb/> onion, fill your pot with slices of ham or <lb/> lean Bacon, a handful of Lettuce + <unclear>Pech</unclear> leaves<lb/> <unclear>(Ti Os)</unclear>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/009|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Some Celery + half a pint of water= when<lb/> stewed well in the oven. strain the Gravey<lb/> while <del><gap/></del> warm from it= when you head<lb/> is quite cold cut it in square pieces<lb/> pareing where it is thick of meat from the <lb/> skin - for your Gravey you must cut in<lb/> slices all the meat from a Knuckle of veal<lb/> and lay it in a well tined sauce pan<lb/> that you can cover close with a few slices<lb/> of Ham some mace pepper onion and a small<lb/> lump of Butter - Set it over a ver<del>r</del>y gentle<lb/> Fire (as you must have no water near it)<lb/> when stewed strain every drop of gravey<lb/> from it after taking off all the fat put in<lb/> <del>put to it</del> your Gravey from your head a<lb/> pint of madeira or white wine (that is <lb/> not sweet) the juice of a whole Lemon
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/010|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
+ Cyan pepper to your taste- - Let the Head<lb/> be stewed in this Gravey about quarter of<lb/> an hour before you dish it up. Garnish<lb/> it with yolks of Eggs boild hard. force meat<lb/> Balls Sausages + Brain cakes not verry thin<lb/> + Lemon some of your Brains strain with<lb/> parsley juice in a sauce pan work a little<lb/> yolk of Egg + Butter stir it untill it is thick<lb/> + lay it in your dish in different parts<lb/> <pb/> <head>Milk of Roses</head><lb/> Half a pint of Rose water, half a pint of<lb/> oil of almonds. - a tea <add>cup</add> of oil of Tartar, + a<lb/> few drops of oil of Rhodium - shake all<lb/> very well together- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Rasensift</unclear>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/011|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<head>To cure Tongues + small Hams</head><lb/> To one Gallon of water put two pounds<lb/> of Suet- three quarters of a Pound of <unclear>Boowr</unclear><lb/> Sugar - one of warter of a pound of Salt<lb/> Petre bruised- let these Boil five minutes<lb/> pore the liquor boiling hot on the Ham<lb/> + Tongues- let them stay in it fourteen,<lb/> Days, turning + rubing them daily-<lb/> then hang them up to dry- <lb/> The same Pickle will do for <unclear>Sheres</unclear>, by <lb/> boiling + skiming it well - you may<lb/> keep the Tongues as long in the Pickel<lb/> as you please, by sometime boiling +<lb/> skiming it- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Harnletterns</unclear> - very good<lb/> <head>Cow Drink - for body <unclear>Gangle</unclear></head><lb/> 1 quart of beer- 1 oz - of cow spice 1 oz of flour<lb/> of Brimstone half a Pound of mustard seed<lb/> all boiled together - when cold give it the Cow, it<lb/> will come great <unclear>fernpurination</unclear> as soon as that is<lb/> over wash the Cow about a short time + turn <gap/> out
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/012|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<head>Mince Meat -</head><lb/> A Pound + quarter of Suet half a<lb/> Pound of raw Beef scraped. a Pound + 1/2 <lb/> good Raisins stoned + choped a little - a <lb/> Pound of Currants - a Pound of Apples, when <lb/> pared- chop them small - a pound + 1/2 of<lb/> Sugar, two Lemons- the peel cut thin + small<lb/> half a nutmeg - a little mace, + few Cloves<lb/> Pounded-. The Suet must be choped<lb/> very small, + the Beef well rubed in first<lb/> then a little of the other ingredients until<lb/> all are put in- The Sugar must be<lb/> added the last + half a pint of good Brandy<lb/> squeeze the Lemons in before the Brandy<lb/> M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Lowes- very good
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/013|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
