<head>Yellow Cabbage </head><lb/> Take a white heart cabbage. cut it into slices and<lb/> put it into a sieve: slice some onions + put a<lb/> layer of onions and a layer of Cabbage alternately;<lb/> sprinkling a handful of salt between each layer<lb/> Cover then over all night; <del>having</del> then put them into <lb/> a jar - Boil vinegar + straine together, and pour<lb/> these into the jar hot- Enclose some bruised mus<lb/> tard seed + half an ounce of Tumeric in a muslin<lb/> bag, suspend then in the jar + tye it up close -<lb/> <head>Cowslip Wine -</head><lb/> Boil 6 Gallons of water. a quarter of on hour<lb/> then add 2 1/2 <add><unclear>n</unclear></add> of <unclear>long</unclear> sugar to every gallon of water.<lb/> Boil it till the skin mix nice and clean <unclear>tody</unclear>.<lb/> When nearly cold pour it into a tub adding one<lb/> spoonful of yeast; let it work one day-<lb/> then put in fifteen quarts of cowslip flowers<lb/> letting it work three or four days. after which<lb/> <unclear>puet</unclear> it with a <unclear>bonal</unclear> with the parings of 6 lemons = 6<lb/> oranges. Make the juice of them into a thick syrup<lb/> with 1 1/2 pound of loaf sugar. When done working. add
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/015|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
the syrup to it with a citron Brandy + stop up the <lb/> vessel very close- when it has stood 3 months<lb/> bottle it- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Dom<lb/> <head>Second Cowslip Wine-</head><lb/> To every gallon of water put 2 pound of good brown<lb/> sugar, boil it a quarter of an hour <sic>sirring</sic> it well. <lb/> Put it into a tub + let it stand till it be cold<lb/> and pour it into the barrel you have drawn<lb/> the first wine pour as soon it be drawn off.<lb/> Stir it very well in the Barrel and stop it<lb/> up.- Bottle it in about 3 weeks- If you have<lb/> a few fresh flowers put them in, with about a<lb/> pint of brandy to every six gallons- The brandy<lb/> must be put into the barrel before it is stoped<lb/> up- M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Lowe<lb/> <head>Cowslip Beer</head><lb/> After you have drawn off your second cowslip<lb/> wine by boiling a pound of <unclear>trewill</unclear> in a Gallon<lb/> of water and putting it into the barrel, in 3 days<lb/> you will have nice beer- When the liquor is cold<lb/> you must put to it a little <unclear>barm</unclear> and stir it well<lb/> in the barrel - M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Lowe
