<hi rend="underline">Staining Wood Red.</hi><lb/> Make a strong infusion of Basil in<lb/> water impregnated with pearl ashes<lb/> in the proportion of 1 drachm of pearl<lb/> ashes to a pint of water, and add to it<lb/> 1/2 ounce of Basil; they must stand<lb/> together two or three days, often stirring<lb/> the mixture. With this infusion<lb/> strained, and made boiling hot, brush<lb/> over the wood to be stained till it appears<lb/> strongly coloured.- then while wet<lb/> brush it over with alum water in the<lb/> proportion of 1 ounce of alum to<lb/> a pint of water.<lb/> For a less bright red, dissolve an<lb/> ounce of dragons blood in a pint<lb/> of spirits of wine; and brush over
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/042|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
the wood with the tincture, till the<lb/> stain appear to be as strong as is<lb/> desired, but this is rather to be<lb/> called lacquering then staining-<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Staining Wood Purple</hi><lb/> Brush the wood to be stained<lb/> several times with a strong decoction<lb/> of log wood + <unclear>Brasit</unclear>; made in the<lb/> proportion of 4 ounces of Log wood<lb/> 1 ounce of <unclear>Brasit</unclear> to a quart of water,<lb/> and boiled for an hour; when the<lb/> wood has been brushed over till<lb/> there is a sufficient body of colour<lb/> let it dry; and then be slightly<lb/> <unclear>paped</unclear> over by a solution of one<lb/> drachm of peat ashes in a quart<lb/> of water; this solution must be <lb/> constantly used; as it will gradually<lb/> change the colour from a brown red
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/010/001/043|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
whisk it will be originally found to<lb/> be; to a dark blue purple; and<lb/> therefore its effect must be estrained<lb/> to the due point for producing<lb/> the colour desired-<lb/> <hi rend="underline">Stain Wood stain.</hi><lb/> Pounded <unclear>Iwmen's</unclear> root, in spirits<lb/> of wine 2 Tea spoonsfull of Tur<lb/> =meric, to a quarter of a pint <lb/> of spirits of wine it must be<lb/> shook every day for a week<lb/> when it should be strained off.-<lb/> add to this a very small quan<lb/> tity of <unclear>Wanladr</unclear> dye, which <unclear>maky</unclear><lb/> it must more like sattin wood<lb/> you must be carefull to make your<lb/> stain lighter than you intended<lb/> it to be when done, as the varnish<lb/> makes it look much darker.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

