<head>To make Turnip Bread</head> <lb/> A correspondent of a respectable periodical <lb/> work has communicated an experiment made<lb/> by him at a time when bread was very dear<lb/> with a view to find some cheaper substitute <lb/> for wheat flour, he directed several Turnips<lb/> to be washed, pared; and boiled, when soft<lb/> enough to be mashed the greatest part of the<lb/> water was prepared out of them, and they were<lb/> mixed with an equal weight of coarse Oatmeal<lb/> The dough was then made in the usual manner<lb/> with yeast, salt and water. It <unclear>rose</unclear> well was<lb/> made up in leaves and baked in the usual<lb/> manner. When drawn from the oven a loaf<lb/> was cut + found to be sweeter than common<lb/> bread. + full as light, and white. with a light<lb/> but not disagreeable taste of the Turnip<lb/> Twelve hours afterwards this taste was scarcely<lb/> perceptible and in 24 hours was entirely gone
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

