6<lb/> Lettuce Ginger<lb/> Take the stalks of large Lettuces when they have grown<lb/> out to the length of 16 or 18, nicked from the root, pull<lb/> off the rind + cut them in pieces from one to two<lb/> nickes in length, according to taste, lay them in spring<lb/> water, changing it every day for 6 days, when you will<lb/> find the stalks look transparent put off any remaining<lb/> pieces, and scald the talks in spring water until they<lb/> are crisp but not tender, the small ones will become<lb/> so <unclear>hifon</unclear> the large + should be taken out, have<lb/> ready some fine ruse Ginger, which has been soaked<lb/> all night and nicely scraped; make a syrup with a <lb/> pint of water to a pound of <add>loaf</add> sugar, let it boil + skim<lb/> it, then put in the ginger and lettuce, let them boil<lb/> a few minutes every three days, for three weeks, then<lb/> put it altogether in a jar for a fortnight, when scald<lb/> and drain it from the syrup + make a second syrup<lb/> with <hi rend="underline">half</hi> a pint of water to a pound of sugar with
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/009|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
7<lb/> a few rakes of <add>such</add> ginger in which simmer the Lettuce until<lb/> it is crisp as you wish, put it into jars and tie down with<lb/> bladders, look at it in 6 weeks, + give it another boil if<lb/> necessary- Nb - the first syrup should be boiled, skimmed,<lb/> + with the ginger put by to serve as a first syrup for<lb/> Melon, which is preserved in the same way-<lb/> Bramhope Augt- 1823-<lb/> To preserve good berries for Tarts- Miss <unclear>Emmerton</unclear><lb/> Gather half ripe <sic>goosberries</sic> of all kinds, to<lb/> 11 lts, put 4 1/2 lb<hi rend="underline">s</hi> of sugar + let them <hi rend="underline">boil</hi><lb/> gently a full hour -<lb/> Green <sic>Goosberry</sic> Cheese- Mrs Rind<lb/> Gather the fruit dry- cut off the stalks and tops<lb/> + put them in cold water, then put them wet<lb/> into a preserving pan, but do not put any more<lb/> water to them, let them simmer over a slow fire<lb/> till they are tender, they must be stirred all the<lb/> time to prevent them sticking to the pan which
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

