8<lb/> would spoil their colour. crush them rather<lb/> small + put to a pound of fruit, three quarters<lb/> of a pound of loaf sugar finely powdered.<lb/> and let it <hi rend="underline">boil gently</hi> till it is very thick, but<lb/> not to spoil the colour. put it into ports, +<lb/> cover with Brandy papers- The large having<lb/> green <sic>goosberry</sic> is <unclear>hist</unclear> for this preserve-<lb/> simmered with the Cheese, to make it more <add>green</add>.<lb/> <sic>Goosberry</sic> Vinegar<lb/> To every four pounds of ripe green gooseberries put<lb/> one Gallon of water, which boil + let stand till cold,<lb/> then bruise your berries and mix them with the water<lb/> + let them stand 24 hours, stirring it often, then strain<lb/> it through a bag put a little yeast on + let it turn<lb/> 24 hours + to every 3 quarts of liquor 1 lb of <hi rend="underline">course</hi><lb/> sugar, let it stand in the cask in a warm place<lb/> till sour- the cask should not be quarter full<lb/> + you must put a paper over the <unclear>Jerry hole</unclear><lb/> pricked full of holes<lb/> M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Rhodes-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

