<hi rend="underline">To dry Apricots-</hi><lb/> <pb/> Take the finest Apricots you can procure-<lb/> prick them at the bottom part, put<lb/> them into cold water + set them upon<lb/> the fire, let them simmer, but not boil.<lb/> when they are soft put them into cold,<lb/> water + when they are cold put them on a cloth<lb/> to dry- then put them into a bason + pour<lb/> some clarified sugar over them- the day<lb/> after take the syrup from the Apricots +<lb/> boil it + repeat it for five days- then<lb/> put the Apricots into the syrup + give them<lb/> a boiling, then put them into the bason<lb/> + let them stand 3 days- take the syrup<lb/> from them + put them on a sieve + let them<lb/> in a hot Closet four days. turn them<lb/> every morning + then put them in <unclear>bokes</unclear> for <lb/> use - NB The same plan will answer for<lb/> Peaches or Nectarines. The Sug<hi rend="superscript">r</hi>. to be poured upon <lb/> the Fruit must be clarified without being suffered to boil<lb/> it it boiled it will spoil the colour of the Fulies/Fenhome 1814
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

