To make Balls for Tea a <unclear>sat Recordhay</unclear> Paste<lb/> 3 lt of Flour 1/2 lt Butter, a pint of new milk, a little<lb/> Cream two Eggs, half a pint of yeast, the Butter must<lb/> be rubbed into the flour before the other ingredients<lb/> are put in. it must be mixed up light, put it<lb/> into cups about half full + let stand to rise<lb/> about half an hour. The cups must be buttered<lb/> (M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> A. R. Horsforth<lb/> <pb/> Broken Glass + China-<lb/> The juice of Garlic applied to the pieces to be pined<lb/> is the strangest Cement that can be used for that<lb/> purpose + if it be done with care well leave very<lb/> like morte-. M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> A R. Horsforth<lb/> <pb/> Paste for Cleaning Copper Brass Tins<lb/> 12 oz of soft soup well boiled with a pint of water<lb/> + then mixed with 1 lt of Bottom stone ground<lb/> then add 2 oz of spirits of wine in which an<lb/> oz of Camphor has been dissolved-. to be rubbed<lb/> in with a little Flannel, wiped well with linen<lb/> rag + polished with wash leather. M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> A R. Horf<hi rend="underline">th</hi><lb/> <pb/> Cheese Marmakines-.<lb/> Mix white bread, milk + Eggs as you would for a <lb/> pudding then add as much grated Cheese<lb/> as suits y<hi rend="superscript">r</hi> taste, bake in a thin crust as a <lb/> Cheese cake or in paper moulds as you like best<lb/> (M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Rhodes Camp field
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

