Hooping Cough-.<lb/> For Infant a few months old give one quarter of<lb/> a grain of powdered alum in a spoonful of sugar<lb/> + water three times a day upon an empty stomach<lb/> increasing the dose on the second day to half<lb/> a grain + give that quantity as above described<lb/> till the cough is removed. To a child a year<lb/> old give 1/2 a grain increasing the quantity to a <lb/> grain- A child four years old may begin<lb/> by taking two grains increasing the quantity<lb/> to 4 grains thus regulating the dose according<lb/> to the age of the patient. an adult may begin<lb/> by taking 10 grains increasing till it amounts<lb/> to 20 or 25 gains-. M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Willam Seacroft.<lb/> <pb/> <unclear>Rice Sauffle</unclear><lb/> Swell the rice with a very little milk over<lb/> a slow fire, when quite soft, put it into<lb/> a sieve for a few minutes, + cover it with<lb/> a napkin, then mix some sifted white<lb/> sugar with it to your taste; put it rough<lb/> into a dish, or make it hollow in the<lb/> middle, and fill it <add>with</add> any sweetmeat you<lb/> like, or with the pulp of apple mixed <add>as <unclear>steved</unclear></add> with<lb/> a little lemon juice + sugar; cover it with<lb/> cream beat quite to a froth, + brown it with<lb/> a Salamander.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/028|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Almond Butter<lb/> 2 ounces of Butter, 2 ounces of almonds<lb/> blanched + beaten. the yolks of 4 Eggs boiled<lb/> hard, a little peel + juice of a lemon sugar<lb/> to your taste- mix all in a mortar till<lb/> reduced to a paste, squeeze this through<lb/> a Cullender into a dish, having first<lb/> covered the <unclear>bokocr</unclear> with macaroons<lb/> or Biscuits soaked in wine- Miss Paley<lb/> <pb/> <unclear>Cobanrgh</unclear> or Pumpkin Soup-<lb/> Take one gourd or Pumpkin, peel it + take<lb/> out the inside, cut it into thin slices, put<lb/> 4 large onions + fry them with the gourd<lb/> in Butter, they must be kept stirring, fry<lb/> them till they are quite tender + look brown<lb/> then add a little water + keep them on the<lb/> fire 1/4 of an hour longer, put them into <lb/> a saucepan with 2 quarts of broth or<lb/> soup with pepper + salt. let it simmer for<lb/> 2 hours then add thin slices of fried<lb/> bread, boil all up together for about 10<lb/> minutes + serve it-. Miss Teason-<lb/> <pb/> Pitched Pork-<lb/> The Pig sh<hi rend="underline">d</hi> be cut down the baack + the lean<lb/> taken out as a spare rib, then cut into square<lb/> pieces + salt <del>it</del> with common salt, salt petre +<lb/> bay salt, in double proportion required for Hams,<lb/> then lay it into a Tub with the skin downwards,
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/029|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
+ a layer of the sauce mixed salt, between each<lb/> + cover over on the top, let it stand till the<lb/> brine appears, which is usually from 2 to 3<lb/> weeks, when wholly melted if there is not<lb/> sufficient to cover the whole some strong<lb/> brine must be added - a piece of stone<lb/> should be kept on the top to prevent the<lb/> meat swimming<del>ing</del> as it requires to be kept<lb/> constantly under the brine + used regularly<lb/> as it is laid in the Tub-.<lb/> Nb. Miss Rhodes has the piece cut large<lb/> + as near the size of the Tub as possible<lb/> it packs better-. (M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Nicholson Roundhay<lb/> Park<lb/> <pb/> Lunna Caustic 100 Grains, Gum Arabic 2<lb/> drachms, Rain water 1 oz- Sap green 1<lb/> somple.-. Preparatory liquid.-<lb/> Sat soda 1 oz- dissolved in 2 oz of rain<lb/> water-. ( Miss Paley-.<lb/> <pb/> From Mould powder-.<lb/> Cream of Tarter one oz - salt of Sorrel<lb/> 4 drachms to be well mixed together +<lb/> used as salt of lemon -. (Miss Paley<lb/> <pb/>
