Piquans Compote.- <lb/> Take young piquans + skimer them as you do for boiling,<lb/> put forcemeat-into the Claws, laid them down the breast<lb/> + fry them brown, then put them into a strong brown<lb/> gravy, and let them stew 3/4 of an hour, thicken it<lb/> with a lump of butter rolled in flour. When you dish<lb/> them up, lay forcemeat balls round them, + strain<lb/> the gravy over them; the forcemeat must be made<lb/> thus; grate the crums of a penny loaf, + scrap a 1/4<lb/> of a lb of fat Bacon instead of suet, chop a little parsley,<lb/> thyme, 2 shalots, an an Onion, grate a little nutmeg<lb/> lemon peel, some pepper + salt, mix them together<lb/> with Eggs.- from M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Nickelson's receipt book.-<lb/> <pb/> Pot Tongue with Veal.-<lb/> Take salted tongue, put your veal + Tongue in separate<lb/> pots, season with Mace, white pepper, nutmeg, + salt;<lb/> put to it as much water s will cover it + lay something<lb/> on the top of the pots + keep them close, let them in a oven<lb/> till quite tender, when enough, beat it <hi rend="underline">fine</hi> with a <lb/> sufficient quantity of clarified butter, <hi rend="underline">taking out all</hi><lb/> <hi rend="underline">required</hi>; put the meat in a pot a layer of <del>Tongue</del> <add>veal</add> + a layer<lb/> of Tongue, so that it may cut out in stripes, red + white;<lb/> cover it with clarified butter.- f<hi rend="superscript">rm</hi>. Mrs Nickelson's recp<hi rend="superscript">t</hi> book
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

