<unclear>Plesales</unclear>-<lb/> Boil 2 sweet breads, a little <unclear>Baun</unclear>, parsley, Lemon,<lb/> thyme, <sic>Shalot</sic>, or green onion, Lemon peel all to<lb/> be chopped very fine, a little cream of bread, pepper,<lb/> salt, mace, nutmeg, to your taste, a little good cream<lb/> to mix them in whatever shape you like roll them<lb/> in yolk of Egg + creams of Bread, fry them a light brown<lb/> lay them on paper to <sic>drane</sic> off the fat; garnish with<lb/> crisp parsley-. M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Nicholsen's book.-<lb/> <pb/> Curry powder.<lb/> Coriander Seed. 3 oz-<lb/> Black Pepper - 2 1/2 oz-<lb/> Turmeric - 2 1/2 oz-<lb/> Thyam Pepper - - 1/2 oz-<lb/> Cummin Seed - 1/2 oz -<lb/> <pb/> Curry-<lb/> Take a Chicken + skin it, fry it in butter, at the same<lb/> time stew over it 2 table spoonfuls of Curry powder<lb/> + a little <unclear>Byan</unclear>, fry it pretty brown; then take 6 <lb/> large onions + a couple of Cucumbers, if you<lb/> have them, cut + fry them in butter + some Curry<lb/> powder over them, the sauce as the Chicken, then<lb/> put altogether into a stew pan, with a good, all<lb/> of Gravy, pepper + Salt to your taste, stew them<lb/> till tender thicken with butter. - M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Nick's book<lb/> <pb/>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/036|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Tupioca Pudding.-<lb/> Take as much Sapinca as you think will make y<hi rend="superscript">r</hi> pudding<lb/> <unclear>ucallecting</unclear> that it swells; wash, + let it stand all night in<lb/> cold milk, add more milk to boil it till a spoon can<lb/> almost stand in it, let it be quite cold + then beat 3 Eggs<lb/> thoroughly into it boil it again, turn it out of the mould, +<lb/> pour wine Sauce on it, with nutmeg, or slice some<lb/> Jelly, or any other sweet <unclear>round</unclear> it, stirring it over with Sugar,<lb/> it is often basted; a little Lemon peel boiled in the milk<lb/> is an improvement or any thing of that kind may be<lb/> added to flavor it with Sugar to taste. - M<unclear>rs</unclear> Nickelson.<lb/> a good preparation of Salts.-<lb/> 14 Oz of <unclear>Tace haves</unclear> infused in a pint of boiling water till<lb/> extracted, strain + add 2 oz of Epsom Salts in a quart bottle<lb/> fill it up with water, add Elixor of vitriol sufficient to make it<lb/> of agreeable acidity; a <del>viy</del> wine glass to be taken daily as an<lb/> alternative, or more if required as a purgative.-<lb/> M<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Nicholsen.<lb/> <pb/> Stew? Breast Lamb.-<lb/> Take a breast of Lamb, take out some of the Ribs +<lb/> turn it round, roast it pretty brown till about half <gap/><lb/> put it into a stew, pan with a handle of sweet herbs, a little<lb/> good broth <add>2</add> or gravy + a little Catchup, strain the gravy +<lb/> thicken it with flour + butter, when dished up cover it<lb/> with French beans or peas.-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/037|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Collar Eels<lb/> Take the eels, skin + clean them well, slit them down the<lb/> belly to the tail, take out the bone, lay them flat on the<lb/> back + wipe them very dry, season them very high with<lb/> pepper, salt, mace, + Cayenne, mixed up together with a<lb/> little shread parsley; roll them flour the tail to the head<lb/> very tight + sew them with thread; then roll it tight in a<lb/> cloth, tie at each end, <hi rend="underline">tight</hi>; the cloth sh<hi rend="underline">d</hi> be pretty fine +<lb/> well rubbed over with butter; put them into an equal <lb/> quantity of vinegar + water; sufficient to cover them cold<lb/> stew them an hour; take them out, tye them down at the<lb/> ends, hang them by one end till well drained + the<lb/> liquor cold; if the pickle is not sour enough with an<lb/> equal quantity of vinegar, add mace, also a handful of<lb/> salt to it before they are boiled.- put them into a jar in the<lb/> cloths as they are, pour the vinegar over them + put them<lb/> in a cool place till wanted; they will keep 3 months by<lb/> boiling the pickle frequently + pouring over them cold.<lb/> Tomato Sauce - from M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Maddon<lb/> When you tomatoes are quite ripe take off the<lb/> green stalks <unclear>ten</unclear> + after wiping them down put the<lb/> with a slow oven until they are quite soft, work<lb/> them through a sieve + take the seeds out. To every<lb/> two pounds of tomatoes put two quarts of <unclear>foathering</unclear><lb/> vinegar, one dozen of <unclear>Cupsicumms</unclear>, a quarter of a<lb/> pound of garlic, quarter of lb of <sic>Shalots</sic> picked<lb/> + sliced very thin, one ounce of white Pepper ground<lb/> a good deal of Salt. boil the whole together till the<lb/> garlic + <sic>Shalots</sic> are soft, strain + skim off the froth
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/038|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
then rub it through a hair Sieve, boil it again,<lb/> + if it is too thick add a little more Vinegar-<lb/> when quite cold, bottle it- N.B. when this Sauce<lb/> is used the bottle must be shook up, otherwise<lb/> the Ingredients will settle to the bottom<lb/> (excellent)<lb/> Apricot Souffle.<lb/> Take apricot Jam + put in a small dish<lb/> with blanched split almonds stuck very<lb/> thick round, the white of four Eggs beat<lb/> up very stiff + poured over; stick almonds<lb/> round + on the top + sifted Sugar + put<lb/> in the oven for a short time to bake.-<lb/> M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> <unclear>Shealfeild's</unclear> Housekeeper<lb/> Little Puddings.-<lb/> Take three Eggs, their weight in fine sugar, <lb/> the same in flour + also in water bake<lb/> them in tea cups. observe to beat these<lb/> wipe oiccents well together, pour melted Butter<lb/> or Wine Sauce over them when served.<lb/> M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> <unclear>Shearfield's</unclear> Housekeeper
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/039|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">Rice <unclear>Gruet</unclear> or Jelly</hi><lb/> One Table spoonful of <hi rend="underline">Ground</hi><lb/> Brir - half a pint of Water - to be<lb/> boiled one hour - this makes about<lb/> half a pint -. flavor or not, to your<lb/> taste, or as required-. Milk may be<lb/> used instead of <hi rend="underline">Water</hi> when it agrees-<lb/> From M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Bind Aug. 29<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">th</hi> 1840 <unclear>Trict</unclear> very good <pb/> Pastry <hi rend="underline">as at Spring Wood</hi><lb/> Take a bit of Dough aabout 6' ozs- from<lb/> the fine bread, + rub <hi rend="underline">well</hi> into it, 5 ozs.<lb/> of Butter, work it well with a little <lb/> flour until of the stiffness of Bread<lb/> ready to be <hi rend="underline">put into the oven</hi><lb/> September 3<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">d</hi> 1840-
