'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/040|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">Currant or Raspberry Cake</hi><lb/> Take equal weight of Fruit + Sugar, bruise it quite<lb/> fine + put it on a flat dish in the oven. stir<lb/> it with a Knife, and when it begging to serve<lb/> on the dish edge, mix it well with the <add>boiling</add> Raspberry<lb/> <add>or Currant</add> Juice which must have been previously boiled<lb/> for ten minutes, turn it on plates to dry - + put it<lb/> in the Scum- <lb/> <pb/> Currant Jelly<lb/> Take equal parts of Red + White Currants, pick<lb/> + put them into a Jar in the Oven, strain the <lb/> Juice through a Jelly bag <add>(do not squeeze the Fruit)</add> and to every quart<lb/> of <hi rend="underline">clear</hi> juice put 1 lb of lump Sugar, stir it<lb/> int the pan till dissolved, + let it boil quickly<lb/> 22 minutes, it must be put into the pots<lb/> boiling hot. - From very dear Mothers receipt.<lb/> Book... C. M. -<lb/> <pb/> Boiled Lemon Pudding as at the Rocks<lb/> Equal quantities of cream of Bread + Veal Suet<lb/> or the sweetest you can get- the juice of 2 Lemons<lb/> + the rind of one chopped <hi rend="underline">very fine</hi>, a little<lb/> Sugar + Nutmeg + a very little mace 2 or 3 <hi rend="underline">yolks</hi><lb/> of Eggs (none of the white). boil it in a mould<lb/> + have White wine, + Lemon juice, sweetened, in the<lb/> dish for sauce. this is a good Pudding (M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> Sweetland
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/041|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Nicholsen's Receipt to fine a <unclear>Phd</unclear> of <unclear>Medeua</unclear> or Shrimp<lb/> <pb/> Dissolve an Oz of Isinglass in two Quarts of the Wine<lb/> intended to be fined, when like warm put it<lb/> into a Can, + draw off about a gallon of the wine,<lb/> beat it well up with a whisk till it becomes<lb/> a very stiff froth, then put it into the Cask +<lb/> beat it well round with a stick, let it stand<lb/> 3 weeks or a month, + it will be ready to<lb/> bottle- { Copied f<hi rend="underline">rm</hi> very dear Mothers receipt <lb/> Book July 25<hi rend="superscript"><hi rend="underline">th</hi> /4 2<lb/> <pb/> To take the stains of Port Wine out of Linen<lb/> Let the Linen be well washed in <hi rend="underline">cold</hi> water<lb/> + Soup, if it has not been in hot water <del>it</del> <add>this</add> will <lb/> ingeneral take it out-<lb/> <pb/> <hi rend="underline">To make Orange Marmalade</hi><lb/> Peel the oranges + throw the rinds away.<lb/> seasoning a few of the best to make chips<lb/> of - Cut out the white + boil it until<lb/> tender enough to be pierced easily with<lb/> a froth. then pound it very fine in a<lb/> mortar-. Boil 6 lbs of Sugar in<lb/> 2 pints of <unclear>water</unclear>, skim it, then add
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/042|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
2 lbs of the pounded white, Boil it fast,<lb/> till the Syrup is very thick, stirring it<lb/> carefully all the time- then put 2 pints<lb/> + a half of the juice, some chips of the<lb/> outer rind cut very thin + fine (about<lb/> a plateful is sufficient for the quantity)<lb/> + a pint of apple <unclear>sater</unclear> - boil all<lb/> gently together till well <unclear>gillied</unclear>, which <lb/> will be in about 20 minutes or 1/2 an hour<lb/> (taking care that it does not set to the Pan)<lb/> then put it into Pots-.<lb/> The Apple <unclear>Sater</unclear> is made by cutting up<lb/> three large apples with the rind on (being<lb/> previously washed) + boiling them in a<lb/> pint of <unclear>sater</unclear> + straining the Liquor<lb/> off through a Sieve-<lb/> For making Marmalade the Seville<lb/> Orange is used. four Clogem Oranges at<lb/> beast are <hi rend="underline">required for this</hi> quantity-<lb/> (Excellent) frm M<hi rend="superscript">rs</hi> <unclear>Shoalfield</unclear>/Feb 7:/1844
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/043|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">Barley Water</hi> (Superior)<lb/> Pare a Lemon very thin, + put the rind<lb/> into a Jug with <hi rend="underline">three</hi> ounces of loaf sugar<lb/> + 2 Table spoonfuls of Pearl Barley (well<lb/> washed) pour upon this five pints of<lb/> <hi rend="underline">boiling</hi> water- Cover with a fine<lb/> cloth + let it stand in the open air<lb/> all night- Mrs <unclear>Strensfould</unclear>.<lb/> Feb 7 1844-<lb/> <pb/> <hi rend="underline">To make Rabbit Soup</hi> (Mrs Nicholson<lb/> Take three Rabbits cut them up, put<lb/> them in a pan with a little Butter,<lb/> fry brown, then put to the Rabbit a<lb/> pint of Stock 2 <unclear>rools</unclear> of Celery, a Carrot<lb/> a Turnip 3 Onions 2 Cloves + a bunch<lb/> of Herbs- Simmer an hour, fill up<lb/> with second <unclear>Shek</unclear>, boil an hour,<lb/> strain it- cut the meat off the bones<lb/> in mouthfuls- next day take off the<lb/> fat warm it with the meat, thicken<lb/> with a little flour + Butter adding
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/044|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
half a pint of Cream the last<lb/> thing, with pepper + salt to taste. +<lb/> a little chopped parsley-<lb/> To boil Rice, to be eaten with <unclear>Nuat</unclear><lb/> Put the quantity of Rice you require into<lb/> a brass pan with cold water, after it boils,<lb/> let it boil for 20 minutes, more or less,<lb/> as required- put the rice into cold water,<lb/> wash it again- then strain quite dry.<lb/> + set it before the fire to dry, moving<lb/> it about - occasionally so that the whole<lb/> may become equally dry-<lb/> an excellent receipt <lb/> Leamington April 1844.
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/045|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
wine measure/ take 16 lb<hi rend="underline">s</hi><lb/> boil till clear, then skin<lb/> <gap/>ite ginger bruised +<lb/> let it boil 1/4 of an hour,<lb/> vessel + when new<lb/> spoonsful of yeast,<lb/> + the next morning put<lb/> ding the juice of 8 Lemons,<lb/> fortnight, then taste<lb/> then put to it a pint<lb/> Isinglass, keep it clove<lb/> Mrs Rhodes-<unclear>Cegumpfield</unclear>
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

