'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/050|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
Cowslip Wine, after M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Will<hi rend="superscript">m</hi> Walker's receipt. got<lb/> f<hi rend="underline">rm</hi> M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Bickerdick. her late housekeeper-.<lb/> To one Gallon of water put one Quartem<lb/> of Cowslip peeps, 3 lbs of Sugar, boil the sugar +<lb/> water half an hour, + when cold, put in the <lb/> peeps with a little fresh yeast + let it ferment<lb/> 2 or 3 days. then put it into the Barrel with one<lb/> Lemon to the Gallon, let it stand 3 weeks, then<lb/> bottle it- let the peeps be remain in the<lb/> barrel, hurry <hi rend="underline">it close up till you</hi> want it again
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/051|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
To make Cream Cheese in twelve hours (M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Hind)<lb/> Put a quart of very thick Cream into a wet Napkin,<lb/> tie it up close, lay a pound weight upon it, change<lb/> the cloth every three hours, sprinkle on the last<lb/> a little salt. - The Cheese will be fit to use in<lb/> twelve hours.-<lb/> <pb/> <head>Elder Flower Wine</head>.<lb/> To every six gallons of water, put eighteen pounds<lb/> of good loaf sugar, boil it half an hour, when<lb/> new milk warm, put to it 1/4 of a peck of Elder-<lb/> flowers, picked very clean from the stalks,<lb/> then the juice of 6 Lemons, and their rinds, pared<lb/> as thin as possible- six pounds of Raisins<lb/> stoned, and four or <hi rend="underline">five</hi> <add>(to much</add> spoonful of yeast,<lb/> let it stand in a Tub to work four or five days,<lb/> stirring it two or three times a day. there put<lb/> the whole into a Barrel, with half an ounce of<lb/> Isinglass dissolved in a little of the liquor-<lb/> Bung it up, and let it stand fine or 6 weeks-<lb/> This wine will keep for years although<lb/> without Brandy.- M<hi rend="underline">rs</hi> Hind. Newton <unclear>Peen</unclear>-<lb/> N.B. very good for those who like the flavour<lb/> of Elder flowers-
<!-- DO NOT EDIT BELOW THIS LINE --> {{Metadata:{{PAGENAME}}}}

'[{{fullurl:TD/011/001/052|action=edit}} Click Here To Edit]''' <!-- ENTER TRANSCRIPTION BELOW THIS LINE -->
<hi rend="underline">Apple</hi> Cake<lb/> 3 lb of apples 2 lb of Sugar<lb/> Put a pint of water to<lb/> to the Sugar put it on to<lb/> Boil on a quick stove Put the<lb/> apples to the Sugar put the<lb/> Cover on the Cake till apples are<lb/> in mash then stir till they become<lb/> a thick marmalade oil your<lb/> mould then for the apple into <lb/> the mould then fill it up with<lb/> (some will Beat up without<lb/> Sugar when you turn it out)<lb/> <unclear>drul</unclear> Put it in water heel use a<lb/> hot <unclear>Choath</unclear><lb/> <unclear>Marchulls</unclear> from July 28<hi rend="superscript">th</hi><lb/> <hi rend="underline">1851</hi>
